const READING_PASSAGES = [{"id":"rd-kc-01-1","level":"kc","title":"The Hour Without Bells","topic":"school life","words":313,"passage":"Every Wednesday morning at Hoa Sen Secondary School begins in an unusual way. There is no bell, no assembly, and no teacher standing at the front of the room. Instead, for exactly sixty minutes, the whole school reads. Students may choose any book they like, sit anywhere on the school grounds, and nobody is allowed to ask them a question about what they have read.<br><br>The idea came from Ms Thu, the school librarian, who noticed that borrowing had dropped by almost half in three years. \"Students told me they had no time,\" she says. \"Between homework, extra classes and their phones, reading had simply fallen off the list.\" She suggested taking one hour from the timetable and giving it back to books. The head teacher agreed to a trial of one term.<br><br>At first, not everyone was pleased. Some parents worried that their children would lose an hour of maths or English. A few students admitted that they spent the first two sessions staring at the same page. But by the middle of the term, something had changed. The library was running out of popular titles, and students had started swapping books with each other in the corridors.<br><br>The school has also noticed some effects it did not expect. Teachers report that Wednesday classes are calmer, and several students who rarely spoke in lessons have joined a new book club that meets at lunchtime. \"I used to think reading was something you did for a test,\" says Minh, a grade 9 student. \"Now I read on the bus, in the queue, everywhere.\"<br><br>The trial has now been extended to the whole year. Ms Thu is careful not to call it a success yet. \"One hour a week won't fix everything,\" she says. \"But it has reminded a lot of people that reading can be a pleasure, and that was the whole point.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A lunchtime book club started by grade 9 students","How a school library lost half of its books in three years","A school's weekly reading hour and the effects it has had","Why parents at Hoa Sen are against changes to the timetable"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Every paragraph returns to the sixty-minute reading hour: where it came from, how people reacted, and what changed. The library, the parents and the book club are details, not the main topic."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Ms Thu suggest the reading hour?","options":["Because students had complained about too much homework","Because the school had bought too many new books","Because the head teacher asked her to find a new activity","Because fewer students were borrowing books from the library"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she <b>noticed that borrowing had dropped by almost half in three years</b>. The head teacher only agreed to her idea; he did not ask for it."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Students must read their books inside the library.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says students may <b>sit anywhere on the school grounds</b>, so the statement contradicts the passage."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Ms Thu has worked at the school for more than three years.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The three years refer to the fall in borrowing, not to how long Ms Thu has worked there. The passage never says when she joined the school."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"fallen off the list\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to:","options":["become too expensive","been forgotten by teachers","become less important to them","been banned by the school"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Ms Thu is explaining that homework, extra classes and phones left no room for reading; it had dropped out of students' priorities. Nothing was banned or cost money."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the new book club?","options":["It meets every Wednesday morning during the reading hour","Its members are required to swap books with each other","It was planned by Ms Thu before the trial began","It attracted students who were usually quiet in class"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says <b>several students who rarely spoke in lessons</b> joined the club, and lists it among effects the school <b>did not expect</b>. It meets at lunchtime, not during the reading hour."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In the last sentence, \"that\" refers to:","options":["reminding people that reading can be enjoyable","the number of books in the library","extending the trial to the whole year","fixing every problem at the school"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"That\" points back to the clause just before it: the reading hour <b>has reminded a lot of people that reading can be a pleasure</b>, which Ms Thu calls the whole point."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-01-2","level":"kc","title":"Football Where Running Is Against the Rules","topic":"sport","words":317,"passage":"Imagine a football match in which nobody is allowed to run. The ball moves, the players move, but the moment anyone breaks into a jog, the referee blows the whistle and gives the ball to the other team. This is walking football, and it is one of the fastest-growing sports in Britain, though it is played mostly by people over fifty.<br><br>The game was invented in 2011 by a football club in the north of England that wanted its older supporters to stay active. Its rules are simple. Teams have five or six players, the pitch is small, the ball must stay below head height, and there is no physical contact. Matches last about twenty minutes each way, which sounds short until you have tried walking quickly for that long.<br><br>The sport's popularity has surprised even the people who started it. Today there are more than a thousand clubs across the country, and national tournaments attract hundreds of teams. Doctors have begun to recommend it to patients recovering from heart problems, because it raises the heart rate without the sudden stops and jumps that make ordinary football risky for older bodies.<br><br>For many players, however, the health benefits are only part of the story. \"I played every weekend until I was forty,\" says Ray, sixty-eight, who now captains a team in Manchester. \"Then my knees gave up and I thought that was the end. Walking football gave me my Saturday mornings back.\" Players often mention the friendships they have made, and clubs say that members who live alone are the ones who almost never miss a session.<br><br>Not everyone approves. Some critics argue that a game without running is not really football, and a few players have been known to bend the rules when the referee is not looking. But as Ray puts it, \"At my age, being told off for running too fast is a compliment.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a club in the north of England won a national tournament","How Ray recovered from a serious knee injury","Why ordinary football is dangerous for everyone","A slower form of football and why it appeals to older people"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The whole text describes walking football: its rules, its growth, its health and social benefits for older players. Ray is only one example, and the passage never says ordinary football is dangerous for everyone."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to the passage, what happens when a player runs?","options":["The player is sent off the pitch","The match is stopped for twenty minutes","The other team is given the ball","The player must leave the club"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: the referee <b>blows the whistle and gives the ball to the other team</b>. Twenty minutes is the length of each half, not a punishment."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Walking football was created to help a club's older fans keep fit.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the game was invented by a club <b>that wanted its older supporters to stay active</b>, which matches the statement."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Ray had an operation on his knees.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Ray only says his <b>knees gave up</b>. The passage does not mention any operation, so there is no information to confirm or deny the statement."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"gave up\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to:","options":["became much stronger","surrendered to the other team","were replaced by new ones","stopped working properly"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Ray played every weekend until his knees <b>gave up</b> and he thought it was the end of football for him, so the knees had failed. \"Surrender\" is the usual meaning of the phrase, but it does not fit here."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer say that twenty minutes each way \"sounds short until you have tried walking quickly for that long\"?","options":["To criticise the rules of walking football","To explain why some players bend the rules","To suggest that the game is harder than it seems","To show that matches are too long for older players"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts what the rule <b>sounds</b> like with the reality of doing it, hinting that fast walking for forty minutes is tiring. The writer is not criticising the rules or saying matches are too long."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the passage suggest about players who live alone?","options":["They are usually the weakest players in their team","They value the social side of the club particularly highly","They are the ones most likely to break the rules","They only join because their doctors insist"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Right after mentioning <b>the friendships players have made</b>, the writer notes that members who live alone <b>almost never miss a session</b>, implying the company matters most to them."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-01-3","level":"kc","title":"The Farmers of the Forest Floor","topic":"animals","words":325,"passage":"Walk through a rainforest in Central or South America and you may notice a thin green line moving across the ground. Look closer and you will see thousands of ants, each carrying a piece of leaf many times larger than its own body. These are leaf-cutter ants, and although they spend their days collecting leaves, they never actually eat them.<br><br>Instead, the ants are farmers. Back in the nest, the leaves are chewed into a paste and used to grow a special fungus, which is the colony's only food. The fungus cannot survive outside the nest, and the ants cannot survive without the fungus. Scientists believe this partnership began more than fifty million years ago, long before humans planted their first crops.<br><br>A single colony can contain several million ants, and each has a job to do. The largest are soldiers, whose powerful jaws defend the nest. Medium-sized workers cut and carry the leaves. The smallest ants ride on top of the leaf pieces as they are carried home. For a long time, nobody understood why. Then researchers discovered that a type of fly attacks the workers while they walk, laying eggs on their heads. The tiny riders act as guards, chasing the flies away.<br><br>The ants are also careful gardeners. If a piece of leaf turns out to be harmful to the fungus, the workers stop collecting from that plant within a day or two. They remove diseased parts of the garden and carry waste to a separate area far from the growing fungus, in much the same way that human farmers keep a compost heap away from their fields.<br><br>Farmers in the region, of course, do not always see the ants as fellow farmers. A large colony can strip a fruit tree of its leaves overnight. Still, many scientists argue that we have a great deal to learn from an animal that solved the problems of agriculture millions of years before we did.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How leaf-cutter ants grow their own food and organise their colony","How scientists discovered a fly that attacks ants","Why the fungus cannot live outside an ant nest","Why leaf-cutter ants are a danger to farmers in South America"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The text explains the ants' farming of the fungus, the jobs in the colony and the way they look after their garden. The fly, the fungus and the fruit trees are supporting details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What do leaf-cutter ants eat?","options":["Pieces of fresh leaves","A fungus grown inside the nest","The flies that attack the workers","Fruit from trees near the nest"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says they <b>never actually eat</b> the leaves; paragraph 2 says the fungus is <b>the colony's only food</b>."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What is the job of the smallest ants?","options":["Cutting the leaves into pieces","Defending the nest with their powerful jaws","Protecting the workers from flies","Carrying waste out of the nest"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3: the smallest ants ride on the leaf pieces and <b>act as guards, chasing the flies away</b>. Cutting is the workers' job and defending the nest is the soldiers'."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Scientists understood the role of the smallest ants as soon as they began studying the colony.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says <b>for a long time, nobody understood why</b> the small ants rode on the leaves, so the statement contradicts the passage."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"There are more soldier ants in a colony than workers.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage describes the size and job of each group but never compares their numbers, so the statement is neither confirmed nor denied."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"strip\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to:","options":["cover completely","remove everything from","climb to the top of","protect from insects"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A colony can <b>strip a fruit tree of its leaves</b> overnight, which explains why farmers are unhappy; the ants take all the leaves away."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In the last sentence, \"before we did\" means before humans:","options":["planted leaves in the rainforest","discovered the special fungus","solved the problems of agriculture","began to study leaf-cutter ants"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Did\" replaces the verb phrase just before it: the ants <b>solved the problems of agriculture</b> millions of years before humans solved them."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-02-1","level":"kc","title":"The Calendar on the Fridge","topic":"family","words":318,"passage":"When my parents both started working full-time, our house changed. Nobody knew who was picking up my little brother from football, whose turn it was to feed the dog, or why the fridge was empty again on Thursday. My mother's answer was a large paper calendar, stuck to the fridge door with four magnets, where every member of the family had to write their plans.<br><br>At first my brother and I thought it was silly. Ten-year-old Nam drew pictures instead of words, and I \"forgot\" to write anything at all because I did not want my parents to know how often I stayed late at my friend's house. But the calendar slowly did its job. When my father wrote \"Late meeting, back at 8\" on a Tuesday, my mother knew she would have to cook, and she asked me to help. When I wrote \"Maths test\" on a Friday, nobody asked me to wash the car that evening.<br><br>The most surprising result was that we started talking more, not less. Every Sunday evening, we stood in front of the fridge for ten minutes and planned the week. Those ten minutes often turned into an hour. My father told us about problems at his office; Nam explained the rules of a game he had invented; I admitted that I was worried about choosing a school. It was the first time I had heard my parents discuss money in front of us, and although it was a little frightening, it made me feel trusted.<br><br>Two years later, my mother has a calendar on her phone that sends everyone a message automatically. It is faster and it never runs out of space. Yet the paper one is still on the fridge, covered in Nam's drawings, and on Sunday evenings we still meet in the kitchen. The plans are on the phone now. The family, it turns out, is on the fridge.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why the writer's parents decided to work full-time","The advantages of phone calendars over paper ones","How the writer's younger brother learned to write his plans","How a paper calendar helped a family organise its week and grow closer"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Every paragraph returns to the calendar and what it did for the family; the parents' jobs and the phone app are only details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the writer at first write nothing on the calendar?","options":["The calendar had no space left for another person.","The writer preferred to draw pictures instead of words.","The writer did not want the parents to know about visits to a friend's house.","The writer was too busy preparing for a Maths test."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: the writer \"forgot\" on purpose so the parents would not know how often the writer stayed late at a friend's house. Drawing pictures was Nam, not the writer."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the family's Sunday evening meetings?","options":["They were led by the writer's father.","They led to conversations the family had not had before.","They stopped when the family started using a phone calendar.","They always lasted exactly ten minutes."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer says it was \"the first time\" the parents had discussed money in front of the children, and the meetings still continue in the last paragraph. Ten minutes \"often turned into an hour\", so they were not always ten minutes."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"did its job\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","options":["worked as it was meant to","caused arguments","was taken down","became too full"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentences that follow give examples of the calendar working: the family knew who would cook and when the writer was busy."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, \"It is faster and it never runs out of space\" – the word \"It\" refers to","options":["the fridge","the family's kitchen","the paper calendar","the calendar on the mother's phone"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The previous sentence introduces the calendar on the phone that sends messages automatically; \"It\" continues that subject, and the paper one is mentioned only afterwards with \"Yet\"."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The family stopped using the paper calendar once the phone calendar was set up.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The last paragraph says the paper calendar \"is still on the fridge\" and the family still meets in the kitchen on Sundays."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Nam is usually the person who feeds the family's dog.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage mentions that nobody knew whose turn it was to feed the dog, but it never says who normally does it."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-02-2","level":"kc","title":"What a Week of Cooking Taught Me","topic":"food","words":347,"passage":"Last month our science teacher gave the class an unusual homework task: for one week, each of us had to cook one meal a day for our family and keep a diary. Most of my classmates groaned. I was quietly pleased, because I had always watched my grandmother in the kitchen and believed I knew what I was doing.<br><br>I was wrong. On the first evening I decided to make fried rice, which looked simple in every video I had seen. I put the rice in the pan while it was still hot from the cooker, added too much oil, and turned the heat up as high as possible. The result was a pale, sticky mess that my father ate politely and my sister refused to touch. My grandmother laughed and told me the secret: fried rice needs rice that was cooked yesterday and has dried out in the fridge. Fresh rice is too wet.<br><br>Each day after that I learned one more thing that videos never mention. A knife should be sharp, not because it cuts faster but because a blunt one slips. Salt added at the end tastes stronger than salt added at the start. Vegetables should go into the soup in the order of how long they take to soften, so the carrots before the leaves. None of this was complicated, but nobody had ever explained it to me, and I had never asked.<br><br>By Friday my sister ate everything on her plate. By Sunday my grandmother sat down at the table and let me serve her, which she had never done before. When I handed in my diary, I expected the teacher to talk about vitamins and energy, which we had studied in class. Instead she asked us one question: what had we learned about the people we cooked for? I wrote that my sister will eat anything if it is cut small enough, that my father says \"delicious\" before he has tasted it, and that my grandmother, who never sits still, will stop for a meal made by someone who has tried.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["The writer's grandmother is the best cook in the family.","Cooking videos give wrong information about fried rice.","A week of cooking taught the writer practical skills and something about the family.","Science homework is more useful when it is done at home."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage moves from the writer's mistakes, to the small skills learned, to the teacher's final question about the people the writer cooked for. The videos and the grandmother are details, not the point."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What went wrong with the writer's fried rice?","options":["The heat was too low to fry the rice properly.","The rice had been left in the fridge for too long.","The writer forgot to add any oil.","The rice was freshly cooked and still wet."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The grandmother explains that fried rice needs rice cooked the day before, because \"fresh rice is too wet\". The writer used too much oil and too much heat, not too little."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to the passage, why should a kitchen knife be sharp?","options":["because a knife that is not sharp can slip","because sharp knives make food taste stronger","so that food can be cut more quickly","so that vegetables soften faster in the soup"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says a knife should be sharp \"not because it cuts faster but because a blunt one slips\". Speed is the tempting wrong answer the writer rejects."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"blunt\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["too heavy","not sharp","very expensive","made of plastic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts a sharp knife with a \"blunt one\", so blunt must mean the opposite of sharp."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the writer's grandmother?","options":["She helped the writer cook every evening of the week.","She thinks cooking videos are a good way to learn.","She usually prepares and serves the meals herself.","She refused to eat the writer's fried rice."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Letting the writer serve her was something \"she had never done before\", and she \"never sits still\", which suggests she is normally the one doing the cooking. It was the sister, not the grandmother, who refused the fried rice."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention that the teacher did not talk about vitamins and energy?","options":["to explain why the homework was set by a science teacher","to suggest that the meals the writer cooked were not healthy","to show that the teacher had forgotten the science lesson","to contrast what the writer expected with the question the teacher actually asked"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer \"expected\" a talk about nutrition; \"Instead\" the teacher asked about the people they cooked for. The contrast leads to the final, most important lesson."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's father says a meal is delicious before he has tried it.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The last sentence states directly that the father says \"delicious\" before he has tasted the food."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-02-3","level":"kc","title":"The Night the Wi-Fi Went Off","topic":"technology at home","words":332,"passage":"When the storm knocked out the internet in our building last month, my first feeling was panic. It was seven in the evening, I had homework to submit online, and my younger brother had already announced that his life was over. My mother, who grew up in a house with one telephone in the hallway, simply lit a candle and asked what we wanted to do.<br><br>The truth is that our home depends on the internet more than any of us had noticed. The lights in the living room are switched on with a phone. The fridge sends a message when the milk is finished. Even the front door has a small camera that shows visitors on our screens. Without the connection, the lights still worked, but only from the old switch on the wall, which my brother could not find at first. The fridge went silent. The door became a door again.<br><br>What surprised me was how quickly the evening filled up. My brother found a box of board games under his bed that he had not opened since he was nine. My father, who normally reads the news on his tablet after dinner, told us instead about the games he had played as a boy in a village with no electricity at all. I finished my homework on paper and photographed it the next morning, and my teacher accepted it without a word.<br><br>I am not going to pretend that we have given up technology. The internet came back at midnight, and by half past twelve my brother was online again. But the evening changed one thing. We now have a \"no screens\" hour after dinner on Wednesdays. It was my brother's idea, which shows how much a single evening can change a person. Modern homes are wonderful, and I would not want to live without the fridge that talks. It is just useful to know that, if it stops talking, we still know how to talk to each other.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How dangerous storms can be for electrical equipment in a home","What a family discovered when their home lost its internet for an evening","Why smart fridges and door cameras are a waste of money","How the writer's father grew up in a village without electricity"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The storm and the father's childhood are single details; the whole passage describes one evening without internet and what the family learned from it. The writer does not say smart devices are a waste of money."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following still worked while the internet was off?","options":["the living-room lights, using the switch on the wall","the system for submitting homework online","the camera on the front door","the messages from the fridge"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: the lights \"still worked, but only from the old switch on the wall\". The fridge went silent, the door camera stopped, and the writer had to do the homework on paper."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"knocked out\" in the first sentence is closest in meaning to","options":["slowed down","made more expensive","repaired","put out of action"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The storm caused the internet to stop working completely; it did not come back until midnight."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, \"It was my brother's idea\" – the word \"It\" refers to","options":["the storm","the box of board games","the hour without screens after dinner on Wednesdays","the internet connection"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence before introduces the \"no screens\" hour, and the writer then says whose idea it was. The board games belong to the previous paragraph."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention that the mother grew up with one telephone in the hallway?","options":["to describe how old the family's building is","to explain why the mother stayed calm when the internet stopped","to show that the mother dislikes modern technology","to suggest that telephones are more reliable than the internet"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The detail comes right before the mother \"simply lit a candle\": someone who grew up with little technology is not worried by losing it. Nothing says she dislikes technology."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's teacher refused to accept the homework because it was done on paper.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the teacher \"accepted it without a word\" after the writer photographed the paper homework."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's father and mother grew up in the same village.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We are told the father grew up in a village with no electricity and the mother in a house with one telephone, but the passage never says where the mother lived or whether it was the same place."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-03-1","level":"kc","title":"Slow Travel: Why Some Tourists Are Taking the Long Way","topic":"travel","words":321,"passage":"When Linh's family planned their first trip to Da Lat, her father wanted to fly. A plane would take fifty minutes; the bus would take eight hours. In the end, they chose the bus, and Linh says it was the best decision of the holiday. \"On the plane you see clouds,\" she explains. \"On the bus we saw rice fields, waterfalls and a village where every house was painted blue.\"<br><br>Linh's family are part of a growing movement known as \"slow travel\". Instead of visiting as many places as possible in a week, slow travellers stay longer in one town, walk or cycle rather than take taxis, and eat where local people eat. The idea is simple: a holiday should be about experience, not about ticking off a list of famous sights.<br><br>Supporters of slow travel point to several advantages. It is often cheaper, because buses and trains cost less than flights and a week in a rented room costs less than seven nights in seven hotels. It is also better for the environment, since a single flight produces far more carbon dioxide per passenger than a train journey of the same distance. Perhaps most importantly, travellers say they return home less tired. \"A fast holiday feels like homework,\" one blogger wrote. \"A slow one feels like a rest.\"<br><br>Of course, slow travel is not for everyone. People with only three days off cannot spend two of them on a train. Families with very young children may find long journeys difficult. And some travellers simply enjoy the excitement of a new city every morning.<br><br>Still, the trend is spreading. Several Vietnamese travel companies now sell \"one town, one week\" packages, and the number of overnight train tickets sold between Ha Noi and Hue rose by nearly a third last year. Linh has already decided where her family will go next summer. \"Somewhere far,\" she says, \"and we are definitely not flying.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A style of travelling that values experience over speed","Why flying is becoming less popular in Vietnam","The problems families face when they travel with children","The best ways to travel from Ho Chi Minh City to Da Lat"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The whole text explains \"slow travel\" — staying longer, moving more slowly and focusing on experience. Da Lat, flights and children each appear in only one paragraph."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to paragraph 2, slow travellers usually","options":["stay in a different hotel every night","spend more time in a single place","avoid eating in restaurants","visit as many famous sights as they can"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says slow travellers \"stay longer in one town\". They eat where local people eat — that is not avoiding restaurants — and they do not chase as many sights as possible."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the blogger quoted in paragraph 3?","options":["He or she prefers to travel by plane.","He or she works for a Vietnamese travel company.","He or she has tried both fast and slow holidays.","He or she thinks holidays should include some homework."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The blogger compares how a fast holiday and a slow one <b>feel</b>, which suggests personal experience of both. Nothing links the blogger to a travel company or to planes."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"ticking off\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","options":["arguing about","paying for","photographing","completing one by one"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To \"tick off a list\" is to mark each item as done. The sentence contrasts experience with finishing a list of sights one after another."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"It\" at the start of the second sentence of paragraph 3 refers to","options":["slow travel","the environment","a rented room","a flight"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The paragraph lists the advantages of slow travel; \"It is often cheaper\" and \"It is also better for the environment\" both describe slow travel."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Linh's father was the first person in the family to suggest taking the bus.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says her father <b>wanted to fly</b>; the family chose the bus in the end. The statement contradicts the text, so it is False."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Overnight train tickets between Ha Noi and Hue became cheaper last year.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The last paragraph says the <b>number</b> of tickets sold rose by nearly a third. It says nothing about the price, so the statement is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-03-2","level":"kc","title":"What a Weather Forecast Really Promises","topic":"weather","words":319,"passage":"Every evening, millions of people check tomorrow's weather before they go to bed. If the forecast says \"70% chance of rain\", most of them will pack an umbrella. But surprisingly few could explain what that number actually means, and the confusion leads to a lot of unfair complaints.<br><br>A forecast of \"70% chance of rain\" does not mean that it will rain for 70% of the day, or that 70% of the city will get wet. It means that, on days with conditions like tomorrow's, rain fell at your location seven times out of ten. Three times out of ten, it stayed dry. So if you wake up to blue sky, the forecaster was not wrong. The forecast simply described a possibility, and the less likely result happened.<br><br>Forecasters gather information from thousands of sources: weather stations on the ground, balloons that rise twenty kilometres into the sky, ships, aircraft and satellites. Powerful computers then use this information to calculate how the air will move over the next few days. The calculations are remarkably good for the first three days. After that, tiny differences in the starting information grow larger and larger, and by day ten the forecast is little better than a guess based on the season.<br><br>This is why a forecaster in Ho Chi Minh City can be quite confident about tomorrow afternoon's storm but much less certain about the weekend after next. It is also why the same app may change its prediction several times in a week. The forecast is not being careless; it is being updated with fresh information as the date gets closer.<br><br>Teachers in some schools now use forecasts to introduce probability in maths lessons. Students record the predicted chance of rain each morning and note whether it actually rained. After a month, they compare the two. The results usually show that the forecasts were far more reliable than the class expected.","questions":[{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","options":["To warn readers that weather apps are often careless","To explain how forecasts work and what their numbers mean","To describe a maths lesson about probability","To compare forecasting in different cities"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer explains what \"70% chance of rain\" means and how forecasters produce it. The maths lesson is only a final example, and the text says forecasts are <b>not</b> careless."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to paragraph 2, a forecast of \"70% chance of rain\" means that","options":["in similar conditions, it has rained at that place 7 times out of 10","the forecaster is 70% sure that it will not rain","it will rain during 70% of the day","70% of the city will receive rain"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The text rejects the first two ideas directly and then gives the real meaning: in conditions like these, rain fell \"seven times out of ten\"."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a source of information for forecasters?","options":["Balloons","Satellites","Ships","Mountain climbers"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists weather stations, balloons, ships, aircraft and satellites. Mountain climbers are not mentioned anywhere."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the same weather app change its prediction several times in a week?","options":["Because users complain about the earlier forecast","Because the forecasters keep making mistakes","Because it receives new information as the day approaches","Because different cities send different data"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the forecast is \"being updated with fresh information as the date gets closer\", not that anyone made a mistake."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"reliable\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["expensive","trustworthy","surprising","difficult"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Something reliable can be trusted or depended on. The students found the forecasts could be trusted more than they had thought."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Forecasts for the next three days are usually more accurate than forecasts for ten days ahead.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the calculations are \"remarkably good for the first three days\" but by day ten are \"little better than a guess\". So the statement is True."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Most students in the maths lessons later decided to become forecasters.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The last paragraph only says students compared forecasts with real weather and found them reliable. Nothing is said about their future careers, so it is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-03-3","level":"kc","title":"The Boy Who Collects Sounds","topic":"hobbies","words":323,"passage":"Most teenagers collect something at some point: stamps, football cards, key rings, perhaps old coins. Fifteen-year-old Minh collects sounds. On his phone he has more than four hundred short recordings: rain on a metal roof, a motorbike starting on a cold morning, his grandmother's kettle, the school bell on the last day of term.<br><br>The hobby began by accident. Two years ago, Minh was waiting for a bus and heard a street seller singing to advertise sticky rice. He recorded it so that he could play it to his cousins in Australia, who had never heard the song. When he listened again at home, he realised that the recording had captured something a photograph never could: the whole feeling of that early morning corner.<br><br>Since then, Minh has recorded almost every day. He does not need expensive equipment; a phone and a cheap wind cover for the microphone are enough. What he does need, he says, is patience. \"You have to stand still for a long time and let the place forget you are there. If you move, the sound changes.\"<br><br>His friends thought the hobby was strange at first. Some still do. But Minh's recordings have found a surprising audience. His history teacher played the sticky-rice song to a class studying old Saigon trades. A cousin used the sound of the family kitchen in a short film for a university project. And last month, a community radio station in District 5 broadcast ten minutes of Minh's morning market recordings, with no talking at all. Listeners phoned in to say it reminded them of their childhood.<br><br>Minh has no plans to become a professional sound engineer. \"That would turn it into work,\" he says. For now, he is happy simply to keep listening. His next project is to record the same street corner at the same time every month for a year, to find out how a place changes when nobody is looking.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is the best summary of the passage?","options":["Why photographs cannot capture the feeling of a place","The best equipment for teenagers who want to record music","A teenager's unusual hobby of recording everyday sounds and the interest it has attracted","How a community radio station in District 5 found a new type of programme"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The text follows Minh's hobby from its beginning to the audience it now has. The radio station and the photograph comparison are single details, and the recordings are of everyday sounds, not music."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Minh make his very first recording?","options":["He was making a short film for a university project.","He wanted to test a new microphone he had bought.","His history teacher asked him to record old Saigon trades.","He wanted to share the street seller's song with relatives abroad."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he recorded the sticky-rice song \"so that he could play it to his cousins in Australia\". The teacher and the film came much later."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does Minh mean when he says a place must \"forget you are there\"?","options":["The recorder should stay still so that normal sounds continue naturally.","It is best to record places where there are no people.","People should not be told that they are being recorded.","A recorder should hide behind something so nobody can see him."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The quotation continues: \"If you move, the sound changes.\" His point is about staying still and letting the place return to normal, not about hiding or secrecy."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"captured\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","options":["made louder","caught and kept","destroyed","copied from someone else"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Here \"captured\" means recorded or held onto — the recording caught and kept the feeling of the morning corner."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"it\" in \"it reminded them of their childhood\" (paragraph 4) refers to","options":["the sticky-rice song","the community radio station","the broadcast of Minh's market recordings","the university project"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Listeners phoned the station after hearing the ten minutes of market recordings; \"it\" is that broadcast. The sticky-rice song was played to a history class, not on the radio."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Minh uses professional recording equipment that cost a lot of money.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he \"does not need expensive equipment; a phone and a cheap wind cover ... are enough\". The statement is False."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"All of Minh's friends now think his hobby is a good idea.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says his friends thought the hobby was strange at first and \"Some still do.\" So not all of them approve — the statement is False."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-04-1","level":"kc","title":"The Homework Nobody Sets","topic":"health","words":310,"passage":"Every evening, fifteen-year-old Linh finishes her exercises at about eleven o'clock, checks her phone \"for a minute\", and finally turns off the light after midnight. Six hours later her alarm rings. She is not unusual. A survey of secondary schools in Ho Chi Minh City last year found that fewer than one student in five was getting the eight to ten hours of sleep that doctors recommend for teenagers.<br><br>Why does it matter? Sleep is not simply a pause between two days. While we sleep, the brain sorts through what it has learned and moves the useful parts into long-term memory. A student who studies until two in the morning may remember less the next day than one who stopped at ten and slept properly. Sleep also repairs muscles, controls appetite and helps the body fight illness. Teenagers who regularly sleep too little are more likely to catch colds, gain weight and feel low.<br><br>Part of the problem is biology. During the teenage years the body's internal clock shifts later, so a fifteen-year-old genuinely does not feel tired at ten o'clock in the way a ten-year-old does. Schools, however, still start early. The other part of the problem is light. Screens give off a blue light which tells the brain that it is still daytime, so scrolling in bed delays sleep even further.<br><br>The good news is that small changes help. Doctors suggest keeping the same bedtime at weekends, putting the phone outside the bedroom, and doing the hardest homework first, while the mind is fresh. Some schools in other countries have moved their first lesson to nine o'clock and reported better marks and fewer absences. Linh has not persuaded her school to do the same, but she now charges her phone in the kitchen. \"I still don't love my alarm,\" she says, \"but at least I hear it now.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why teenagers should study late at night","The importance of sleep for teenagers and how to get more of it","The results of a survey of schools in Ho Chi Minh City","The dangers of using mobile phones"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The survey and the phone are only details in one paragraph each. The whole passage explains why teenagers need sleep (paragraphs 2–3) and what helps them get it (paragraph 4)."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to the survey, how many students were getting enough sleep?","options":["Fewer than twenty per cent","About half","Eight out of ten","Nine out of ten"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Fewer than one student in five\" means fewer than 20%. \"Eight to ten\" in the same sentence is the number of hours, not the number of students."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does paragraph 2 suggest about a student who studies until two in the morning?","options":["He or she remembers more because of the extra hours of study.","He or she will gain weight the next day.","He or she will certainly fail the next test.","He or she may remember less than a student who slept properly."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer says such a student \"may remember less the next day\" — a possibility, not a certainty. Weight gain is linked to <b>regularly</b> sleeping too little, not to one late night."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"shifts\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["breaks","speeds up","moves","stops"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The internal clock \"shifts later\", i.e. it <b>moves</b> to a later time, which is why teenagers do not feel tired at ten o'clock."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, \"the same\" refers to","options":["keeping the same bedtime at weekends","starting the first lesson at nine o'clock","doing the hardest homework first","charging the phone in the kitchen"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence before describes schools that moved their first lesson to nine o'clock; Linh \"has not persuaded her school to do the same\" — to make that change."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Linh's school now begins lessons at nine o'clock.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says Linh \"has not persuaded her school\" to move its first lesson, so the statement is false. Only schools in other countries have done this."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Linh's marks have improved since she moved her phone out of her bedroom.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Better marks are mentioned only for schools abroad that changed their timetable. The passage says nothing about Linh's marks, so this is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-04-2","level":"kc","title":"Life in the Alley","topic":"city life","words":302,"passage":"Visitors to Vietnam's big cities usually notice the main roads first: the wide avenues, the tall glass towers and the endless stream of motorbikes. Yet most city people do not actually live on those roads. They live in the alleys, the narrow lanes that run behind the main streets like the branches of a tree. Some are wide enough for a car; many are so narrow that two motorbikes must slow down to pass each other.<br><br>An alley is more than a way to get home. In the early morning it becomes a market, with women selling vegetables and sticky rice from baskets. By mid-morning it is a workshop: a man repairs electric fans outside his door, and a neighbour hangs freshly washed clothes across the passage. In the evening the same few square metres turn into a dining room, a football pitch and a meeting place for grandparents, who bring out plastic chairs and watch the world go by.<br><br>This shared life has real advantages. Neighbours know who belongs and who does not, so children play outside with little supervision, and an elderly person who has not opened their door by ten o'clock will soon receive a knock. Compared with a block of flats, where families may live for years without learning each other's names, the alley is remarkably social.<br><br>It has its problems too. Fire engines cannot enter the narrowest alleys, and rubbish collection is difficult. As land prices rise, developers are keen to replace whole neighbourhoods with apartment towers, and some younger residents welcome the idea of a lift and a car park. Others are not so sure. \"In a tower you have privacy,\" says Mrs Hoa, who has sold noodles from her doorway for thirty years. \"Here I have people. I know which one I would rather lose.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How alleys work as shared living spaces and why they may change","Why developers prefer to build apartment towers","The daily routine of a noodle seller","The traffic problems of Vietnam's big cities"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraphs 2–3 describe how the alley is used and shared, and paragraph 4 explains the pressure to replace it. Traffic, developers and Mrs Hoa are details, not the main topic."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Which activity is NOT mentioned in paragraph 2?","options":["Repairing electrical goods","Washing motorbikes","Playing football","Selling food from baskets"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 mentions selling sticky rice, repairing fans, hanging washed <b>clothes</b> and a football pitch. Nobody washes a motorbike — the washing is of clothes."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about an elderly person who has not opened their door by ten o'clock?","options":["They need supervision in the same way that children do.","They are certainly ill.","A neighbour will come to check that they are all right.","They live alone in a block of flats."],"correct":2,"explanation":"They \"will soon receive a knock\" because neighbours notice — the knock is a check, not proof of illness. The block of flats is the contrasting example, not where this person lives."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"keen\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["afraid","unable","slow","eager"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Developers are \"keen to replace\" neighbourhoods because land prices are rising — they are <b>eager</b> to do it. \"Keen to\" + verb means wanting very much to."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer include Mrs Hoa's words at the end?","options":["To show what some residents feel they would lose in a tower","To explain why her noodles are popular","To prove that towers offer more privacy than alleys","To show that every resident is against the new towers"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Her quote follows \"Others are not so sure\" and shows the view of residents who value people over privacy. \"Some\" younger residents welcome the towers, so not everyone is against them."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Fire engines are able to reach every alley in the city.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that fire engines \"cannot enter the narrowest alleys\", so the statement is false."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mrs Hoa's children would like to move to an apartment tower.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says \"some younger residents\" welcome the towers, but it never mentions Mrs Hoa's children, so this is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-04-3","level":"kc","title":"Is It Too Late to Learn the Guitar?","topic":"music","words":311,"passage":"Ask most people when they should have started learning an instrument and they will sigh and say \"as a child\". There is some truth in this. Young children pick up rhythm and pitch easily, and their hands grow used to the shapes of chords before they know the names of the notes. But the idea that a teenager or an adult has missed the boat is simply wrong, and it stops thousands of people from doing something they would enjoy.<br><br>Research on learning suggests that older beginners have advantages of their own. A fifteen-year-old can read instructions, understand why a scale is built the way it is, and practise on purpose rather than by accident. Children often need a parent to remind them; a teenager who has chosen the guitar herself will find twenty minutes for it without being asked. Motivation, not age, is the best predictor of who will still be playing a year later.<br><br>What does hold older beginners back is embarrassment. A six-year-old does not mind sounding terrible; a sixteen-year-old minds very much, especially when a cousin has been playing since she was four. Teachers say the students who improve fastest are those who accept that the first month will sound bad and simply keep going. Recording yourself once a week, and listening back a month later, is one way to see progress that is invisible from day to day.<br><br>The other secret is to play music you actually like. Traditional lessons often begin with exercises that nobody would choose to hear, and beginners give up before they reach a real song. Learning three chords and a song you love in the first week does more for a new player than a term of scales. The scales can come later — and, oddly enough, they usually do, once the player wants to understand what she is already doing.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main point?","options":["Scales and exercises are a waste of time.","Embarrassment is the main reason people stop playing.","Children learn instruments much faster than teenagers.","It is not too late for a teenager to learn an instrument."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the idea that older learners have \"missed the boat\" is wrong, and the rest of the passage explains why. Scales are not useless — the writer says they \"can come later\"."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to paragraph 2, what best predicts who will still be playing after a year?","options":["Whether they can read instructions","The age at which they started","How motivated they are","Whether a parent reminds them to practise"],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"Motivation, not age, is the best predictor.\" Reading instructions and parents' reminders are mentioned in the paragraph but are not named as the predictor."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"missed the boat\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to","options":["forgotten something important","lost the chance","arrived late for a lesson","made a serious mistake"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence is about the belief that a teenager is now too old to start — that the chance has gone. \"Miss the boat\" is an idiom meaning to lose an opportunity."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention a cousin who has played since she was four?","options":["To show how comparing yourself with others causes embarrassment","To prove that four is the best age to begin","To suggest that the cousin has probably given up","To show that the cousin would make a good teacher"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The cousin appears in the sentence about a sixteen-year-old who \"minds very much\" sounding bad — the comparison is what makes the embarrassment worse."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In the last sentence, \"they\" refers to","options":["the beginners","the three chords","the traditional lessons","the scales"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"The scales can come later — and … they usually do\": <b>they</b> = the scales, which do come later once the player wants to understand the music."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer advises beginners to record themselves every day.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The advice is to record yourself \"once a week\" and listen back a month later, so \"every day\" makes the statement false."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer plays the guitar herself.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage gives the views of teachers and research but never says whether the writer plays an instrument, so this is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-05-1","level":"kc","title":"Two Chairs by the Window","topic":"friendship","words":317,"passage":"When my family moved to Da Nang, I was eleven and certain that I would never make another friend. My old school was five hundred kilometres away, and my best friend, Linh, had promised to write every week. She kept her promise for about a month. After that, the letters became shorter, and then they stopped. I did not blame her. She had a new class, a new football team and, I supposed, new friends.<br><br>The girl who sat next to me in my new school was called Mai. She did not say much at first. In fact, for the whole of the first term, our conversations were mostly about borrowing pens. Then one rainy afternoon, when the buses were late, we sat on two plastic chairs by the classroom window and talked for an hour. I discovered that she collected old stamps, that she was afraid of dogs, and that she had also moved schools, twice. By the end of the year, we were doing homework at each other's houses.<br><br>People sometimes say that a true friend is someone you can be silent with. I think that is only half the story. Mai and I could be silent together, but we could also argue, and the arguments never lasted. Once, we did not speak for three days after a disagreement about a school project. On the fourth day, she left a stamp from Brazil on my desk. Neither of us mentioned the argument again.<br><br>I am fifteen now, and Linh and I still send each other a message on our birthdays. I do not think of her as a lost friend, just a friend from another chapter. What the last four years have taught me is that friendship does not depend on how long you have known someone. It depends on whether both people are willing to sit down on those two chairs and keep talking.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why silence is the true test of a friendship","Why the writer's old friend stopped writing letters","How the writer came to understand what keeps a friendship alive","The difficulties of moving to a new city at the age of eleven"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Every paragraph builds towards the final lesson: friendship depends on both people being willing to keep talking. Linh's letters and the move are details, and the writer says silence is only <b>half</b> the story."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to the first paragraph, why did Linh's letters stop?","options":["The writer stopped replying to them.","She was angry that the writer had moved away.","The distance was too great for letters to arrive.","She had become busy with her new life."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer does not blame Linh because she had a new class, a new team and, probably, new friends — in other words, a new life. Nothing suggests anger or a problem with the post."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about Mai during the first term?","options":["She was quiet and took time to open up.","She never brought her own pens to school.","She was often absent from class.","She disliked the writer and avoided her."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Mai 'did not say much at first' and the two only talked about pens until a rainy afternoon changed things. That points to shyness, not dislike — later they became close friends."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'chapter' in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["a section of a book","a period of someone's life","a school year","a distant town"],"correct":1,"explanation":"'A friend from another chapter' means a friend from an earlier period of the writer's life. The literal book meaning does not fit the context."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, 'that' in 'I think that is only half the story' refers to","options":["the disagreement about a school project","the writer's friendship with Mai","the saying that a true friend is someone you can be silent with","the fact that arguments never lasted"],"correct":2,"explanation":"'That' points back to the sentence just before it — the popular saying about silence. The writer then adds the other half: real friends can also argue and recover."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mai had changed schools more times than the writer.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The only school change the writer describes is the one move to Da Nang, while Mai <b>had also moved schools, twice</b>. The word also invites the comparison: two moves against one, so the statement is True."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer and Linh have met in person since the move to Da Nang.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We are only told that they exchange birthday messages. The passage says nothing about whether they have ever met again, so the answer is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-05-2","level":"kc","title":"Jobs That Did Not Have Names","topic":"jobs","words":318,"passage":"Ask your grandparents what they wanted to be when they were fifteen, and you will probably hear a short list: teacher, doctor, farmer, soldier, shopkeeper. Ask a fifteen-year-old today and the list is longer and stranger. Some want to be game designers, drone pilots or online video makers. Twenty years ago, most of those jobs did not even have names.<br><br>Careers advisers say this change is happening faster than ever. A report from a Vietnamese employment website last year found that nearly a third of the jobs advertised in Ho Chi Minh City did not exist in the same form in 2010. Many of them are connected to technology, but not all. Delivery riders, for example, have become a common sight in every city, and their work depends on a phone app that was invented only a few years ago.<br><br>For students, this can sound worrying. How can you prepare for a job that has not been invented yet? Nguyen Thi Hoa, who advises secondary-school students in Hanoi, tells them not to panic. \"Nobody expects you to choose one job for life anymore,\" she says. \"Employers are looking for people who can learn quickly and work well with others. Those skills do not go out of date.\"<br><br>She also warns against following the crowd. When a job looks exciting on social media, thousands of young people rush towards it, and within a few years there are too many of them. Careers that look boring today, such as plumbing or nursing, may be the ones with the best pay and the most security in 2040.<br><br>Perhaps the best advice comes from Hoa's own story. She trained as an accountant, disliked it, worked in a bookshop for six years, and finally became a careers adviser at thirty-five. \"Every job I did taught me something I use now,\" she says. \"The path was not straight, but none of it was wasted.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main message of the passage?","options":["Grandparents had fewer job choices than young people today.","Technology has created most of the jobs that exist today.","Students should train for jobs such as plumbing and nursing.","Since jobs keep changing, students should build skills that last rather than fix on one career."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage moves from 'jobs are changing fast' to Hoa's advice: learn quickly, work with others, do not choose one job for life. Plumbing and nursing are just examples, and technology explains 'many but not all' of the new jobs."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What did the employment website's report find about jobs advertised in Ho Chi Minh City?","options":["Nearly a third had not existed twenty years ago.","Almost all of them were connected to technology.","Nearly a third had not existed in the same form in 2010.","Most of them were for delivery riders."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The report compares today's jobs with 2010, not with twenty years ago, and says nearly a third did not exist 'in the same form'. Technology explains many, not almost all."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does Hoa mention plumbing and nursing?","options":["Because she once worked in both of them.","Because unfashionable jobs may turn out to be the best paid and most secure.","Because they are likely to disappear by 2040.","Because they are the jobs most young people rush towards."],"correct":1,"explanation":"They are given as examples of careers that 'look boring today' but may offer 'the best pay and the most security in 2040' — the opposite of following the crowd."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase 'go out of date' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["stop being useful","take a long time to learn","become too expensive","change their names"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Something that goes out of date is no longer useful or wanted. Hoa means that learning fast and working with others will always be needed."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer include Hoa's own story in the last paragraph?","options":["To recommend working in a bookshop before choosing a career","To prove that Hoa made a serious mistake when she was young","To show that accountants are badly paid","To show that a career path can change direction without being wasted"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Her story ends with 'The path was not straight, but none of it was wasted' — a real-life example of the flexible attitude she recommends. She never calls her early choices a mistake."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The work of delivery riders depends on a recently invented phone app.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says their work 'depends on a phone app that was invented only a few years ago'. True."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Hoa left the bookshop because it closed down.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We know she worked in a bookshop for six years and then became a careers adviser, but the passage gives no reason for leaving. Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-05-3","level":"kc","title":"The Forests Under the Waves","topic":"the sea","words":327,"passage":"When people imagine a forest, they picture tall trees, birdsong and a floor covered with leaves. Very few think of the sea. Yet along many cold, rocky coasts, from California to South Africa to Japan, there are forests that grow entirely under water. They are made not of trees but of kelp, a giant brown seaweed that can grow half a metre in a single day and reach the height of a ten-storey building.<br><br>Kelp does not have roots. Instead, it holds onto rocks with a strong, hand-like part called a holdfast, and it rises towards the light with the help of small air-filled balls along its stem. From below, a kelp forest looks like a cathedral: long golden columns with sunlight moving between them.<br><br>These forests are home to an astonishing number of creatures. Small fish hide among the leaves from larger hunters. Sea urchins eat the kelp itself, and sea otters eat the urchins. This last relationship matters more than it sounds. In the 1800s, otters were hunted almost to extinction for their fur. With the otters gone, urchin numbers exploded, and whole kelp forests were eaten down to bare rock. Scientists call these empty areas 'urchin barrens'. When otters were protected and returned to the coast of California, the kelp slowly came back.<br><br>Kelp forests are also useful to people. They soften the force of waves, protecting beaches and harbours from storms. They take in carbon dioxide as they grow. And kelp is harvested for food and for a substance used in ice cream and toothpaste.<br><br>Today, however, kelp forests are shrinking in several parts of the world, mainly because warmer seas weaken the plants and encourage the animals that eat them. Some scientists are now replanting kelp by hand, much as foresters replant trees on land. It is slow work, but early results suggest that a forest under the waves, like one above them, can grow back if it is given the chance.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best summarises the passage?","options":["Kelp forests are rich and useful ecosystems that are under threat but can recover.","Sea otters are the most important animals in the ocean.","Kelp is a valuable product used in food and toothpaste.","Warmer seas have destroyed most of the world's kelp forests."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage describes what kelp forests are, who lives in them, why they matter and how they can come back. Otters and toothpaste are single details, and 'most' overstates the shrinking described in the last paragraph."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"How does kelp stay attached to the sea floor?","options":["With deep roots that grow into the sand","With a hand-like part that grips the rocks","With air-filled balls along its stem","By wrapping its leaves around stones"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says kelp has no roots; it holds onto rocks with a 'holdfast'. The air-filled balls help it float upwards, not stay attached."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the story of the sea otters show?","options":["Otters eat kelp when there are no urchins left.","Sea urchins have now died out along the coast of California.","Removing one animal can change a whole underwater forest.","Hunting otters for fur is still common today."],"correct":2,"explanation":"When the otters disappeared, urchins multiplied and ate whole forests; when otters returned, the kelp came back. One missing animal changed everything. The passage never says otters eat kelp or that urchins died out."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'exploded' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["became dangerous","fell suddenly","burst with a loud noise","increased very quickly"],"correct":3,"explanation":"'Urchin numbers exploded' means the urchin population grew very fast — so fast that whole forests were eaten. It has nothing to do with a real explosion."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, 'This last relationship' refers to","options":["sea otters eating the sea urchins","people hunting otters for fur","small fish hiding from larger hunters","sea urchins eating the kelp"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence before lists three feeding links; the last one mentioned is 'sea otters eat the urchins', and the rest of the paragraph explains why that link matters."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Kelp forests are found only in warm tropical seas.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says they grow along 'cold, rocky coasts' and the last paragraph says warmer seas weaken them. So the statement is false."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Most of the kelp harvested for food is eaten in Japan.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage mentions that kelp is harvested for food and that Japan has kelp forests, but it never says where the food is eaten. Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-11-1","level":"kc","title":"Forty Minutes and One Pan","topic":"a cooking competition","words":318,"passage":"The rules of the Riverside Youth Cooking Cup are simple, and that is exactly what makes it hard. Each team of two receives the same basket of ingredients, one pan, one small stove and forty minutes. Nobody knows what is in the basket until a bell rings. Last Saturday, in the town hall, twelve teams opened their baskets to find a fish, three eggs, a bunch of spring onions, half a lemon and a bag of rice.<br><br>Fourteen-year-old Linh and her partner Bao had been practising for weeks, but not in the way most people expected. \"We didn't learn recipes,\" Linh explains. \"We learned to plan. In the first two minutes we decide who does what, and we don't change the plan even if something goes wrong.\" While Bao boiled the rice, Linh cleaned the fish and cut the onions. Their fish rice with lemon and egg was ready with three minutes to spare.<br><br>Other teams were less fortunate. One pair spent so long arguing about what to cook that their rice was still hard when the final bell rang. Another team produced a dish the judges described as \"beautiful but cold\", because they had plated it too early. The head judge, a chef named Mr Duc, said afterwards that the contest was never really about cooking. \"I'm watching how they work under pressure,\" he said. \"A calm team with a plain dish will beat a talented team in chaos every time.\"<br><br>Linh and Bao finished second. The winners, two boys from a school across the river, made a fish soup that Mr Duc called \"the only dish I wanted a second bowl of\". Linh says she is not disappointed. \"We did what we planned to do,\" she says. \"Next year we'll plan something braver.\" The organisers have already announced that next year's basket will contain one ingredient that most of the contestants will never have cooked before.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why Linh and Bao failed to win the Riverside Youth Cooking Cup","The history of cooking competitions held in the town hall","How to cook fish rice with lemon and egg in under forty minutes","A cooking contest and what the judges were really looking for"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage describes the contest and returns to the same idea: the judges cared about planning and calm under pressure, not just food. The recipe and the second place are details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"How did Linh and Bao prepare for the competition?","options":["They memorised a large number of recipes.","They practised cooking fish soup every weekend.","They practised dividing the work and sticking to a plan.","They asked Mr Duc for advice about the basket."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Linh says, \"We didn't learn recipes. We learned to plan,\" and that they decide who does what and do not change the plan. Fish soup was the winners' dish, not theirs."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the team whose dish was \"beautiful but cold\"?","options":["They used more than one pan.","They finished cooking too long before the judges tasted it.","They forgot to turn on their stove.","They spent most of the time arguing."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says they had \"plated it too early\", so the food cooled before the judges tasted it. The arguing team is a different pair mentioned in the same paragraph."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"chaos\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["complete disorder","a small kitchen","great skill","silence"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Mr Duc contrasts \"a calm team\" with \"a talented team in chaos\". The opposite of calm here is disorder or confusion."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 1, \"that\" in \"that is exactly what makes it hard\" refers to","options":["the bell","the basket of ingredients","the town hall","the fact that the rules are simple"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence says the rules are simple, and \"that\" (the simplicity) is what makes the contest hard."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Linh and Bao's dish was finished before the time ran out.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage says their dish \"was ready with three minutes to spare\", so it was finished before the forty minutes ended."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mr Duc tasted every dish twice.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"He said the winners' soup was the only dish he \"wanted a second bowl of\", but the passage never says how many times he actually tasted any dish."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-11-2","level":"kc","title":"Where the Town Wakes Up","topic":"street markets","words":335,"passage":"If you want to know what a town is really like, do not visit its museum. Go to its street market at six in the morning. In Tan An, a small town in the south, the market fills three streets around an old banyan tree, and by the time most people are awake, half of the day's business is already done.<br><br>The market has no roof and no fixed stalls. Each seller arrives with a bamboo basket, a plastic sheet or, at most, a small cart, and takes the same spot every day. Mrs Hai, who has sold bananas under the banyan tree for twenty-two years, says the sellers have their own quiet system. \"Nobody has written it down,\" she says, \"but everyone knows whose place is whose. If a new person sits in my spot, the others will explain it to them before I even arrive.\"<br><br>Prices here are rarely fixed. A customer asks, the seller answers, and the real price is somewhere in between. Visitors from the city sometimes find this tiring, but regular customers say it is the best part of the morning. \"It isn't really about saving money,\" says Mr Thanh, a retired teacher who shops there daily. \"You ask about her grandson, she asks about your knee, and then you agree on a price. It's how we stay friends.\"<br><br>The market is not without problems. Motorbikes squeeze between the baskets, and on rainy days the streets turn to mud. Two years ago, the town council proposed moving all the sellers into a new covered building on the edge of town. The plan was dropped after almost every seller refused to go. \"A market is not a building,\" Mrs Hai said at the meeting. \"It's the people who come to it, and they won't walk that far.\"<br><br>For now, the banyan tree stays, and so do the baskets beneath it. By nine o'clock the streets are almost empty again, swept clean, as if the market had never been there at all.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best describes the passage?","options":["A guide to the cheapest fruit in Tan An","An argument for moving markets into modern buildings","A description of a street market and why people value it","The life story of a banana seller"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage describes how the market works, how people bargain and why sellers refused to move. Mrs Hai is one example, not the whole subject."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"How do sellers know where to set up each day?","options":["The first seller to arrive chooses the best place.","Mrs Hai decides where the new sellers should sit.","The town council gives each seller a written permit.","There is an unwritten agreement that everyone understands."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Mrs Hai says \"Nobody has written it down, but everyone knows whose place is whose.\" It is a shared understanding, not a permit or a first-come rule."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does Mr Thanh suggest about bargaining?","options":["It is a social habit that keeps people connected.","It is something only city visitors enjoy.","It should be replaced by fixed prices.","It is mainly a way to get the lowest price."],"correct":0,"explanation":"He says \"It isn't really about saving money\" and describes asking about family and health before agreeing a price. For him, bargaining is about friendship."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"proposed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["refused","suggested","finished","forgot"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The council \"proposed moving all the sellers\" and the plan was later dropped, so the council put forward or suggested the idea rather than carrying it out."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention motorbikes and rainy days?","options":["To prove that the new building was a better idea","To describe the town's main tourist attraction","To show that the market has real disadvantages","To explain why the market closes by nine o'clock"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The paragraph begins \"The market is not without problems,\" and the motorbikes and mud are examples of those problems. The writer does not say the building was better."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The covered building was built on the edge of town but the sellers refused to use it.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The building was only proposed, and \"the plan was dropped\". The passage does not say it was ever built."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mrs Hai has sold bananas at the market for more than twenty years.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage says she has sold bananas under the banyan tree \"for twenty-two years\", which is more than twenty."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-11-3","level":"kc","title":"Ten Minutes Before Breakfast","topic":"learning an instrument","words":321,"passage":"When Khanh, aged thirteen, asked for a guitar, his parents agreed on one condition: he had to keep playing for a year. He said yes without thinking. Three weeks later, his fingertips hurt, the strings buzzed whenever he touched them, and the guitar was leaning in a corner of his bedroom, gathering dust.<br><br>What changed things was not a better teacher or a more expensive instrument. It was a suggestion from his uncle, who had played in a band when he was young. \"Don't try to practise for an hour,\" his uncle said. \"You'll never find an hour. Play for ten minutes before breakfast, every single day, and don't miss one.\" Khanh thought ten minutes was too short to be useful, but he agreed to try.<br><br>The first month was boring. He played the same three chords again and again while his rice was getting cold. But because the sessions were so short, he never had a reason to skip one. By the second month, the buzzing had stopped. By the third, he could change chords without looking at his fingers, and he began adding a fourth chord, then a fifth.<br><br>Music teachers say this pattern is common. Learners who practise a little every day usually improve faster than those who practise for a long time once a week, because the hands forget quickly. A short daily session also removes the biggest obstacle, which is simply starting. \"The hardest note,\" one teacher told me, \"is the first one.\"<br><br>Khanh has now played for fourteen months, longer than the year his parents asked for. He still practises before breakfast, though the sessions have grown to twenty minutes because he does not want to stop. Last week he played his first song in front of his class. He made two mistakes in the middle, and nobody noticed. \"I used to think I wasn't musical,\" he says. \"Now I think I just wasn't patient.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main message of the passage?","options":["Expensive instruments make learning easier.","Short, regular practice is more effective than occasional long sessions.","Children should not be forced to learn music.","Learning the guitar always hurts your fingers at first."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The uncle's advice, Khanh's progress and the teachers' view all support the same point: a little practice every day works better than a long session once a week."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why had Khanh stopped playing after three weeks?","options":["Playing was painful and the sound was poor.","He was too busy with school to find an hour.","His parents took the guitar away.","His uncle told him to wait until he was older."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage says his fingertips hurt and the strings buzzed, and the guitar was left in a corner. The hour problem is what the uncle warns about later, not why he stopped."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the short sessions help Khanh keep going?","options":["Because he could play five chords by the end of the first week","Because ten minutes was too little time to feel any pain","Because his uncle checked on him every morning","Because it was hard to find an excuse to miss such a short session"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage says that \"because the sessions were so short, he never had a reason to skip one\". Nothing suggests his uncle checked on him."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"obstacle\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["a kind of chord","a long lesson","something that blocks progress","a musical instrument"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence explains that the biggest obstacle \"is simply starting\", so the word means a difficulty or barrier that stops you from moving forward."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, \"this pattern\" refers to","options":["the three chords Khanh played","improving steadily through short daily practice","the way rice gets cold during practice","the sound of strings buzzing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"This pattern\" points back to Khanh's experience in paragraph 3, where daily ten-minute sessions led to gradual improvement, and the teachers say this is common."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Khanh's classmates noticed the mistakes in his song.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says he made two mistakes \"and nobody noticed\"."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Khanh's uncle still plays in a band.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We are told only that the uncle \"had played in a band when he was young\". Whether he plays now is not mentioned."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-11-4","level":"kc","title":"The Paper Nobody Picked Up","topic":"a school newspaper","words":325,"passage":"For years, the newspaper at Binh Minh Secondary School was printed four times a year, and for years it went straight from the printer to the recycling bin. It contained the head teacher's message, a list of exam results and a poem chosen by the literature department. Students walked past the pile by the front gate without looking at it.<br><br>That changed when a grade 9 student named Trang took over as editor. Her first decision was to ask a simple question in every class: \"What would you actually read?\" The answers surprised the teachers. Nobody wanted the exam results. Students wanted to know which food stall near the school was worth the walk, why the football pitch was closed for a month, and whether the rumour about a new uniform was true.<br><br>Trang's team of eight reporters set out to answer those questions. They interviewed the school cook, timed the queue at the canteen and tested every drink sold outside the gate. When the football pitch story revealed that the money for repairs had been delayed, the head teacher was not pleased at first. But she allowed the article to be printed, and the repairs were finished two weeks later.<br><br>The paper now comes out every month, and copies disappear within a day. It is still printed rather than posted online because, as Trang points out, phones are not allowed in class but a folded newspaper can be read under a desk. Teachers have accepted this with a smile.<br><br>Not everything has worked. A column of anonymous complaints had to be stopped after it turned into a place for arguments between classes. Trang says the team learned an important lesson from it. \"A newspaper is not a wall for shouting,\" she says. \"Every story needs a name on it, and someone willing to explain why they wrote it.\" She graduates next year and is already training the grade 8 student who will replace her.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a school newspaper became popular by reporting what students cared about","Why exam results should not be published in school newspapers","The problems caused by a column of anonymous complaints","How the head teacher repaired the school football pitch"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The whole passage traces the paper from being ignored to being read within a day, because Trang asked what students would actually read. The complaints column and the pitch are details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What was Trang's first action as editor?","options":["She replaced the old team of reporters.","She asked students in every class what they would read.","She moved the newspaper online.","She wrote an article about the football pitch."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage states that \"her first decision was to ask a simple question in every class\". The pitch article came later, and the paper is still printed, not online."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the passage suggest about the football pitch article?","options":["It caused arguments between classes.","It was never printed because the head teacher stopped it.","It helped speed up the repairs to the pitch.","It was written by the head teacher herself."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The head teacher allowed the article, and \"the repairs were finished two weeks later\", which suggests the story pushed the work forward. The arguments came from the complaints column."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"anonymous\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","options":["printed in colour","very long","written in a foreign language","without the writer's name"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The column was stopped and Trang concludes that \"every story needs a name on it\", so anonymous means the writers' names were not given."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention that phones are not allowed in class?","options":["To explain why the paper is printed instead of published online","To show that students prefer reading on screens","To suggest that teachers dislike the newspaper","To criticise the school's strict rules"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence directly explains the decision to print: a phone cannot be used in class, but a folded paper can be read under a desk."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The newspaper is now published more often than it used to be.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"It was printed four times a year before, and \"now comes out every month\", which is more frequent."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-12-1","level":"kc","title":"Three Hives on the Roof","topic":"keeping bees","words":350,"passage":"When my uncle Tuan told the family that he was going to keep bees on the roof of his apartment building, my grandmother laughed and said he would be stung to death in a week. That was two years ago. Today there are three wooden hives up there, and my grandmother puts his honey in her tea every morning.<br><br>Uncle Tuan did not start by buying bees. He spent a whole winter reading and watching videos, and then joined a small club of beekeepers who meet once a month in a park. An older member gave him his first colony in April, when the weather was warm and there were plenty of flowers on the trees along the streets. \"Bees do not care whether they live in the countryside or in a city,\" the man told him. \"They only care about food and a dry home.\"<br><br>The work is lighter than most people imagine. In spring and summer, my uncle opens each hive every ten days or so to check that the queen is laying eggs and that the bees have enough space. He wears a white suit with a net over his face, but he says the bees are usually calm if he moves slowly and does not open the hive on a cloudy day. In winter, he hardly touches the hives at all; the bees stay inside in a tight ball to keep warm, and he simply makes sure they have enough honey left to eat.<br><br>The first harvest was a surprise. From three hives he collected almost forty kilograms of honey, far more than one family could use. He gave jars to the neighbours, who had at first been nervous about living under thousands of insects. Now several of them have planted flowers on their balconies, and one young couple downstairs has asked him to teach them.<br><br>My uncle says the honey is only part of the reward. \"The bees have made me notice things,\" he told me. \"Which trees are flowering, when it is going to rain. I never looked at the city that way before.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why honey from city bees tastes better than honey from the countryside","The dangers of keeping insects in a crowded apartment building","How a man started keeping bees in the city and what it brought him","A monthly beekeepers' club that meets in a public park"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Every paragraph follows Uncle Tuan's beekeeping: how he began, the work it involves, the harvest and what he learned. The club and the neighbours are details, and the passage never compares the taste of different honeys."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"How did Uncle Tuan get his first bees?","options":["A young couple in his building offered them as a present.","He bought them after a winter of reading about them.","He caught a wild colony living in a tree in the park.","An experienced beekeeper from his club gave them to him."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says an older member of the club gave him his first colony in April. He read and watched videos first, but the passage says he did not start by buying bees."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to paragraph 3, why does Uncle Tuan avoid opening the hives on cloudy days?","options":["The bees are more likely to be calm only in good weather.","The queen stops laying eggs when the sun is not out.","The honey becomes too thick to collect in cool weather.","He cannot see the eggs clearly without bright sunlight."],"correct":0,"explanation":"He says the bees are usually calm if he moves slowly and does not open the hive on a cloudy day, so cloudy weather makes them less calm. Nothing is said about the queen, the honey or visibility."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"harvest\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["visit","collection","winter","hive"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"The first harvest\" is followed by \"he collected almost forty kilograms of honey\", so a harvest here is the collecting of the honey. It is not a place, a season or a visit."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the neighbours?","options":["They refused to accept the jars of honey.","They have all started keeping bees on their own balconies.","Their feelings about the bees have changed since the first harvest.","They complained to the building manager about the hives."],"correct":2,"explanation":"They were \"at first nervous\", but now several plant flowers for the bees and one couple wants lessons, which shows their attitude has changed. Only one couple asked to learn, not everyone, and no complaint is mentioned."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Uncle Tuan checks the hives more often in winter than in summer.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he opens the hives every ten days in spring and summer but \"hardly touches\" them in winter, so the statement is the opposite of what the passage says."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Uncle Tuan sells his honey at the monthly club meeting.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says he gave jars to the neighbours, but it never mentions selling honey anywhere, so we cannot say whether this is true or false."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-12-2","level":"kc","title":"The Village Above the Clouds","topic":"a mountain village","words":338,"passage":"Sin Ho is a village of about forty houses that sits on a shelf of land 1,500 metres above the valley floor. On most mornings, the villagers wake up above a sea of white cloud, and the fields below cannot be seen until the sun has been up for two or three hours. Visitors often say it looks like an island floating in the sky.<br><br>Life here follows the seasons more closely than it does in the towns. In spring, families plant rice on narrow terraces that were cut into the mountainside by their great-grandparents. In the hot months, the children lead water buffalo up to the high grass while the adults repair the stone walls that hold the terraces in place. The harvest in October is the busiest time of the year, and school closes for two weeks so that everyone can help.<br><br>Until 2015, the only way to reach Sin Ho was a footpath that took four hours to climb. Then a narrow road was built, and motorbikes could finally reach the village. The road changed many things. Fresh vegetables and medicine now arrive weekly, and the village has a small clinic with a nurse who comes every Tuesday. But the road also made it easier to leave. Several young people moved down to the town to find work, and two houses on the edge of the village now stand empty.<br><br>The village leader, Mrs Mai, believes the answer is tourism, but only a little of it. Last year, three families opened their homes to guests, who sleep on the wooden floor, eat with the family and are woken at dawn to watch the clouds. \"We do not want a hotel,\" she says. \"We want people to see how we live, and then go home.\"<br><br>So far, her plan seems to be working. One of the young men who left has returned to guide walkers, and the money from guests has paid for new roofs on four houses. The clouds, of course, cost nothing at all.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The building of a road to a village in 2015","Why young people are leaving Vietnam's mountain villages","The best time of year for tourists to see the clouds at Sin Ho","A mountain village, how it lives and how a new road has changed it"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage describes the village, its seasons, the effects of the road and the leader's plan. The road, the young people and tourism are each only part of that picture."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the school close for two weeks in October?","options":["Because the road is blocked by cloud in the autumn","Because the children have to lead the buffalo to the high grass","Because everyone is needed for the rice harvest","Because the teacher leaves to work in the town"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the October harvest is the busiest time, and school closes \"so that everyone can help\". Leading the buffalo happens in the hot months, not October."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, the word \"it\" in \"made it easier to leave\" refers to","options":["the village","leaving","the road","the clinic"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This \"it\" is an empty subject standing for the action \"to leave\": the road made leaving easier. The road is the thing that did the making, not what \"it\" refers to."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does Mrs Mai most likely mean by \"only a little of it\"?","options":["She wants visitors, but not so many that the village changes.","She thinks tourism will bring in only a small amount of money.","She would prefer a small hotel rather than a large one.","She wants tourists to stay for only a short time each day."],"correct":0,"explanation":"She says \"We do not want a hotel. We want people to see how we live, and then go home,\" which shows she wants tourism kept small so village life stays the same. She rejects a hotel of any size."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"terraces\" in paragraph 2 refers to","options":["wooden houses built on the edge of the village","paths that lead up to the high grass","walls made of stone to keep animals out","flat steps of land for growing crops on a slope"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Families \"plant rice on narrow terraces that were cut into the mountainside\", and stone walls hold them in place, so terraces are flat pieces of land for farming on the slope. The walls are separate from the terraces."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Before 2015, it took about four hours on foot to reach Sin Ho.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that until 2015 the only way to the village was a footpath that took four hours to climb."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The guests who stay in the village pay more than they would in a town hotel.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says money from guests paid for new roofs, but it never compares the price with a hotel, so there is no information to decide."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-12-3","level":"kc","title":"The Bin Under the Sink","topic":"recycling at home","words":324,"passage":"For most of my life, our kitchen had one bin. Everything went into it: banana skins, plastic bottles, old newspapers and the foil from last night's dinner. Once a week my father carried the bag downstairs, and that was the end of the story. Then my younger sister came home from a school project and announced that our family was \"doing it all wrong\".<br><br>Her first idea was simple. She put three boxes under the sink and labelled them: paper, plastic and metal, and food. The food box went out every evening, because in our climate it starts to smell within a day. The other two were emptied only when they were full. Within a week we noticed something strange: the box for paper, plastic and metal filled up much faster than the one for food. We had never realised how much packaging we brought home from the supermarket.<br><br>Not everything went smoothly. My father kept dropping used tea bags into the plastic box, and I found out that greasy pizza boxes cannot be recycled with clean paper, no matter how carefully you fold them. My sister made a chart and stuck it on the fridge, with pictures for the things people got wrong most often. My grandmother, who cannot read English labels, found the pictures more useful than any of us.<br><br>The bigger change happened at the shops. Once you have to sort your rubbish, you start to see it before you buy it. My mother began choosing vegetables that were not wrapped in plastic, and we bought a large bottle of washing liquid instead of five small ones. Our weekly bag of real rubbish shrank to about half its old size.<br><br>My sister is now planning a compost box for the balcony, so that the food waste can feed her plants instead of going to the dump. My father says three boxes are enough for one apartment. I suspect she will win.","questions":[{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?","options":["To describe how one family began sorting its rubbish and what they learned","To persuade readers to build a compost box on their balcony","To complain about the amount of packaging supermarkets use","To explain the rules for recycling in the writer's city"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The writer tells the story of the family's change, from one bin to three boxes to shopping differently. Packaging and the compost box are parts of that story, not the aim of the whole passage."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why was the food box emptied every evening?","options":["Because the family produced more food waste than anything else","Because food waste quickly begins to smell in a hot climate","Because the compost box on the balcony needed feeding","Because it was the smallest of the three boxes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the food box went out every evening \"because in our climate it starts to smell within a day\". In fact the paper, plastic and metal box filled faster, and the compost box does not exist yet."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following did the family discover could NOT be recycled?","options":["Old newspapers","Used tea bags","Greasy pizza boxes","Foil from dinner"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer found out that greasy pizza boxes cannot be recycled with clean paper. Tea bags were put in the wrong box, but the passage does not say they cannot be recycled."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the grandmother find the chart especially useful?","options":["She was the person who made the most mistakes.","She wanted to teach the rules to the writer's father.","She had never used a recycling box before.","She could understand the pictures even though she could not read the labels."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage says she \"cannot read English labels\" and \"found the pictures more useful than any of us\", so the pictures helped her where words could not. The father, not the grandmother, is the one described making mistakes."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"shrank\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["became smaller","disappeared","was thrown away","grew heavier"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The bag \"shrank to about half its old size\", so it became smaller. It did not disappear completely."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The family now buys one large bottle of washing liquid instead of several small ones.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says they \"bought a large bottle of washing liquid instead of five small ones\"."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's father has agreed to the plan for a compost box.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The father says three boxes are enough for one apartment, which means he is against the compost box. The writer only guesses that the sister will win."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-12-4","level":"kc","title":"Fourteen Hours to the Coast","topic":"a long bus ride","words":335,"passage":"The ticket said the journey would take twelve hours. It took fourteen, and I would not have wanted it to be any shorter.<br><br>I was travelling alone for the first time, going to stay with my cousin in a town on the coast. My mother had wanted me to fly, but the bus was a third of the price, and I had promised to pay for the trip myself. So at six in the evening I climbed into a sleeper bus with three rows of narrow beds and a driver who did not smile once.<br><br>For the first hour I stared at my phone. Then the signal disappeared as we entered the hills, and I had no choice but to look out of the window. The road twisted between dark shapes of mountains, and every so often we passed a village where a single light was still on. At a rest stop around midnight, an old woman in the bed next to mine shared her rice cakes with me and asked why I was travelling alone. When I told her, she laughed and said she had done the same thing at fifteen, forty years earlier, and had never once regretted it.<br><br>I did not sleep well. The beds are made for people shorter than me, and the bus stopped several times to pick up passengers who seemed to appear from nowhere on the dark road. Somewhere around four in the morning, the driver put on loud music, perhaps to keep himself awake. Nobody complained.<br><br>Then, just after sunrise, the mountains ended. The road dropped down towards a flat green plain, and in the far distance I saw a thin silver line that could only be the sea. The old woman was awake too. \"Worth it?\" she asked. I nodded.<br><br>My cousin was waiting at the station with a motorbike and a bottle of water. \"You look terrible,\" he said. I told him I felt wonderful. Next time, I think, I will take the bus again.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which sentence best describes the passage?","options":["A comparison of the cost of travelling by bus and by plane","The story of a teenager's first long journey alone and how it felt","A description of a friendship between a girl and an old woman","A warning about the dangers of night buses on mountain roads"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer describes the whole journey, from choosing the bus to arriving, and ends by saying they would do it again. The price and the old woman are parts of the story, not its subject."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the writer choose the bus instead of the plane?","options":["The writer was paying and the bus was much cheaper.","There were no flights to the coastal town.","The writer wanted to meet other travellers.","The writer's mother preferred it."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the bus was a third of the price and the writer had promised to pay for the trip. The mother actually wanted the writer to fly."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What made the writer stop looking at the phone?","options":["The bus stopped at a rest stop.","The driver asked everyone to turn off their phones.","The old woman started a conversation.","The phone lost its signal in the hills."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the signal disappeared as they entered the hills, so the writer \"had no choice but to look out of the window\". The old woman spoke later, at the midnight stop."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer most likely mention that \"Nobody complained\" about the loud music?","options":["To criticise the passengers for being too polite","To show that the passengers were all asleep","To suggest the passengers understood the driver needed to stay awake","To show that the music was not really very loud"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer guesses the music was \"perhaps to keep himself awake\", and the fact that nobody complained suggests the passengers accepted this. The writer was awake and describes the music as loud."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"regretted\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["repeated","felt sorry about","been proud of","forgotten"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The old woman had \"never once regretted\" travelling alone at fifteen, meaning she was never sorry she did it. This fits her cheerful, encouraging attitude."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 5, what does \"it\" in \"Worth it?\" refer to?","options":["The long, uncomfortable journey","The ticket price","The view of the sea","The rice cakes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The old woman asks the question as the sea appears, meaning \"was the difficult journey worth it?\". The view is the reward, not the thing being paid for."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The bus arrived at the coast two hours later than the ticket said.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The ticket said twelve hours and the journey took fourteen, which is two hours longer."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-13-1","level":"kc","title":"Birds That Chose the Sea","topic":"penguins","words":315,"passage":"Ask a child to draw a penguin and you will probably get a black-and-white bird standing on ice. The picture is not wrong, but it is only part of the story. Of the eighteen kinds of penguin alive today, just a few spend their lives in Antarctica. Others live on the beaches of South Africa, the rocky islands of New Zealand, and even the Galápagos Islands, which sit right on the equator. What every penguin shares is not the cold, but the sea.<br><br>Penguins are birds, yet they cannot fly. Over millions of years, their wings became short and stiff, more like paddles than wings. On land this makes them slow and rather clumsy, which is why they seem so funny to us. Under water, however, they are as fast and graceful as any fish. A penguin's body is shaped like a torpedo, and it steers with its feet and tail. Some species can dive deeper than a hundred metres and hold their breath for many minutes.<br><br>Staying warm in cold water is a serious problem, and penguins solve it in several ways. Their feathers are small and packed tightly together, so that water cannot reach the skin. Under the feathers is a thick layer of fat. Emperor penguins, which live through the Antarctic winter, go one step further: thousands of birds stand pressed against one another in a huge group, and they take turns moving from the cold outside to the warm centre.<br><br>Sadly, many penguin populations are shrinking. Fishing boats take the small fish that penguins depend on, oil spills damage their feathers, and warmer seas push their food further away. Scientists who count penguins each year say that some colonies are half the size they were thirty years ago. The birds that once seemed so far from us are now telling us something about the health of the oceans we all share.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why penguins can only survive in Antarctica","How penguins are built for life in the sea, and the dangers they now face","The reason penguins lost the ability to fly","How scientists count penguin colonies every year"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Each paragraph is about penguins and the sea: where they live, how they swim, how they keep warm in the water and what is harming them. Antarctica, flying and counting are single details, and the first option contradicts paragraph 1."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to paragraph 1, what do all penguins have in common?","options":["They depend on the sea.","They are found on the equator.","They live in very cold places.","They live on small islands."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The last sentence of paragraph 1 says directly: 'What every penguin shares is not the cold, but the sea.' The islands and the equator apply only to some species."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'clumsy' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","options":["lazy","noisy","dangerous","awkward"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts penguins on land ('slow and rather clumsy', 'funny') with penguins in the water ('fast and graceful'). The opposite of graceful is awkward."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about emperor penguins?","options":["They cannot dive as deep as other species.","They are the only penguins with a layer of fat.","They face colder conditions than most other penguins.","They never leave the centre of the group."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Emperor penguins 'live through the Antarctic winter' and 'go one step further' than the fat and feathers all penguins have, which suggests their cold is more extreme. The passage says the birds take turns in the centre, so they do leave it."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, 'penguins solve it in several ways' — what does 'it' refer to?","options":["cold water","the problem of staying warm","a penguin's body","the thick layer of fat"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence begins 'Staying warm in cold water is a serious problem', and 'it' is that problem. The feathers and fat are the ways it is solved, not the thing being solved."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Some penguin colonies are now half the size they were three decades ago.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says scientists report that 'some colonies are half the size they were thirty years ago'. Thirty years is three decades."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Oil spills are the greatest danger that penguins face.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Oil spills are listed as one of three dangers, alongside fishing and warmer seas, but the passage never ranks them. There is no information about which is the greatest."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-13-2","level":"kc","title":"Thirty Minutes with a Broken Chain","topic":"a broken bicycle","words":336,"passage":"The chain came off halfway up the hill on Tran Phu Street, and then, with a sound like a dropped spoon, it snapped in two. I was already late for school. For a moment I stood there in the heat, holding a bicycle that had suddenly become nothing more than a heavy piece of metal.<br><br>The bike was not new. It had belonged to my older cousin, and before that to someone in his neighbourhood. The paint was scratched, the bell did not ring, and the back brake only worked if you pulled it with both hands. My mother had promised that I could have a new one when I started grade 10, so my first thought that morning was simple: leave it, walk to school, and wait for September.<br><br>Instead, I pushed it to the repair stand at the corner. The old man who ran it, Mr Bay, looked at the chain and then looked at me. 'I can fix this in ten minutes,' he said, 'or you can fix it in thirty, and then you'll never need me again.' I wanted to say that I was late, but something in the way he said it made me put my bag down.<br><br>He did not do the work for me. He showed me how to find the broken link, how to push the pin out with a small tool, and how to join the two ends again. My fingers were black with oil, and I got to school in the middle of second period with a note from nobody. But the chain held.<br><br>That was two years ago. I did get the new bicycle, and it is lighter and faster and the bell works. Yet on Sundays I still ride the old one to the market. When it breaks, and it does, I take out the tools that Mr Bay sold me for the price of a bowl of noodles. I have learned that a thing is only broken until somebody decides to understand it.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the story mainly about?","options":["A morning that taught the narrator the value of repairing things","The daily work of an old repairman named Mr Bay","Why the narrator was late for school one day","How the narrator finally received a new bicycle for grade 10"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The broken chain, Mr Bay's lesson and the final sentence about understanding broken things all point to one idea: learning to repair rather than replace. The new bicycle and the lateness are details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What was wrong with the bicycle before the chain broke?","options":["The front brake did not work at all.","The bell did not ring.","The chain had already been repaired once.","The paint was new but the tyres were old."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 lists scratched paint, a bell that did not ring and a weak back brake. It is the back brake, not the front, and nothing is said about the chain or the tyres."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Mr Bay offer the narrator a choice?","options":["He did not have the tool needed for the job.","He was too busy to repair the chain himself.","He wanted the narrator to be able to repair the bike alone in future.","He was not sure the narrator could pay him."],"correct":2,"explanation":"His words 'then you'll never need me again' show his aim was to make the narrator independent. He clearly had the tool, since he later sold the narrator a set."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase 'the chain held' in paragraph 4 means that the chain","options":["was carried by the narrator","was too tight to move","was kept by Mr Bay","stayed joined together"],"correct":3,"explanation":"'Held' here means it did not break again. The word 'But' contrasts the messy, late arrival with the one thing that went right: the repair worked."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention 'a note from nobody'?","options":["to show that the narrator had no written excuse for being late","to show that Mr Bay refused to write a note","to show that the teacher did not mind lateness","to show that the narrator forgot to bring the note"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A student who arrives late normally brings a note from a parent or doctor. 'A note from nobody' is a humorous way of saying the narrator arrived with no excuse at all."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The narrator still uses the old bicycle.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says 'on Sundays I still ride the old one to the market', even though the narrator now owns a new bicycle."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mr Bay gave the narrator a set of tools for free.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The tools were 'sold' to the narrator 'for the price of a bowl of noodles'. That is cheap, but it is not free."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-13-3","level":"kc","title":"The Library in My Grandmother's Head","topic":"a grandmother's stories","words":347,"passage":"My grandmother has never written anything longer than a shopping list, but she carries a whole library in her head. Every evening after dinner, when the dishes are done and the television has been switched off, she settles into the wooden chair by the window and begins. 'Did I ever tell you about the year the river came into the kitchen?' She has, many times. We listen anyway.<br><br>When I was small, I thought her stories were simply entertainment, like cartoons. There was the one about the buffalo that walked into the classroom, the one about my grandfather arriving for their first meeting on a borrowed bicycle with one pedal missing, and the one about the flood, which is the longest and the saddest. I knew them so well that I could correct her if she changed a detail.<br><br>It was a history project in grade 8 that made me see them differently. Our teacher asked us to interview an older relative about life in the past. I recorded my grandmother for an hour on my phone, expecting to hear the same tales. But this time I was listening for facts. How much did rice cost? How far away was the nearest doctor? How did people know the flood was coming? Her stories, it turned out, were full of answers. The buffalo story was really about a school with no walls. The bicycle story was about a time when a bicycle was the most valuable thing a family owned.<br><br>My grandmother is eighty-three now, and some days she asks me the same question twice. The doctor says this is normal at her age, but it frightens me a little. So I keep recording. I have more than twenty hours saved, and I have started writing them down, exactly as she tells them, with the pauses and the jokes.<br><br>She laughed when I told her what I was doing. 'Nobody wants old stories,' she said. I think she is wrong. A story is one of the few things that becomes more valuable the more it is given away.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A flood that once reached the family's kitchen","Why elderly people often repeat themselves","How the narrator completed a grade 8 history project","How the narrator came to see a grandmother's stories as valuable family history"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage moves from seeing the stories as 'entertainment' to discovering they were 'full of answers' about the past, and ends with the narrator saving them. The project, the flood and the repeated questions are details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What did the teacher ask the students to do for the history project?","options":["Find out how much rice used to cost","Write down every story their grandparents told","Interview an older family member about life in the past","Record a relative for at least twenty hours"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states the task: 'interview an older relative about life in the past'. The twenty hours and the writing down came later, by the narrator's own choice, and the price of rice was just one question asked."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the narrator suggest about the grandmother's memory?","options":["It is as strong as it has always been.","It is beginning to weaken.","It has already been completely lost.","The doctor believes it is unusual for her age."],"correct":1,"explanation":"She 'asks me the same question twice' on some days, which 'frightens' the narrator, and this is why the narrator 'keeps recording'. The doctor calls it normal, and she can still tell her stories, so it is not lost."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'tales' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["stories","questions","facts","jokes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The narrator expected 'to hear the same tales', meaning the stories already described in paragraph 2. The contrast in the next sentence is with 'facts', so 'facts' cannot be the meaning."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, 'made me see them differently' — what does 'them' refer to?","options":["the cartoons","the older relatives","the facts","the grandmother's stories"],"correct":3,"explanation":"'Them' points back to the stories described throughout paragraph 2. The rest of paragraph 3 explains how those same stories turned out to be full of facts."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer end with the sentence 'A story is one of the few things that becomes more valuable the more it is given away'?","options":["to suggest that the recordings should be sold","to warn that the stories will soon be forgotten","to disagree with the grandmother's view that nobody wants old stories","to explain why the grandmother laughed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence comes straight after 'I think she is wrong', so it gives the reason the narrator disagrees with 'Nobody wants old stories'. Nothing is said about selling the recordings."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The grandmother has written some of her stories down herself.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The first sentence says she 'has never written anything longer than a shopping list'. It is the narrator, not the grandmother, who has started writing the stories down."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-13-4","level":"kc","title":"Ninety Minutes in the Rain","topic":"a rainy football match","words":340,"passage":"The rain started during the warm-up and did not stop for the whole afternoon. By the time the referee blew his whistle for the final of the district schools' cup, the pitch behind Nguyen Du Secondary was less a football field than a shallow brown lake with white lines painted on it.<br><br>I was the goalkeeper, which on a dry day is the loneliest position on the pitch. In the rain it is the busiest. The ball would not roll; it stopped dead in the puddles or skidded off at strange angles. Twice in the first half, a shot that should have been easy to catch slipped through my gloves like a bar of soap. We were two goals down at half-time, and I could not look at my teammates.<br><br>Our coach, Mr Hung, did not shout. He handed out dry towels, which were wet within a minute, and said only one thing: 'Stop trying to play the game you practised. Play the game that is in front of you.' It sounded like a line from a film, but it turned out to be practical advice. In the second half we stopped passing along the ground, where the ball died, and started lifting it over the puddles. Our tallest defender was moved up front, because in that mud nobody could turn quickly and height mattered more than speed.<br><br>We scored in the sixtieth minute and again in the eighty-fifth. The second goal came from a corner that the wind carried straight into the net, and for a moment nobody was sure whether it counted. It did. The match ended two–two, and the cup was shared, because the organisers decided that nobody could take a penalty shoot-out seriously in that weather.<br><br>I have played in better matches since, and won some of them properly. But when people ask me about football, that is the afternoon I describe: the cold, the mud, the towels that were no use, and the strange discovery that a plan is only good until the weather changes.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A coach's half-time speech that came from a film","How a school team won the district cup in a penalty shoot-out","A rainy final in which a team learned to change its plan","Why goalkeepers dislike playing in wet weather"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The story builds to the coach's advice, the change of tactics in the second half and the final lesson that 'a plan is only good until the weather changes'. There was no shoot-out, and the speech and the goalkeeper's problems are details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why was the tallest defender moved forward?","options":["Because he had already scored in the first half","Because the wind was blowing towards the goal","Because he was the fastest player on the team","Because players could not turn quickly in the mud"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains: 'in that mud nobody could turn quickly and height mattered more than speed'. The wind is mentioned only for the second goal, and the team did not score in the first half."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the two first-half goals?","options":["The narrator feels partly responsible for them.","The referee should not have allowed them.","Both were scored from corners.","They were the result of the wet towels."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The narrator says shots that 'should have been easy to catch' slipped through and 'I could not look at my teammates', which shows a feeling of guilt. Nothing suggests the referee was wrong or that corners were involved."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'skidded' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","options":["stopped","slid","bounced high","floated"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The ball either 'stopped dead' or 'skidded off at strange angles', so skidding is the opposite of stopping: sliding quickly and uncontrollably across the wet surface."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, the short sentence 'It did.' means that","options":["the match ended","the wind stopped","the goal counted","the corner was taken again"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence before says 'nobody was sure whether it counted'. 'It did' answers that doubt: the goal was allowed, which is why the score became two–two."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The final was played on the narrator's own school pitch.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The pitch was 'behind Nguyen Du Secondary', but the passage never says which school the narrator attends. We cannot tell whether it was a home pitch."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The narrator's team lost the final.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The match 'ended two–two, and the cup was shared'. The team did not win outright, but it certainly did not lose."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-14-1","level":"kc","title":"The Room Next to the Canteen","topic":"the school library","words":323,"passage":"For most of its history, the library at Tran Phu Secondary School sat on the top floor, at the end of a long corridor, behind a heavy door that was usually closed. It was quiet, it was clean, and it was almost always empty. A survey in 2023 found that fewer than one student in ten had borrowed a book that year.<br><br>Last summer, the school tried something different. When the old canteen was rebuilt, the library was moved into the room next to it on the ground floor. The heavy door was replaced with a wide open entrance, and the librarian, Mr Khoa, took down the sign that said \"Silence\". \"A library does not have to be a temple,\" he explains. \"It has to be a place people want to walk into.\"<br><br>The new room is not large, but it is arranged so that it is easy to use. Comics and magazines sit on shelves near the entrance, where students can pick them up while they wait for lunch. Novels and study books are further inside. There are beanbags in one corner, and a long table where students may eat a snack while they read, as long as they wipe it afterwards. Borrowing is done on trust: a student simply writes a name and a title in a notebook by the door.<br><br>Some teachers were doubtful. They expected noise, mess and missing books. In fact, the number of books that have not come back is lower than it was before the move, and the noise, Mr Khoa says, is \"the good kind\". Borrowing has risen to four times last year's level, and the beanbags are so popular that the school has ordered more.<br><br>The biggest change, though, may be a small one. Students who once went to the library only when a teacher sent them now go because they walk past it every day, and something in the window catches their eye.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How moving and redesigning a school library changed the way students use it","The rules students must follow in the new school canteen","A survey that compared reading habits at several schools","Why the library at Tran Phu Secondary School was nearly closed"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Every paragraph is about the library: where it used to be, how it was moved and rearranged, and what happened afterwards. The survey and the canteen are details that support this, not the main topic."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to the passage, what did the 2023 survey show?","options":["That most students wanted the library moved downstairs","That fewer than ten per cent of students had borrowed a book that year","That students preferred comics to novels","That the library was open for fewer than ten hours a week"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the survey found that <b>fewer than one student in ten had borrowed a book that year</b>. One in ten is ten per cent. The other options are not mentioned as survey results."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Where in the new library would a student find a magazine?","options":["In the notebook kept by the door","On the long table in the middle of the room","Near the entrance, close to where students wait for lunch","Further inside, next to the study books"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says <b>comics and magazines sit on shelves near the entrance</b>. Novels and study books are the ones kept further inside."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, what does \"it\" in \"as long as they wipe it afterwards\" refer to?","options":["the notebook by the door","the snack","the corner with the beanbags","the long table"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence describes a <b>long table where students may eat a snack while they read</b>; the thing they must wipe afterwards is that table."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"doubtful\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["not sure the idea would work","surprised","excited","angry"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The teachers were doubtful because <b>they expected noise, mess and missing books</b> — in other words, they were not sure the plan would succeed. Nothing suggests they were angry or excited."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"More books have gone missing since the library moved to the ground floor.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the number of books that have not come back is <b>lower than it was before the move</b>, so the statement contradicts the passage."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mr Khoa worked as a librarian at another school before coming to Tran Phu.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage tells us what Mr Khoa did and said at Tran Phu, but says nothing about his earlier career, so there is no way to know."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-14-2","level":"kc","title":"Have You Seen Mochi?","topic":"a lost dog poster","words":321,"passage":"When Linh came home from school on a Tuesday afternoon, the gate was open and her dog, Mochi, was gone. He was a small brown dog with one white ear, and he had never left the yard before. Her mother had already walked around the block twice. Her little brother, Bao, was crying.<br><br>Linh's first idea was to post a photo online, and she did. But her grandmother, who does not use a phone, said something that made her stop. \"The people who will find him are the people who live on these streets,\" she said. \"Most of them are not looking at a screen. They are looking at the road.\"<br><br>So that evening, Linh and Bao made a poster. The first version was a mess. Bao wanted to write everything: Mochi's favourite food, the songs he liked, the way he barked at motorbikes. Linh crossed most of it out. \"Nobody will stop to read all that,\" she said. What was left was short. A large photo. The words LOST DOG in thick letters. His name, his colour, the white ear, the street where he lived, and a phone number. At the bottom, Bao insisted on adding one line: \"He is scared of thunder. Please be kind.\"<br><br>They printed twenty copies and put them on lampposts, on the gate of the market, and in the window of the shop where their mother bought rice. The shop owner promised to tell every customer.<br><br>For two days, nothing happened. Then, on Thursday night, the phone rang. A woman from three streets away had seen the poster in the shop window. A small brown dog with a white ear had been sleeping under her stairs since the storm on Tuesday, too frightened to come out.<br><br>Bao says the last line of the poster is what did it. Linh is not so sure, but she has kept one copy in her drawer, just in case.","questions":[{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","options":["To explain how to train a dog to stay in the yard","To tell the story of how a family found their lost dog by making a poster","To argue that posting online is useless when a pet is lost","To describe the storm that hit Linh's town on Tuesday"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the family from the moment Mochi disappears to the phone call that brings him back, and the poster is at the centre of the story. Linh did post online too, so the passage does not argue that online posts are useless."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the grandmother think a poster would work better than an online post?","options":["Because the neighbours were more likely to be looking at the street than at a screen","Because the family could not afford to pay for an online advertisement","Because the police had told her to put up posters","Because she did not know how to use the internet"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In paragraph 2 she says the people who will find Mochi are the ones who live on these streets, and <b>most of them are not looking at a screen</b>. She does not use a phone herself, but that is not the reason she gives."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"a mess\" in paragraph 3 means that the first poster was","options":["printed in the wrong colour","torn and dirty","too small to read","untidy and badly organised"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentences that follow explain the mess: Bao <b>wanted to write everything</b> and Linh had to cross most of it out. The problem was too much information, not damage or size."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why had Mochi most likely stayed under the woman's stairs for two days?","options":["Because he did not like Linh's family","Because the woman had locked him in","Because he was frightened by the storm","Because he had hurt his leg"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The poster says Mochi <b>is scared of thunder</b>, and paragraph 5 says he had been under the stairs <b>since the storm on Tuesday, too frightened to come out</b>. The passage does not mention an injury or the woman keeping him."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Where did the woman see the poster?","options":["On the gate of the market","In the window of a shop","On Linh's online post","On a lamppost near her house"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says she had <b>seen the poster in the shop window</b>. Lampposts and the market gate are other places the posters were put, but not where she saw it."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The family printed more than twenty copies of the poster.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says <b>they printed twenty copies</b> — exactly twenty, not more — so the statement is false."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The woman who found Mochi had a dog of her own.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says she lived three streets away and saw the poster, but nothing is said about whether she owned a dog."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-14-3","level":"kc","title":"The Third Jar","topic":"saving pocket money","words":323,"passage":"When Minh was twelve, he wanted a second-hand guitar that cost 1,400,000 dong. His parents gave him 20,000 dong every school day for snacks, so if he spent nothing at all, the guitar was fourteen weeks away. He was sure he could do it. He lasted four days.<br><br>The problem, he now says, was not that he was greedy. It was that he had no plan. Money that sat in his pocket got spent, because there was always a good reason: a friend's birthday, a hot afternoon, a new flavour of ice cream at the school gate.<br><br>His grandmother, who had run a small fruit stall for thirty years, listened to his complaint and gave him three old glass jars. On the first she wrote SPEND, on the second SAVE, and on the third she wrote nothing. Every day, Minh was to put half of his money in the first jar and half in the second, and use only the first for anything he wanted. The third jar, she said, was for money that arrived unexpectedly: a gift from an uncle, a prize, a note found in a coat pocket. That money was never to be touched.<br><br>Minh thought the rule about the third jar was strange, but he followed it. The SAVE jar grew slowly, at 50,000 dong a week. What surprised him was the third jar. Lucky money at Tet, a small reward for helping a neighbour move house, a 100,000-dong note from his father for a good report: by the end of the term, it held more than the SAVE jar did.<br><br>He bought the guitar in May, seventeen weeks after he started. It took longer than his first plan, but this time it actually happened.<br><br>Minh still has the jars, though they are now for bigger things. \"The trick,\" he says, \"is not to save more. It is to decide where the money goes before it reaches your pocket.\"","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a grandmother ran a fruit stall for thirty years","Why children should not receive lucky money at Tet","How a boy learned to save money using a system of three jars","Why second-hand guitars are cheaper than new ones"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The whole passage follows Minh from his failed first attempt to save, through the three-jar system, to buying the guitar. The fruit stall and Tet money are only details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to Minh, why did his first attempt to save money fail?","options":["Because he was too greedy to stop buying ice cream","Because his parents stopped giving him snack money","Because the guitar became more expensive","Because he had no plan and the money in his pocket got spent"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the problem <b>was not that he was greedy. It was that he had no plan</b>. Option D is the tempting half-truth the passage directly rejects."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What was the third jar for?","options":["Money that arrived unexpectedly, such as a gift or a prize","The half of his daily money he was allowed to spend","Money to pay his grandmother back","Money for a friend's birthday present"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that the third jar was <b>for money that arrived unexpectedly: a gift from an uncle, a prize, a note found in a coat pocket</b>. The daily money was split between the SPEND and SAVE jars."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, \"That money was never to be touched\" means the money","options":["could be used in an emergency","must not be spent","must be counted every day","should be given to someone else"],"correct":1,"explanation":"\"Touched\" here means used or spent. The grandmother's rule was that money in the third jar stayed there; the passage gives no exception for emergencies."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does Minh mean by his final comment?","options":["The best way to save is to ask your parents for more money","Saving is impossible if your pocket money is small","Deciding in advance where money will go matters more than trying to save a larger amount","Money should never be kept in a pocket because it can be lost"],"correct":2,"explanation":"He says the trick <b>is not to save more</b> but <b>to decide where the money goes before it reaches your pocket</b> — the plan comes first, the amount second."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Minh bought the guitar sooner than his original plan had predicted.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"His first plan said the guitar was <b>fourteen weeks away</b>, but he bought it <b>seventeen weeks after he started</b>. It took longer, not less time, so the statement is false."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Minh's grandmother had used the three-jar method herself when she ran her stall.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We are told she ran a fruit stall for thirty years and gave Minh the jars, but the passage never says whether she used the method herself."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-14-4","level":"kc","title":"Lane Six","topic":"a first swimming race","words":321,"passage":"The pool at the district sports centre looked much bigger on the morning of the race than it had during training. Perhaps it was the flags, or the rows of parents on the plastic seats, or the loudspeaker that kept calling out names. Whatever it was, Hoang, who was eleven and had been swimming for less than a year, suddenly felt very small.<br><br>His event was the 50-metre freestyle, and he had been given lane six, at the edge of the pool. His coach, Ms Yen, said this was good news. \"The fast swimmers are in the middle lanes,\" she said. \"Nobody expects anything from lane six. You can just swim.\" Hoang did not find this as comforting as she meant it to be.<br><br>Waiting behind the block was the worst part. He checked his goggles four times. He watched the boy in lane five, who was taller and wore a proper racing cap, shake out his arms like someone on television. When the whistle went, Hoang stepped up onto the block and felt his legs tremble.<br><br>Then the horn sounded, and there was no more time to worry. He hit the water badly, too flat, and swallowed a mouthful of it. For the first few strokes he could not see anyone else and thought he must be last. Halfway down the pool, though, he settled into the rhythm he had practised, and stopped thinking about lanes and caps and parents altogether.<br><br>He touched the wall fifth out of eight. His time, 41.2 seconds, was two seconds slower than his best in training. The boy in lane five won.<br><br>Ms Yen met him with a towel. \"Terrible dive,\" she said. \"Good race.\" Hoang understood the difference. The dive could be fixed in a week. What could not be taught was the moment, halfway down the pool, when he had forgotten to be afraid. He asked her when the next race was.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why the middle lanes of a pool are given to the fastest swimmers","A coach who was disappointed with her swimmer's result","How to perform a perfect racing dive","A boy's first swimming race and what he discovered about himself during it"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage follows Hoang from his nerves before the race to the moment he forgot to be afraid and asked about the next one. The lanes and the dive are details, and Ms Yen calls it a <b>good race</b>, so she is not disappointed."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Ms Yen say that lane six was good news?","options":["Because it was the closest lane to the parents' seats","Because the water was calmer at the edge of the pool","Because nobody would expect anything from a swimmer there, so Hoang could just swim","Because the boy in lane five was slow"],"correct":2,"explanation":"She explains in paragraph 2 that the fast swimmers are in the middle lanes and <b>nobody expects anything from lane six</b>. The boy in lane five, in fact, went on to win."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 2, what does \"this\" in \"said this was good news\" refer to?","options":["The loudspeaker calling out names","Hoang being given lane six","The 50-metre freestyle event","The fact that Hoang had trained for less than a year"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The previous sentence says <b>he had been given lane six, at the edge of the pool</b>; \"this\" points back to that lane assignment, which Ms Yen then explains is a good thing."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"comforting\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","options":["reassuring","surprising","confusing","funny"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ms Yen meant her words to make Hoang feel calmer and less worried — to reassure him — but he <b>did not find this as comforting as she meant it to be</b>."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does Hoang ask about the next race at the end of the passage?","options":["Because he thinks he can win if he gets a middle lane","Because he wants to beat the boy in lane five","Because Ms Yen told him he had to enter it","Because he had found a moment during the race when he stopped being afraid, and wants to feel it again"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The last paragraph says the thing that could not be taught was <b>the moment when he had forgotten to be afraid</b>, and the very next sentence is his question about the next race. Nothing in the passage mentions revenge on lane five or a middle lane."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Hoang swam faster in the race than he ever had in training.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says his time <b>was two seconds slower than his best in training</b>, so the statement contradicts the passage."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The boy in lane five had won races before this one.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We know he was taller, wore a proper racing cap, and won this race, but the passage says nothing about his earlier results."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-15-1","level":"kc","title":"One Night Without Streetlights","topic":"the night sky","words":323,"passage":"Every year on the last Saturday of October, the small coastal town of Cửa Đông does something that surprises visitors. At nine o'clock in the evening, the street lamps along the harbour go out, the cafés turn off their coloured signs, and families carry blankets down to the beach. For two hours, the only light comes from the sky.<br><br>The event, called Dark Sky Night, was started six years ago by Mr Hải, a retired ship's engineer. As a young sailor, he had learned to find his way by the stars, and he was sad that his grandchildren had never seen the Milky Way. \"They could name every planet from their textbook,\" he says, \"but they thought the sky above our town was empty.\" He asked the town council to switch off the lights for one evening. To his surprise, they agreed, on the condition that it did not cost anything.<br><br>The first year, about forty people came. Mr Hải borrowed a telescope from a secondary school and pointed out Saturn, whose rings made several children shout. Since then, the crowd has grown to over a thousand, and local teachers now prepare star maps that visitors can read with a red torch, which does not spoil night vision the way white light does.<br><br>Not everyone was happy at first. Some shop owners feared losing customers, and a few residents felt unsafe walking home in the dark. The council answered by keeping the lights on in the side streets and asking volunteers to guide people along the beach path.<br><br>For Mr Hải, the best moment comes each year just after the lamps go off. \"There is always a silence,\" he says, \"and then someone gasps. That is the sound of a person seeing the sky properly for the first time.\" He hopes that one day the town will use dimmer lamps all year round, so that the stars are not only a once-a-year event.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The dangers of walking home in the dark in a coastal town","Why children can name the planets but never look at the sky","How a retired sailor learned to navigate by the stars","A yearly event in which a town turns off its lights to see the stars"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Every paragraph is about Dark Sky Night: how it began, how it grew, the problems it caused and what it means to Mr Hải. His years as a sailor and the safety worries are details inside that story."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"On what condition did the town council agree to Mr Hải's request?","options":["That shop owners along the harbour supported the idea","That the event would attract visitors to the town","That the event would not cost the town any money","That Mr Hải would provide a telescope himself"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the council agreed <b>on the condition that it did not cost anything</b>. The telescope was borrowed, and the shop owners were actually against the idea at first."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why do visitors read the star maps with a red torch?","options":["Because white light is banned in the town on that evening","Because red light does not damage people's night vision","Because red torches are cheaper for the school to buy","So that the volunteers can see where the visitors are"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that a red torch <b>does not spoil night vision the way white light does</b>. Nothing is said about a ban, the price of torches or the volunteers."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about how the council dealt with the complaints?","options":["It found a compromise instead of stopping the event","It told the shop owners to close early on Dark Sky Night","It refused to listen to the residents who felt unsafe","It cancelled the event for one year while it found a solution"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The council kept the lights on in the side streets and provided volunteer guides, which answered the safety worries without ending the event. That is a compromise, not a cancellation or a refusal."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"gasps\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to:","options":["begins to cry quietly","shouts a person's name","laughs loudly","takes a sudden breath in surprise"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Mr Hải describes a silence followed by the sound of someone seeing the sky properly for the first time. A gasp is the short, sudden breath a person takes when amazed."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 2, \"they agreed\" refers to:","options":["the visitors to the town","the shop owners","the town council","Mr Hải's grandchildren"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence before says Mr Hải <b>asked the town council</b> to switch off the lights, so \"they\" is the council that agreed to his request."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mr Hải owned the telescope that was used on the first Dark Sky Night.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he <b>borrowed</b> the telescope from a secondary school, so the statement contradicts the passage."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-15-2","level":"kc","title":"A Museum Without Glass Cases","topic":"a class trip to a museum","words":321,"passage":"Last Friday, Class 9A2 at Nguyễn Trãi Secondary School spent the day at the Museum of Traditional Crafts on the edge of the city. Most of the students expected a quiet morning of walking past glass cases and reading small labels. What they found was quite different.<br><br>The museum opened two years ago in a former rice warehouse. Instead of keeping objects behind glass, it invites visitors to touch, lift and even use them. In the first room, a guide named Ms Lan handed each student a piece of wet clay and showed them how a village potter shapes a bowl on a turning wheel. \"Half of them made something that looked like a hat,\" she laughed, \"but they will remember how hard it is.\"<br><br>The trip had been planned by the class's history teacher, Mr Dũng, who wanted his students to understand a chapter about village trades that they had found dull in the textbook. He asked them to choose one craft during the visit and interview a museum worker about it. Later, each group would present what they had learned to the rest of the class.<br><br>For Phương, a student who admits she nearly stayed at home with a cold, the highlight was the weaving room. An elderly woman there had been making silk for fifty years, and she let Phương try the loom. \"I thought weaving was slow and boring,\" Phương said. \"After ten minutes my arms hurt and I had made about three centimetres of cloth. Now I understand why silk was so expensive.\"<br><br>The day was not perfect. The bus arrived forty minutes late, and the museum café ran out of food before the last group had eaten. Even so, when Mr Dũng asked whether the class would like to visit again, every hand went up. He plans to make the trip a yearly event, and the museum has offered a discount for future school groups.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why school trips are often badly organised","How silk was made in Vietnamese villages in the past","A class visit to a museum where visitors can handle the objects","The history of a rice warehouse that became a museum"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage follows Class 9A2 through the visit, what they did there and how they felt about it. The warehouse, the silk and the late bus are single details within that account."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Mr Dũng organise the trip?","options":["Because he wanted the class to practise interviewing people","Because the museum had offered his class a discount","Because his students had asked to go somewhere outside school","Because his students had found a textbook chapter boring"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he wanted the students to understand <b>a chapter about village trades that they had found dull</b>. The interviews were a task during the trip, and the discount was offered only after the visit."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does Ms Lan's comment about the \"hat\" suggest?","options":["Most of the students found the pottery difficult","The students were not listening to her instructions","She was disappointed with the class's behaviour","The students had been asked to make hats, not bowls"],"correct":0,"explanation":"She laughs that half of the bowls looked like hats and adds that the students <b>will remember how hard it is</b>. Her point is that shaping clay is harder than it looks, not that the class misbehaved."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"highlight\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to:","options":["the most difficult task","the best part","the final activity","the brightest room"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Phương goes on to describe the weaving room as the experience that changed her mind, so \"highlight\" means the most enjoyable or memorable part of the day."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention the late bus and the café running out of food?","options":["To prove that the trip had been planned too quickly","To criticise the museum for being poorly managed","To show that the day had problems but the students still enjoyed it","To explain why the students want to visit the museum again"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The paragraph begins \"The day was not perfect\" and continues with <b>Even so</b>, every hand went up. The problems are mentioned to make the students' enthusiasm more convincing."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Phương had visited the Museum of Traditional Crafts before this trip.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says Phương nearly stayed at home with a cold and describes her first try at the loom, but it never says whether she had been to the museum before."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The museum is in a building that was once used to store rice.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the museum opened <b>in a former rice warehouse</b>, which is a building where rice was stored."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-15-3","level":"kc","title":"Twelve Floors Up","topic":"growing vegetables","words":319,"passage":"When Bảo's family moved into a flat on the twelfth floor of a new building, his mother made one condition: she wanted to grow her own vegetables. Bảo's father pointed out that they no longer had a garden, only a narrow balcony that got sun for a few hours each morning. His mother simply replied that she would start small.<br><br>She began with three plastic boxes filled with soil from a market stall. In the first one she planted water spinach, which she said was almost impossible to kill. In the second she sowed lettuce, and in the third some chilli seeds she had saved from the kitchen. Bảo was given the job of watering them before school, and he expected to forget within a week.<br><br>To his surprise, the water spinach was ready to cut after three weeks. His mother stir-fried it with garlic, and the whole family agreed that it tasted sweeter than anything from the supermarket. The lettuce was a different story. Snails climbed up from the drain and ate most of it, and the leaves that survived turned bitter in the afternoon heat. \"Every garden teaches you something,\" his mother said, and moved the box into the shade.<br><br>By the end of the year, the balcony held eleven boxes, two hanging pots of mint and a tomato plant that had grown taller than Bảo. Neighbours on the floor below began asking for seedlings, and a woman on the ninth floor now trades her spring onions for Bảo's mother's chillies.<br><br>Bảo has noticed that the balcony has changed more than their meals. His mother, who used to spend her evenings on her phone, now goes outside to check the plants. Bảo himself has started reading about which vegetables can grow well together. He still forgets to water the boxes sometimes, but he no longer thinks of a garden as something that needs a piece of ground.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why vegetables from a balcony taste better than supermarket ones","How a family grew vegetables on a small balcony and what it changed","The problems of keeping snails away from a lettuce crop","A boy who learned to enjoy watering plants every morning"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the balcony garden from three boxes to eleven, and ends with how it changed the family's habits. The taste, the snails and the watering are details along the way."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the first three crops was mostly lost?","options":["The lettuce","The mint","The water spinach","The chillies"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says snails <b>ate most of</b> the lettuce and the rest turned bitter. The water spinach was a success, and the mint was only added later."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Where did the chilli seeds come from?","options":["From Bảo's school","From a market stall","From a neighbour on the ninth floor","From the family's kitchen"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the chilli seeds were ones his mother <b>had saved from the kitchen</b>. The market stall supplied the soil, not the seeds, and the neighbour trades spring onions."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the family's neighbours?","options":["They were experienced gardeners before the family arrived","They have larger balconies than Bảo's family","They became interested in growing vegetables themselves","They complained about water dripping from the balcony"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Neighbours ask for seedlings and one woman swaps her own spring onions for chillies, which shows they have started growing things too. Nothing suggests complaints, experience or bigger balconies."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"sowed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to:","options":["watered","planted seeds of","cut","bought"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence lists what went into each box: water spinach was planted, lettuce was sowed, chilli seeds went into the third. \"Sow\" means to put seeds in the soil."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, \"the box\" that was moved into the shade is:","options":["the box of lettuce","the box of soil from the market","the box of water spinach","the box of chilli plants"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentences before describe the lettuce being eaten by snails and turning bitter <b>in the afternoon heat</b>, so moving it into the shade is the lesson the garden taught."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Bảo's father was against the idea of growing vegetables in the flat.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"His father only <b>pointed out</b> that there was no garden and little sun. The passage does not say whether he opposed the idea or supported it."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-15-4","level":"kc","title":"The Man at Number Seventeen","topic":"a new neighbour","words":327,"passage":"When the house at number seventeen was finally sold last spring, everyone on our street was curious. It had stood empty for almost two years, and my younger brother Tí was sure a family with children would move in. Instead, a small van arrived one morning with an old man, a dog and about thirty boxes of books.<br><br>The man's name was Mr Tâm, and for the first month we hardly saw him. He did not come to the tea stall on the corner where the other neighbours gathered, and when my mother called good morning across the fence, he did not answer. Tí decided he was unfriendly. Mrs Hồng next door, who knows everything about everyone, said he had been a professor and probably thought he was too clever for us.<br><br>Everything changed on a rainy Tuesday in June. I was pushing my bicycle home with a broken chain when Mr Tâm's gate opened. He waved me in, turned the bicycle upside down on his porch and fixed the chain in five minutes without a word. Then he wrote something in a small notebook and held it up: \"I hear very little. Please face me when you speak.\"<br><br>I felt terrible. We had all been talking about him for weeks, and he had not been able to hear a single one of our greetings. That evening I told my mother, and the next day she went to his gate with a plate of bánh xèo and a sheet of paper. Since then, the notebook has become a habit on our street. Tí has learned to tap Mr Tâm on the shoulder before speaking, and Mrs Hồng, to everyone's surprise, has stopped guessing and started asking.<br><br>Mr Tâm was indeed a professor, of engineering, and he now repairs bicycles for half the children in the neighbourhood. He refuses to take money, but he accepts cakes, and he says that he has never lived on a friendlier street.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a street misjudged a new neighbour before learning the truth about him","A retired professor who opens a bicycle repair shop","The problems that people with hearing loss face every day","Why a house stood empty for almost two years"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The story moves from the neighbours' wrong ideas about Mr Tâm to the discovery that he could not hear them, and how the street changed as a result. The repairs and the empty house are only details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Mr Tâm not reply when the narrator's mother greeted him?","options":["He was too busy unpacking his boxes of books","He could not hear her","He did not want to be disturbed by children","He thought he was too clever to talk to his neighbours"],"correct":1,"explanation":"His note explains it: <b>I hear very little</b>. The idea that he felt too clever for the street was only Mrs Hồng's guess, which turned out to be wrong."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about Mrs Hồng?","options":["She also has difficulty hearing","She had known Mr Tâm before he moved to the street","She tends to make up stories about people she does not know","She works at the tea stall on the corner"],"correct":2,"explanation":"She \"knows everything about everyone\" and decided Mr Tâm was a proud professor without speaking to him. The ending, where she <b>stopped guessing and started asking</b>, confirms this habit."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"gathered\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to:","options":["sold tea","argued","waited for the bus","met together"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The tea stall is described as the place <b>where the other neighbours gathered</b>, that is, where they came together to talk."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer include the sentence \"He refuses to take money, but he accepts cakes\"?","options":["To show that Mr Tâm is generous and has become part of the community","To suggest that Mr Tâm does not trust his neighbours","To show that Mr Tâm is poor and needs food","To explain why the children on the street like him"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Fixing bicycles for free and taking cakes instead shows a friendly exchange between Mr Tâm and the neighbours, which fits the final words about the friendliest street he has lived on."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mr Tâm had worked as a professor of engineering.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The last paragraph confirms that he <b>was indeed a professor, of engineering</b>, so Mrs Hồng was right about his job even if wrong about his character."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The house at number seventeen had been empty for more than three years.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says it had stood empty for <b>almost two years</b>, so the statement contradicts the passage."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-16-1","level":"kc","title":"The Well at the End of the Lane","topic":"a village well","words":283,"passage":"In the village of Phu An, where my grandmother grew up, there is a stone well at the end of a narrow lane. Today most houses have taps, and the well is used mainly by the two oldest families, who say the water tastes sweeter than anything that comes through a pipe. Fifty years ago, however, the well was the busiest place in the village.<br><br>My grandmother remembers going there twice a day with a wooden pole across her shoulders and a metal bucket hanging from each end. In the dry season the water level dropped so low that children had to lean right over the edge to fill their buckets, and mothers stood behind them holding their shirts. \"We never fell in,\" she laughs, \"but not because we were careful. It was because someone was always watching.\"<br><br>The well was also where news travelled. Women waiting their turn talked about weddings, prices at the market and which family had a new baby. If a stranger arrived in Phu An, the whole village knew before he had finished his first bucket. There was no telephone in the village until 1985, but my grandmother says nobody missed it.<br><br>Two years ago, the village council suggested filling in the well because the stone wall around it was cracked and a child might get hurt. To everyone's surprise, the strongest protest came not from the old people but from the teenagers. They collected money to repair the wall, painted a wooden cover for it, and now hold a small festival beside it every spring. The water is still cold and clear. My grandmother says that a well is only dangerous when people forget it is there.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A spring festival organised by the village teenagers","How the village of Phu An finally got running water","A village well and the part it has played in village life","The dangers that old wells present to young children"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Every paragraph returns to the well: who uses it now, what it was like fifty years ago, how news spread there and how it was saved. Running water, danger and the festival are details, not the main topic."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did mothers hold their children's shirts at the well?","options":["Because the stone wall around the well had cracked","Because the children were carrying two buckets at the same time","Because the children were pushing each other to get a turn","Because the water was so low that children had to lean over the edge"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that in the dry season <b>the water level dropped so low that children had to lean right over the edge</b>. The cracked wall belongs to a different time, two years ago, in paragraph 4."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the grandmother mean when she says that nobody missed the telephone?","options":["News already travelled fast enough through talk at the well","The villagers did not like speaking to strangers","The villagers could not afford to pay for telephone calls","The telephone arrived in the village earlier than people expected"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence comes at the end of the paragraph about the well being <b>where news travelled</b>, so the point is that the well already did the telephone's job. Money and strangers are not the reason given."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"protest\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","options":["celebration","objection","suggestion","payment"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The council wanted to fill in the well and the teenagers were against the plan, so the <b>protest</b> is their objection. The celebration and the money came later."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In the last sentence, what does \"it\" refer to?","options":["the wall","the festival","the well","the water"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence says <b>a well is only dangerous when people forget it is there</b>, so \"it\" refers back to the well in the same sentence."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The two oldest families in Phu An still take water from the well.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the well <b>is used mainly by the two oldest families</b>, who prefer its taste, so the statement agrees with the passage."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The well is more than a hundred years old.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage describes the well fifty years ago and today, but it never says when the well was built, so its age is not given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-16-2","level":"kc","title":"Thirty-Two Pieces and a Quiet Room","topic":"chess club","words":319,"passage":"When Minh joined the chess club at Nguyen Du Secondary School, he expected to spend his Thursday afternoons in silence. He was partly right. Room 204 is the quietest place in the building between four and five o'clock, and the club's only rule, written on a card by the door, is \"Think before you touch.\" What he did not expect was how much he would learn about losing.<br><br>The club was started three years ago by Mr Hai, a physics teacher who had never played in a tournament in his life. He simply noticed that a few students were playing chess on their phones during break and thought they might enjoy a real board. Twelve students came to the first meeting. There are now forty-one, and the school has had to buy a second cupboard for the boards and clocks.<br><br>Beginners are paired with stronger players for their first month, and the stronger player is not allowed to win quickly. Instead, after every game they must point to the exact move where the beginner went wrong and explain why. \"It is easy to beat someone,\" Mr Hai tells the older members. \"It is much harder to show them why they lost.\" Several members admit that teaching a beginner has improved their own game more than any book.<br><br>The club's proudest moment came last April, when a team of four travelled to the district championship. They did not win; in fact, they finished ninth out of fourteen. But Lan, a grade 8 student who had learned the moves only six months earlier, drew her game against the top player from the champion school. Her opponent shook her hand and asked which club she belonged to.<br><br>Minh now plays every Thursday and has lost more games than he can count. He says that each defeat comes with a lesson, and that no lesson has ever cost him more than an hour.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why Mr Hai stopped teaching physics to run a club","The dangers of playing chess on a mobile phone","How a school team won the district chess championship","A school chess club and what its members gain from it"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage describes how the club began, how it teaches beginners, its trip to the championship and what Minh has learned. The team did not win, and Mr Hai still teaches physics."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Mr Hai start the chess club?","options":["Twelve students came to him and asked for a club","He had been a tournament player when he was younger","He noticed students playing chess on their phones at break","The school asked every teacher to run an after-school activity"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he <b>noticed that a few students were playing chess on their phones during break</b>. He had never played in a tournament, and the twelve students came after the club was announced, not before."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What must a stronger player do when paired with a beginner?","options":["Finish each game as quickly as possible","Explain after each game where the beginner's key mistake was","Lend the beginner books about chess","Allow the beginner to win the first few games"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the stronger player must <b>point to the exact move where the beginner went wrong and explain why</b>. They are not allowed to win quickly, but nothing says they must let the beginner win."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer suggest about Lan's game at the championship?","options":["It impressed even the player she was up against","It showed that she had been playing chess for years","It was the only game her team did not lose","It won the championship for her school"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Her opponent, the top player from the champion school, <b>shook her hand and asked which club she belonged to</b>, which shows he was impressed. Lan had learned the moves only six months earlier, and the team finished ninth."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"drew\" in paragraph 4 means that Lan","options":["refused to continue playing","lost the game by a very small margin","made a picture of the game afterwards","finished the game with neither player winning"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In games, to <b>draw</b> means to end with no winner. The context makes clear it was a good result against a much stronger player, so it was not a loss."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer end with the sentence about the cost of a lesson?","options":["To remind readers what time the club meets","To complain that the club charges its members too much","To show that Minh sees losing a game as a cheap way to learn","To explain that Minh is planning to leave the club"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The \"cost\" is time, not money: each lost game takes at most an hour and brings a lesson. This links back to the first paragraph, where Minh learns <b>about losing</b>."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Mr Hai had played in chess tournaments before he started the club.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes him as a teacher <b>who had never played in a tournament in his life</b>, so the statement contradicts the passage."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-16-3","level":"kc","title":"Five Days at Bai Xep","topic":"a seaside holiday","words":319,"passage":"Last summer my family spent five days at Bai Xep, a small fishing village on the central coast. My father chose it because it was cheap and because there was no hotel with a swimming pool. \"If you want a pool,\" he said, \"we can stay at home and visit the one near the market.\" My sister and I were not impressed, and we said so all the way there.<br><br>The bus dropped us at the top of a hill, and we carried our bags down a sandy path to a guesthouse with six rooms and one very old dog. There was no air conditioning, only a fan that turned slowly and made a noise like a sleepy bee. On the first night I could not sleep at all. On the second night I slept better than I had for months.<br><br>Every morning at six, the fishing boats came back. My sister and I stood on the beach with the village children and watched the men pull the boats onto the sand. One of them, a man named Tam, let us help sort the fish. Small ones went into blue baskets, big ones into red, and anything with too many spines went back into the sea. We did this every day, and on the fourth morning Tam paid us with a squid each, which the guesthouse owner cooked for our lunch.<br><br>In the afternoons the beach belonged to us. There were no umbrellas to rent and nobody selling ice cream, so we built things out of driftwood instead. My father read the same book for five days and never finished it. My mother, who normally checks her phone every ten minutes, forgot where she had put it.<br><br>On the last day, my sister asked whether we could come back next year. My father looked at me, and I pretended not to hear the question. But I had already decided.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a family's holiday in a simple village turned out better than expected","The daily work of the fishermen at Bai Xep","Why swimming in the sea is healthier than swimming in a pool","A comparison of different hotels on the central coast"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The children start the holiday unhappy and end it wanting to return; the fishing, the guesthouse and the beach are the stages of that change. The fishermen are only one part of the story."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the writer's father choose Bai Xep?","options":["Because it had a well-known hotel by the sea","Because it was inexpensive and had no hotel with a pool","Because his children had asked to go there","Because his friend Tam lived in the village"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he chose it <b>because it was cheap and because there was no hotel with a swimming pool</b>. The children complained about the choice, and Tam is a fisherman they met there."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the last sentence, \"But I had already decided\", suggest?","options":["The writer was annoyed by his sister's question","The writer did not want to return to Bai Xep","The writer also wanted to come back the next year","The writer had chosen a different place for the next holiday"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer pretends not to hear but has <b>already decided</b>; after a paragraph about how much the family enjoyed the beach, the natural reading is that he agrees with his sister without wanting to admit it."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"not impressed\" in paragraph 1 means the children were","options":["not surprised by the plan","not frightened by the journey","not tired after the journey","not pleased with the plan"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To be <b>impressed</b> is to admire or approve of something. The children did not think the plan was a good one, and <b>said so all the way there</b>."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, what does \"this\" in \"We did this every day\" refer to?","options":["sorting the fish into baskets","standing on the beach with the village children","eating squid for lunch","watching the boats arrive"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentences just before describe sorting fish into blue and red baskets and throwing some back, and Tam <b>paid</b> them for it, so \"this\" is the sorting work."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Every room in the guesthouse had air conditioning.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says there was <b>no air conditioning, only a fan</b>, so the statement contradicts the passage."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's mother uses her phone for her job.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says she normally checks her phone every ten minutes, but it never says why or whether it is connected with work, so this is not given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-16-4","level":"kc","title":"The Twenty-Minute Rule","topic":"homework habits","words":311,"passage":"For most of grade 8, Ngoc did her homework the same way every evening. She sat at the kitchen table with her phone beside her, opened all her books at once, and started with whichever subject was on top. By ten o'clock she had usually finished, but she often could not remember what she had actually written. Her marks were fine. Her sleep was not.<br><br>In September her form teacher, Mrs Lien, ran a short session on study habits. She did not tell the class to work harder. Instead, she asked them to keep a diary for one week and write down, honestly, how many minutes of each homework hour they spent actually working. Ngoc's diary was embarrassing. In one sixty-minute maths session she had counted twenty-two minutes of maths, fourteen minutes of messages, and the rest lost to getting drinks, looking for a pen and staring out of the window.<br><br>Mrs Lien's suggestion was simple. Work for twenty minutes with the phone in another room, then take a five-minute break, then start again. Do one subject at a time, and put the others out of sight. Ngoc thought it sounded childish. She tried it anyway, mainly because her friend Phuong had promised to try it too and they wanted to compare results.<br><br>The first week was hard. Twenty minutes felt very long when there was nothing to check. But by the third week, Ngoc noticed that she was finishing an hour earlier than before and remembering more of what she had done. The strange part, she says, was that the breaks mattered as much as the work. Knowing a break was coming made it easier not to reach for the phone.<br><br>Ngoc still breaks the rule on some evenings. But she now knows exactly how much each broken rule costs her, and she says that knowing is half the habit.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why Ngoc's marks went down during grade 8","How keeping a diary and changing her routine improved Ngoc's homework","Mrs Lien's opinion about mobile phones in schools","A competition between Ngoc and her friend Phuong"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage follows Ngoc from her old routine, through the diary and the twenty-minute rule, to the result. Her marks were <b>fine</b> all along, and Phuong is mentioned only once."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What did Mrs Lien ask the class to do first?","options":["Record how many minutes of each hour they really spent working","Work for twenty minutes without stopping","Do their homework at school instead of at home","Switch off their phones while doing homework"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she asked them to <b>keep a diary for one week</b> and write down their working minutes honestly. The twenty-minute rule came afterwards, in paragraph 3."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Ngoc decide to try Mrs Lien's method?","options":["Because her parents had taken her phone away","Because Mrs Lien insisted that everyone must try it","Because her marks had started to fall","Because her friend had agreed to try it as well"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she tried it <b>mainly because her friend Phuong had promised to try it too</b> so they could compare results. Her marks were fine, and there is no mention of her parents."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about Ngoc's homework habits before September?","options":["She did her homework in her bedroom","She was the weakest student in her class","She spent less than half of her homework time actually working","She usually failed to finish her homework"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The diary shows <b>twenty-two minutes</b> of real work in a sixty-minute session, which is less than half. She usually finished by ten, worked at the kitchen table, and her marks were fine."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"childish\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","options":["too difficult for a student","suitable only for young children","too expensive to try","dangerous for her health"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ngoc thought a rule about twenty-minute blocks and breaks was the sort of thing you would tell a small child. It has nothing to do with difficulty or cost."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention that Ngoc still breaks the rule on some evenings?","options":["To show that the change is realistic rather than perfect","To criticise Mrs Lien's advice","To explain why Ngoc's marks dropped","To show that the method did not really work"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence is followed by <b>but she now knows exactly how much each broken rule costs her</b>, so the point is that the real gain is awareness, not perfection. Nothing says her marks dropped."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Phuong found the method more useful than Ngoc did.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says the two friends wanted to compare results, but it never reports Phuong's results, so this is not given."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-17-1","level":"kc","title":"The Cake That Slid Off the Plate","topic":"a birthday cake disaster","words":300,"passage":"Last month my brother Nam turned ten, and I decided to bake his birthday cake myself. I had watched my mother make sponge cakes dozens of times, so I was sure it would be easy. I found a recipe online, bought the ingredients on Saturday morning, and told everyone to stay out of the kitchen until four o'clock.<br><br>The first problem appeared almost at once. The recipe asked for three eggs, but we only had two, and the shop on our street was closed for lunch. I decided that two would be enough. Then I could not find the small measuring cup, so I guessed the amount of flour. The mixture looked a little thin, but I put it in the oven anyway and set the timer for forty minutes.<br><br>While the cake was baking, I started on the cream. This went better, and I was quite proud of the pale pink colour I had made with a few drops of strawberry juice. At that moment, however, I smelled something burning. I opened the oven door and found that the cake had risen on one side only, like a small hill, and the top was dark brown.<br><br>I cut off the burnt part, covered everything with cream, and hoped nobody would notice. Unfortunately, the cream was too soft, and by the time the guests arrived, it had slid down the sides and onto the tablecloth. Nam's friends laughed for a long time.<br><br>Nam did not mind at all. He said it was the funniest cake he had ever seen, and he ate two pieces. My mother took a photo, which is now on the fridge. Next year I will follow the recipe exactly, but I have a feeling my family will remember this cake far longer than a perfect one.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why Nam's friends laughed at his birthday party","The writer's attempt to bake a birthday cake, which went badly wrong","How the writer's mother makes birthday cakes for the family","The correct way to bake a sponge cake at home"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Every paragraph follows the writer's own baking attempt from confident start to sliding cream. The laughing friends and the mother's cakes are single details, not the subject."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the writer use only two eggs?","options":["There were only two at home and the shop was closed","The measuring cup could not be found","The writer thought three eggs would make the mixture too thin","The recipe said that two eggs were enough"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: the recipe asked for three, but 'we only had two, and the shop on our street was closed for lunch'. The missing measuring cup was a separate problem about flour."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the writer before the baking began?","options":["She was nervous and asked her mother for help","She had already practised the recipe once","She had baked several cakes on her own before","She expected the job to be easy because she had watched her mother"],"correct":3,"explanation":"She had only <b>watched</b> her mother 'dozens of times' and 'was sure it would be easy' — confidence came from watching, not from her own experience."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"thin\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to:","options":["narrow","slim","watery","pale"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A cake mixture that is 'thin' is too liquid, not thick enough. 'Narrow' and 'slim' describe shapes and people, not a batter."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, \"This went better\" refers to:","options":["baking the cake","making the cream","setting the timer","finding the strawberry juice"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence before says 'I started on the cream', so 'This' is the cream-making, which is contrasted with the cake that was burning."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The guests arrived before the cream slid off the cake.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4: 'by the time the guests arrived, it had slid down the sides' — the past perfect shows the sliding happened first."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Nam's friends brought him presents.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says the friends laughed at the cake, but nothing at all is said about presents."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-17-2","level":"kc","title":"A Dragon Over the Rice Fields","topic":"a kite festival","words":311,"passage":"Every year, at the end of the dry season, the village where my grandparents live holds a kite festival on the wide field behind the pagoda. This year my cousin Linh and I were finally old enough to enter the children's competition, so we spent two weekends building our kite.<br><br>We chose a simple diamond shape because Grandpa said beginners should not try anything fancy. He showed us how to split bamboo into thin strips and bend them over a candle flame until they curved without breaking. The frame took a whole afternoon. For the covering we used strong paper that Linh painted with a red and yellow dragon. The most difficult part was attaching the flute. Many kites in our village carry a small bamboo flute that sings when the wind passes through it, and each family has its own sound.<br><br>On the day of the festival, the field was full of people by seven in the morning. There were kites shaped like fish, birds and butterflies, and one enormous kite that needed four men to hold it. The judges did not only look at how high a kite could fly. They also gave points for how steady it was, how beautiful it looked and, for flute kites, how clear the sound was.<br><br>Our first launch failed. The wind dropped just as Linh let go, and the kite fell into the grass. Grandpa told us to wait and watch the flags on the pagoda. When they began to move again, we ran, and this time the dragon climbed quickly and stayed up for the whole ten minutes.<br><br>We did not win. The first prize went to a girl from the next village whose kite carried three flutes that played together like a tiny band. But walking home with Grandpa, holding our slightly torn dragon, I felt as if we had.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Two cousins building a kite and entering it in a village festival","How judges decide the winner of a kite competition","Why a girl from the next village won the first prize","The history of flute kites in Vietnamese villages"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage follows Linh and the writer from building the kite to flying it at the festival. The judging rules and the winner are details from single paragraphs."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What did Grandpa show the children how to do?","options":["Attach a bamboo flute to the frame","Bend strips of bamboo over a flame","Hold a very large kite in a strong wind","Paint a dragon on strong paper"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2: he 'showed us how to split bamboo into thin strips and bend them over a candle flame'. Linh did the painting, and the flute was simply 'the most difficult part'."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"According to paragraph 3, what did the judges consider?","options":["How quickly a kite could be launched","Only how high each kite could fly","Height, steadiness, beauty and, for some kites, sound","How many people were needed to hold the kite"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The judges 'did not only look at how high a kite could fly' but gave points for steadiness, beauty and, for flute kites, the sound. Option A is exactly what the passage denies."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"steady\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to:","options":["quiet","fast","bright","stable"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A 'steady' kite stays in one position without shaking or dropping — it is stable in the air."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, \"they\" in \"When they began to move again\" refers to:","options":["the flags on the pagoda","Linh and the writer","the other kites","the judges"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Grandpa told them to 'watch the flags on the pagoda'; the flags moving again showed that the wind had returned."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer say \"I felt as if we had\" at the end?","options":["The judges later changed their decision","The writer was proud of what they had achieved, even without a prize","Grandpa had promised to buy them a prize","The writer believed the girl's kite had cheated"],"correct":1,"explanation":"'We did not win' is followed by 'I felt as if we had' — the feeling of winning came from the successful flight and the walk home with Grandpa, not from any prize."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Linh and the writer had taken part in the competition in earlier years.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says they were 'finally old enough to enter' this year, so this was their first time."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-17-3","level":"kc","title":"Twenty Minutes on Foot","topic":"walking to school","words":306,"passage":"When our family moved to a smaller town last year, my parents made a decision I did not like at all. Instead of driving me to school, as they had done in the city, they told me I would walk. The school was only two kilometres away, and Dad said that was a perfectly reasonable distance for a fourteen-year-old.<br><br>For the first week I complained every morning. It was hot, my bag was heavy, and I arrived with my shirt sticking to my back. My mother's answer was to give me a lighter bag and a bottle of water, and to wake me ten minutes earlier so that I did not have to rush.<br><br>Slowly, the walk stopped feeling like a punishment. I found a route through a small park where an old man practised tai chi every morning; after a month, he began to nod to me as I passed. I noticed that the bakery on Tran Phu Street opened at half past six and that the bread smelled best at twenty to seven. I also discovered that two girls from my class lived on the same road, and we started walking together. Now we test each other on vocabulary on the way to school and talk about everything else on the way home.<br><br>There were practical changes too. I sleep better, and my PE teacher says I run faster than I did in September. The money my parents have saved on petrol goes into a jar, and at the end of the year we are going to spend it on a trip to the beach.<br><br>I still take the bus when it rains heavily, and I would not pretend that every morning is pleasant. But when my aunt visited last month and offered to drive me, I surprised everyone, including myself, by saying no.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why the writer's family moved from the city to a smaller town","The dangers of walking to school in hot weather","How the writer's family saved money for a beach holiday","How the writer's feelings about walking to school changed over time"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage moves from 'I complained every morning' to saying no to a lift — the change of attitude is the thread. The move and the holiday money are background details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"How did the writer's mother respond to the complaints?","options":["She bought the writer a bus ticket for the whole year","She agreed to drive the writer on hot days","She gave the writer a lighter bag, water and an earlier wake-up time","She found two classmates to walk with the writer"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 lists exactly these three things. The classmates were discovered by the writer, and the bus is only used in heavy rain."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"rush\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to:","options":["wait","hurry","wake","worry"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Being woken ten minutes earlier means there is enough time, so the writer does not need to 'rush' — to move in a hurry."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 1, \"that\" in \"Dad said that was a perfectly reasonable distance\" refers to:","options":["two kilometres","driving to school","being fourteen years old","the smaller town"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The previous clause says the school 'was only two kilometres away', and 'that' picks up this distance."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention the PE teacher in paragraph 4?","options":["To explain why the writer sleeps better","To complain that PE lessons have become harder","To show that the writer now wants to become an athlete","To give evidence of a physical benefit of walking"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The paragraph is about 'practical changes'; the teacher's comment that the writer runs faster is proof of one such change."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer walks to school in every kind of weather.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5: 'I still take the bus when it rains heavily', so not every day is a walking day."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's aunt lives in the same town as the family.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We only know the aunt 'visited last month' and offered a lift; where she lives is never mentioned."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-17-4","level":"kc","title":"A Rabbit Called Banh Bao","topic":"a pet rabbit","words":315,"passage":"My sister An had wanted a pet for years, but our flat is small and our parents always said no to a dog. Then, last spring, a neighbour who was moving abroad asked if we could take her rabbit. Mum agreed on one condition: An had to look after it herself, and if she stopped, the rabbit would go to another family.<br><br>The rabbit was white with grey ears, round and soft, so An named him Banh Bao after the steamed bun he looked like. We had imagined that a rabbit would simply sit quietly in a cage, but Banh Bao had other ideas. Within a week he had learned to push the cage door open with his nose. We would come home to find him under the sofa or, once, sitting proudly in the middle of the dining table.<br><br>An read everything she could find about rabbits. She learned that they should not live on carrots alone, as cartoons suggest, but need mainly hay and fresh green leaves. She learned that rabbits hate being lifted, so she taught herself to sit on the floor and let him come to her. She also found out that they chew almost anything, which explained the marks on the leg of Dad's chair and the hole in my phone charger.<br><br>The biggest change, though, was in An herself. She used to leave her homework until the last minute, but a pet that needs feeding at the same time every day forced her to make a timetable, and the habit spread to her schoolwork. Her teacher noticed the difference before our parents did.<br><br>Banh Bao is now a full member of the family. He sleeps at the end of An's bed, and he thumps his back foot loudly whenever the doorbell rings. Mum, who was the least keen at the start, is the one who talks to him most.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The damage a rabbit can do to furniture in a small flat","The correct diet for a pet rabbit","How a family took in a rabbit and what changed as a result","Why the writer's parents refused to buy a dog"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage covers how Banh Bao arrived, how An learned to care for him, and how An and the family changed. Diet, the dog and the chewed furniture are each only part of one paragraph."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What condition did Mum set before accepting the rabbit?","options":["An had to read a book about rabbits first","An had to keep the rabbit in its cage at all times","The neighbour had to take the rabbit back after one year","An had to care for the rabbit herself or it would be given away"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 1: 'An had to look after it herself, and if she stopped, the rabbit would go to another family'. The reading was An's own idea later."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does paragraph 2 suggest about Banh Bao?","options":["He was cleverer and more active than the family expected","He preferred to stay quietly in his cage","He was unwell when he arrived","He was frightened of the family at first"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The family 'had imagined' a quiet rabbit in a cage, but within a week he could open the door with his nose and was found on the dining table — he surprised them."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"keen\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to:","options":["careful","enthusiastic","worried","quiet"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Mum was 'the least keen at the start' but now talks to him most — the contrast shows 'keen' means enthusiastic or eager."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention the phone charger in paragraph 3?","options":["To show that the writer dislikes the rabbit","To complain that An does not control her pet","To give an example of the rabbit chewing things","To explain why the family needed a new cage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"It follows 'they chew almost anything, which explained…' — the chair leg and the charger are two examples of that chewing."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The rabbit was given to the family by a relative.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the rabbit came from 'a neighbour who was moving abroad', not a relative."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"An now gets higher marks at school.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says An's timetable habit spread to her schoolwork and her teacher noticed a difference, but nothing is said about her marks."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-18-1","level":"kc","title":"The Ice Cube Race","topic":"a science experiment at home","words":331,"passage":"Last month my science teacher set an unusual piece of homework. Instead of answering questions from the textbook, we had to design an experiment that could be done at home with things from the kitchen, and then write down exactly what happened. My younger brother Bao, who is nine, decided at once that he was going to help me, whether I wanted him to or not.<br><br>We chose a simple question: which material keeps an ice cube frozen for the longest time? I took six ice cubes of the same size out of the freezer and placed each one in a small bowl. One was wrapped in aluminium foil, one in a cotton sock, one in a plastic bag, one in newspaper, one in a woollen glove, and the last was left uncovered so that we had something to compare the others with. Bao wrote the starting time on a sheet of paper and set a timer to ring every ten minutes.<br><br>The results surprised both of us. I had expected the foil to win, because I thought metal would keep the cold in. In fact, the foil cube melted second fastest, just after the uncovered one. The glove was the clear winner: after fifty minutes, its ice cube was still about half its original size. Bao's explanation was that \"the glove is warm, so the ice must like it\". Mine was that wool traps air, and air is a poor conductor of heat, so warmth from the room could not reach the ice easily.<br><br>When I handed in the report, my teacher was less interested in which material had won than in how we had made the test fair. She pointed out that we had done one thing right without knowing it: all the bowls had sat on the same table, away from the window. Next time, she suggested, we should repeat the experiment three times before trusting the result.<br><br>Bao is already planning to test our mother's winter scarf.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A home experiment on keeping ice frozen and what the writer learned from it","Why wool is warmer than metal in winter","A nine-year-old boy who wants to become a scientist","How a science teacher marks homework reports"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage follows one experiment from the homework task to the teacher's comments. Wool and Bao are details inside that story, not the topic of the whole text."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why was one ice cube left uncovered?","options":["Because Bao forgot to wrap the last one","So that the wrapped cubes could be compared with it","Because the teacher asked for exactly six cubes","Because the writer ran out of materials to wrap it in"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the last cube was left uncovered <b>so that we had something to compare the others with</b>. Nothing suggests a mistake or a shortage of materials."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Which material did the writer expect to keep the ice frozen longest?","options":["The plastic bag","The woollen glove","The aluminium foil","The newspaper"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer says <b>I had expected the foil to win</b> because metal seemed likely to keep the cold in. The glove is what actually won, which is a different question."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the teacher's attitude to the experiment?","options":["She was disappointed that the foil lost","She believed the experiment should not be done at home","She thought the result was probably wrong","She cared more about the method than about which material won"],"correct":3,"explanation":"She was <b>less interested in which material had won than in how we had made the test fair</b>, praised the fair placing of the bowls and asked for repeats. That is a focus on method, not on the winner."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, the word \"traps\" is closest in meaning to","options":["holds in and stops from escaping","pushes out","catches an animal","warms up"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Wool <b>traps air</b> means it holds air inside its fibres so the air cannot move away. The sentence goes on to explain why that trapped air keeps warmth from reaching the ice."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, \"Mine\" refers to","options":["the writer's ice cube","the writer's explanation","the writer's glove","the writer's report"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The previous sentence gives <b>Bao's explanation</b>; \"Mine\" is the writer's explanation, set against it."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The ice cube wrapped in foil melted faster than the uncovered one.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The foil cube melted <b>second fastest, just after the uncovered one</b>, so the uncovered cube melted first and the foil cube was slower, not faster."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-18-2","level":"kc","title":"Before the Town Wakes Up","topic":"the town bakery","words":318,"passage":"Most people in Phu An know the bakery on Market Street by its smell rather than its name. By half past five in the morning, long before the shutters of the other shops go up, the scent of warm bread is already drifting across the square. The sign above the door says \"Hoa Bakery\", but everyone simply calls it \"the bakery\", as if there could be no other.<br><br>Mrs Hoa opened the shop twenty-eight years ago with a single oven and a recipe for baguettes that she had learned from her aunt. In those days she baked everything herself and sold it through a window, because there was no room inside for customers. Today the bakery has three ovens, a small café with six tables, and a team of five, including her son Duc, who left a job in the city to come home and take over the early shift.<br><br>Duc has introduced some changes, not all of them popular at first. He replaced the paper bags with cloth ones that customers bring back, added a shelf of wholemeal loaves, and started posting photographs of each morning's bread online. \"Some of the older customers said we were showing off,\" he admits. \"But then their grandchildren started coming in to see whether the bread looked as good as the pictures.\"<br><br>The bakery's busiest hour is between six and seven, when workers from the fish market and students from the secondary school queue together on the pavement. Anything left unsold at four in the afternoon is given to the pagoda at the end of the street, which cooks for elderly people who live alone.<br><br>Mrs Hoa still arrives at four every morning, although she says she no longer needs to. \"The bread would be fine without me,\" she says. \"But I wouldn't be fine without the bread.\" Her son, standing behind her with flour on his sleeves, does not argue.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best describes the passage?","options":["A guide to the best places to eat in Phu An","The story of a long-established bakery and how it is changing","An argument that small shops should sell their bread online","A description of a typical morning at a fish market"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The text moves from the bakery's beginnings to the son's changes and the mother's continuing role. The fish market and online photos appear only as details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did Mrs Hoa originally sell bread through a window?","options":["Because the shop had no space for customers","Because it kept the bread warm for longer","Because her aunt had sold bread in the same way","Because customers preferred not to come inside"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 gives the reason directly: <b>there was no room inside for customers</b>. The aunt is mentioned only as the source of the recipe."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"How did some older customers first react to the photographs online?","options":["They brought their grandchildren to see the bread","They asked for copies of the pictures","They stopped coming to the bakery","They felt the bakery was showing off"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Duc admits that <b>some of the older customers said we were showing off</b>. The grandchildren came on their own, later, to check the bread against the pictures."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 1, the word \"drifting\" is closest in meaning to","options":["being blown away by a strong wind","rising quickly","moving slowly through the air","disappearing completely"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The scent of bread <b>drifting across the square</b> spreads gently and slowly; the calm early-morning picture rules out a strong wind or a sudden rise."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer end the passage with Mrs Hoa's words about the bread?","options":["To show that the bakery cannot run without her","To show how strongly she is attached to her work","To suggest that she and her son disagree about the future","To explain why the bakery opens so early"],"correct":1,"explanation":"She admits the bread <b>would be fine without me</b> but that she would not be fine without it, which shows attachment, not necessity. Her son's silence suggests agreement, not disagreement."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Bread that is not sold by the afternoon is offered at a lower price.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says unsold bread <b>is given to the pagoda</b>, which cooks for elderly people. It is given away, not sold more cheaply."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Duc trained as a baker while he was in the city.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"We are told only that he <b>left a job in the city</b>; the passage never says what that job was or where he learned to bake."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-18-3","level":"kc","title":"The Morning the Road Disappeared","topic":"a snow day","words":319,"passage":"When I woke up, the light in my bedroom was wrong. It was too white, and too quiet. My first thought was that I had overslept and missed the bus. Then I pulled back the curtain and understood: the street, the cars, the roofs and the small park opposite had all disappeared under a thick layer of snow, and more was still falling.<br><br>We had moved to the town only in September, and my parents had warned me that winters here were serious. Even so, none of us was ready for that morning. My father spent twenty minutes searching for the car before he gave up, and my mother discovered that the front door would not open until we had pushed the snow away from the outside with a kitchen tray.<br><br>At half past seven, the message came through from school: closed for the day, all after-school activities cancelled, \"stay safe and enjoy it\". I read the last three words twice. In my old city, a day off school meant a storm, and a storm meant staying indoors with the windows shut. Here, apparently, it meant something else.<br><br>By nine o'clock the park was full. Children I only knew by sight from the school corridors were dragging plastic sleds up the slope and racing down it, screaming. A boy from my maths class, Tomas, handed me a spare sled without a word and pointed at the top. I fell off three times on the first run. On the fourth, I reached the bottom still sitting, and somebody cheered.<br><br>We stayed out until our gloves were soaked through. At home, my mother had made hot chocolate, and my father had finally found the car, by walking into it. The next morning the roads were cleared and the bus came as usual. But in the corridor, Tomas nodded at me, and it felt as if the town had opened a door.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What does the passage mainly describe?","options":["A sledding competition in a town park","The dangers of heavy snow for families with cars","How a snow day helped the writer feel part of a new town","Why schools should close whenever it snows"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The text begins with a newcomer who knows nobody and ends with a classmate's nod and the feeling that <b>the town had opened a door</b>. The car and the sleds are details along the way."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the writer's old city?","options":["It had a larger park than the new town","It had heavier snow than the new town","School was never closed there","Days off school there were caused by storms, not snow"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In the old city <b>a day off school meant a storm</b>, and people stayed indoors with the windows shut. The writer's surprise at \"enjoy it\" shows that snow days were something new."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"What did the family use to clear the snow from the front door?","options":["A kitchen tray","Their gloves","A shovel from the car","A plastic sled"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the door would not open until they had pushed the snow away <b>with a kitchen tray</b>. The sled belongs to a later paragraph, and the car could not even be found."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 5, \"soaked through\" is closest in meaning to","options":["torn in several places","completely wet","too small to wear","frozen stiff"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Gloves that are <b>soaked through</b> are wet all the way to the inside, which is why the children finally went home."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, \"it\" in \"stay safe and enjoy it\" refers to","options":["the school","the message","the day off","the bus"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The school is telling students to enjoy the day it has just cancelled. The next sentences confirm this by comparing what <b>a day off school</b> meant in the old city."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer mean by saying \"the town had opened a door\" in the last sentence?","options":["The school had reopened after the snow day","Tomas had invited the writer to his house","The roads were finally open again after the snow","The writer had started to feel accepted in the new town"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence follows Tomas's nod in the corridor, a sign of friendship from someone who had been a stranger. The door is a picture of being welcomed, not a real road or building."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer had never seen snow before that morning.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The family was <b>not ready</b> for such heavy snow and the old city had storms rather than snow days, but the passage never states whether the writer had seen snow before."}]},{"id":"rd-kc-18-4","level":"kc","title":"The Album Nobody Opened","topic":"a family photo album","words":328,"passage":"At the top of my grandmother's wardrobe, behind a box of winter blankets, there is a photo album with a dark green cover. I had known it was there for years, but nobody ever took it down. Last summer, when my grandmother broke her wrist and could not do much for herself, she asked me to bring it to her, and we spent three afternoons turning the pages together.<br><br>The first photographs were black and white and no bigger than a matchbox. In one, a serious young man in a white shirt stood in front of a wooden school with about thirty barefoot children. \"Your grandfather,\" she said. \"His first job. He was nineteen and terrified of them.\" I had only ever known my grandfather as a quiet old man who fed the chickens, and I found it hard to connect him with the stiff figure in the picture.<br><br>Further on, the pictures became larger and turned to colour, though the colours had faded to orange and pale blue. There was my mother at about my age, with a haircut she has since begged us never to mention, standing beside a bicycle that was clearly too big for her. There was a wedding in the rain, a house being built, and a whole page of a baby I did not recognise until my grandmother laughed and said it was me.<br><br>What struck me most was not the pictures themselves but how much my grandmother remembered around them: who had taken each photo, what had been said, what happened the next day. None of that was written anywhere. If she had not told me, it would have stayed inside her head.<br><br>Before I went home, I photographed every page with my phone and typed her stories underneath. My cousins now argue about which of them will keep the green album one day. My grandmother says that they are welcome to it, as long as they remember to open it.","questions":[{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Grandchildren should visit their grandparents more often when they are ill","A family cannot decide who should own a valuable album","Old photographs lose their colour if they are stored badly","Looking through a family album with a grandmother revealed stories that would otherwise be lost"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer's strongest point is that the memories <b>around</b> the photos were not written anywhere and would have stayed in the grandmother's head. The faded colours and the cousins' argument are smaller details."},{"kind":"detail","type":"mcq","q":"Why did the grandmother finally ask for the album?","options":["Because the writer found it while looking for blankets","Because her grandchildren had been asking to see it","Because she had injured her wrist and could not do much else","Because she wanted to give it to one of her grandchildren"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 links the request to the summer she <b>broke her wrist and could not do much for herself</b>. Giving the album away comes up only at the very end, and as a joke."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"How did the writer feel when looking at the photograph of the grandfather?","options":["Proud that he had been a teacher","Surprised that the young man was the same person as the old man she knew","Sorry that the children in the photo had no shoes","Amused that he had been afraid of the children"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer <b>found it hard to connect</b> the quiet old man who fed chickens with the stiff young teacher. That gap between the two images is the feeling described."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, the word \"faded\" is closest in meaning to","options":["lost their strength and brightness","been painted over","become brighter","changed into black and white"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Colours that have <b>faded to orange and pale blue</b> have grown weak with age. The photos were still in colour, just a dull version of it."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 4, \"None of that\" refers to","options":["the names of the people in the pictures","the afternoons the writer spent with her grandmother","the photographs in the album","the grandmother's memories connected with each photo"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The previous sentence lists what the grandmother remembered: who took each photo, what was said, what happened next. <b>None of that</b> was written down, meaning those memories."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer include the grandmother's last remark?","options":["To explain why the album was kept on top of the wardrobe","To show that she does not care who inherits the album","To make the point that an album only matters if it is actually looked at","To suggest that the cousins will argue for many years"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The condition <b>as long as they remember to open it</b> echoes the title and the first paragraph, where nobody ever took the album down. The value is in the looking, not the owning."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer's mother does not like being reminded of her old haircut.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The mother <b>has since begged us never to mention</b> the haircut, which is exactly this idea in other words."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-06-1","level":"ch","title":"The Slow Death of the Through-Road","topic":"urban planning","words":519,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Some three decades ago, the planners of a mid-sized port city set out to solve a problem that most of their colleagues elsewhere had quietly given up on: how to return the streets of a dense, car-choked district to the people who lived on them. Their answer was neither a new motorway nor a new tram line, but a rearrangement of what already existed. Groups of nine blocks were treated as a single unit; through-traffic was pushed to the perimeter, and the internal streets were surrendered to pedestrians, cyclists and the occasional delivery van crawling at walking pace. The scheme was cheap, reversible and, at first, deeply unpopular.<br><br><b>B.</b> Not until the fourth year did the tide of opinion turn. Shopkeepers who had predicted ruin found that footfall rose once the roar of engines subsided; parents who had once driven their children three hundred metres to school now let them walk. Noise levels dropped by a margin that surprised even the scheme's architects, and the space liberated from parked cars was gradually filled with benches, playgrounds and, in one memorable case, an outdoor chess club that met every evening whatever the weather. What had been sold as a traffic measure turned out to be a social one.<br><br><b>C.</b> Critics, however, were not entirely silenced, and some of their objections have aged rather well. Displacement is the most persistent. Traffic diverted from the interior does not vanish; it piles up on the perimeter roads, where those with the least say in council decisions tend to live. Then there is the question of who ultimately benefits. Calmer streets raise property values, and rising values push out precisely the tenants whose quality of life the scheme was meant to improve. Only where the city stepped in to hold rents steady did the original residents stay long enough to enjoy the benches they had campaigned for.<br><br><b>D.</b> Elsewhere, cities that have tried to import the model have discovered that it does not travel as easily as its admirers claim. A grid of short, regular blocks lends itself to being folded into larger units; a tangle of medieval lanes or a sprawling suburb of cul-de-sacs does not. Had the original planners been working with a different street pattern, it is doubtful the idea would ever have occurred to them. Density matters too: with too few people within walking distance of a shop, pedestrianising the street outside it merely makes it a quieter place to go out of business.<br><br><b>E.</b> What the experiment does prove is something more modest than its champions suggest and more significant than its detractors allow. The layout of a city is not fixed by the asphalt already laid. Paint, planters and a change in the rules of priority can, at a fraction of the cost of new infrastructure, alter how people move, meet and spend their time. Whether a city chooses to make that change, and whom it chooses to protect while doing so, is a question not of engineering but of politics, and it is one that no amount of traffic modelling can answer on the planners' behalf.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why the scheme cannot simply be copied in any city?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D says the model 'does not travel as easily as its admirers claim' and gives two conditions it depends on: a regular street grid and enough density."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a result that the scheme's own designers had not fully expected?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"In section B noise fell 'by a margin that surprised even the scheme's architects', and a traffic measure 'turned out to be a social one'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) suggests that the people who gain most from the scheme may not be the ones it was designed for?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C raises 'the question of who ultimately benefits': rising property values push out the very tenants the scheme was meant to help."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best sums up the writer's overall verdict on the scheme?","options":["It succeeded only because its designers ignored the objections of local shopkeepers.","It shows that new infrastructure is always preferable to cosmetic measures such as paint and planters.","It failed in its original city but has been improved by the cities that later adopted it.","It proves that cheap changes can reshape a city, but deciding who should benefit is a political matter."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Section E states the conclusion: paint, planters and new priority rules can alter city life at low cost, but whom to protect 'is a question not of engineering but of politics'."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the planners who devised the original scheme?","options":["Their idea owed a great deal to the particular street layout they happened to be working with.","They deliberately chose the district with the fewest cars to guarantee success.","They expected the scheme to be reversed within a few years.","They had studied medieval cities before drawing up their proposal."],"correct":0,"explanation":"'Had the original planners been working with a different street pattern, it is doubtful the idea would ever have occurred to them' implies the regular grid, not pure ingenuity, made the idea possible."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section B, the word 'footfall' is closest in meaning to:","options":["the noise made by people walking","the number of people visiting a place","the distance shoppers are prepared to walk","the cost of running a shop"],"correct":1,"explanation":"'Footfall' is the number of people entering a shop or area; shopkeepers who feared ruin found more customers came once the traffic went."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In section E, 'that change' refers to:","options":["laying new asphalt across the district","building infrastructure at great expense","using low-cost measures to alter how people move and meet","choosing which residents deserve protection"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The previous sentence describes paint, planters and new priority rules altering 'how people move, meet and spend their time'; 'that change' picks up this idea."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The scheme was later extended to cover the entire city.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage mentions other cities importing the model but says nothing about the original city widening the scheme beyond the district."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-06-2","level":"ch","title":"The Loop You Cannot See","topic":"psychology of habit","words":508,"passage":"Ask most people why they reach for their phone the moment a lift door closes, and they will offer a reason: boredom, a message they were expecting, a nagging sense that something needs checking. [1] The action has long since detached itself from any decision at all. It is triggered by the context, the closing door and the sudden stillness, and it runs to completion before the part of the mind that supplies reasons has even been consulted.<br><br>Psychologists describe such behaviour as a loop with three components: a cue, a routine and a reward. The cue is whatever the environment offers; the routine is the behaviour itself; the reward is what the brain learns to expect at the end. [2] Once that expectation is in place, the reward no longer has to arrive for the routine to fire. Anticipation does the work that satisfaction once did, which is why habits outlive the pleasure that created them.<br><br>This is also why willpower is such an unreliable ally. [3] By the time it has arrived, the routine is already under way, and interrupting a behaviour in progress costs far more effort than declining to start it. People who succeed in changing their habits rarely do so by summoning more resolve. What they do instead is rearrange the cues, so that the old trigger is absent and a new one is present at the moment the loop would ordinarily begin.<br><br>[4] A colleague who wants to stop snacking at her desk does not throw out the biscuits; she moves them to a cupboard on another floor. A student who cannot bring himself to begin an essay leaves the document open on his screen the night before, so that the first thing he sees in the morning is a half-written sentence. Neither has become a more disciplined person. Both have made the desired routine the path of least resistance and the undesired one a minor expedition.<br><br>None of this means that habits are beyond conscious influence, only that the influence must be applied at a different point from the one we instinctively choose. [5] The cue is where the leverage lies, and the cue, unlike the craving, is something we can usually see coming.<br><br><b>A.</b> The trouble with this account is that it describes the feeling of having decided rather than any decision that actually took place.<br><b>B.</b> The seemingly trivial examples that follow illustrate the principle better than any laboratory study could.<br><b>C.</b> Willpower is a late arrival: it responds to the craving, and the craving is itself a product of the cue.<br><b>D.</b> Rewards, in this model, are not optional extras but the very mechanism by which the loop is welded together.<br><b>E.</b> We tend to fight the routine head-on, at the moment of temptation, which is precisely where our resources are weakest.<br><b>F.</b> Children, for instance, acquire such loops far more quickly than adults and shed them with corresponding ease.<br><b>G.</b> Rewards of this kind are, of course, far easier to identify in laboratory animals than in people.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'This account' refers back to the reasons people offer, and 'rather than any decision that actually took place' leads into 'the action has long since detached itself from any decision at all'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D explains the role of rewards in 'this model' just after the three components are listed, and its 'welded together' sets up 'once that expectation is in place'. G mentions animals, which the passage never returns to."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C introduces willpower as 'a late arrival', and the next sentence continues with 'by the time it has arrived'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B announces 'the seemingly trivial examples that follow', and the paragraph then gives the colleague and the student. F offers a fresh claim about children that nothing afterwards develops."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E describes the point 'we instinctively choose' (the moment of temptation), contrasting with the cue 'where the leverage lies' in the following sentence."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's central claim?","options":["Habits are most effectively changed by altering what triggers them rather than by resisting them.","Most habits are harmless and do not need to be changed at all.","The reward at the end of a habit is the only part of the loop worth studying.","Habits can only be broken by people with unusually strong self-control."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage argues that willpower fails because it arrives too late, and that successful people 'rearrange the cues' instead; 'the cue is where the leverage lies'."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer suggest about the reasons people give for checking their phones in a lift?","options":["They are usually accurate but too embarrassing to admit.","They differ so much from person to person that no pattern can be found.","They show that people are more bored in lifts than elsewhere.","They are invented after the behaviour has already happened."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The routine 'runs to completion before the part of the mind that supplies reasons has even been consulted', so the reasons are produced afterwards, not as causes."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase 'a minor expedition' in the fourth paragraph is used to suggest that the undesired routine has become:","options":["a habit that is slowly returning","impossible to carry out","something that now requires deliberate effort","an activity done together with colleagues"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Moving biscuits to another floor makes snacking a small journey; the joke is that the bad habit now takes planning and effort, unlike the 'path of least resistance'."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-06-3","level":"ch","title":"When the Wind Blows Too Hard","topic":"renewable energy","words":546,"passage":"<b>A.</b> On a blustery night in early spring, the turbines strung along the ridges of northern Scotland can generate more electricity than the whole of Britain is able to use. Some of it is stored, some is exported along undersea cables, and the rest is simply not produced: operators are paid to turn their blades out of the wind and let it pass through unharvested. To the public, when it hears of this at all, the arrangement seems absurd. Having paid to build the machines, why would anyone pay again to switch them off?<br><br><b>B.</b> The answer lies in a mismatch that the early advocates of renewable power rarely mentioned. Wind and sunlight are abundant, free and entirely indifferent to when we want them. A gas plant can be told to raise its output at six in the evening, when kettles and cookers come on; a wind farm cannot be told anything. Nor is the grid itself neutral. Built over a century to carry power from a few large stations near the cities to the households around them, it was never designed to move vast quantities the other way, from windswept hills where almost nobody lives to the crowded south where almost everybody does. On the windiest nights the cables from the north are full long before the turbines are.<br><br><b>C.</b> Three remedies are usually proposed, and each has its champions. The first is more wire: new transmission lines to carry the surplus where it is needed. These take a decade to plan, are fiercely opposed by the communities they cross, and risk being obsolete on the day they open if generation has meanwhile moved elsewhere. The second is storage, whether batteries, pumped reservoirs or hydrogen, to hold tonight's wind for tomorrow's dinner hour. Batteries have grown cheaper at a pace few forecasters dared predict, but they still store hours rather than the weeks that a calm, dark January demands. The third, and least glamorous, is to reshape demand: to persuade factories, water companies and eventually households to use power when it is plentiful rather than when it is convenient.<br><br><b>D.</b> It is this third path that has quietly begun to matter most. Not because it is elegant, for it is not, but because it asks for no new infrastructure and rewards those who adopt it almost immediately. A cold-storage warehouse that chills its produce a few degrees further on a windy night can let its compressors rest the following afternoon. A fleet of electric buses charged at three in the morning is, in effect, a battery the grid did not have to buy. Multiply such arrangements across an economy, and the surplus that once had to be thrown away becomes the cheapest energy in the system.<br><br><b>E.</b> None of this makes the wasted gigawatts of that spring night any less real, and it would be dishonest to pretend that a grid built for one era can be nudged into another without cost. But the lesson is not the one the sceptics draw. The problem is not that the wind blows at the wrong time; it is that we have organised our lives around a kind of power that could be summoned on demand, and we are only now learning to live with one that cannot.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why the electricity network struggles to deliver renewable power to the people who need it?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes a grid built to carry power outward from a few large stations, 'never designed to move vast quantities the other way'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives examples of organisations changing the time at which they use electricity?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D describes the cold-storage warehouse chilling further on a windy night and the bus fleet charging at three in the morning."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) weighs up the drawbacks of several competing solutions?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C sets out three remedies, more wire, storage and reshaped demand, and notes the weaknesses of each."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) rejects the conclusion that critics of wind power usually reach?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E says 'the lesson is not the one the sceptics draw' and reframes the problem as our expectations rather than the wind's timing."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer open with the image of turbines being switched off on a windy night?","options":["to argue that more turbines should be built in the south of Britain","to prove that wind power in Scotland has been a financial failure","to present a situation that appears irrational before explaining the reasons behind it","to show how much money operators earn from the electricity they export"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Section A ends with a puzzled question ('why would anyone pay again to switch them off?') that the rest of the passage answers."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the early supporters of renewable energy?","options":["They expected the grid to be rebuilt before the first turbines were installed.","They favoured solar power over wind because it was more predictable.","They were unaware that gas plants could adjust their output.","They played down a weakness in the technology they were promoting."],"correct":3,"explanation":"A mismatch that advocates 'rarely mentioned' implies they knew about it but preferred not to draw attention to it while making their case."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section C, the word 'obsolete' is closest in meaning to:","options":["no longer useful","extremely expensive","fully operational","environmentally harmful"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A transmission line is 'obsolete' when it is out of date and no longer needed, here because the generation it was built to carry has moved elsewhere."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Batteries can now store enough electricity to cover several weeks of calm weather.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says batteries 'still store hours rather than the weeks that a calm, dark January demands', so the statement contradicts the text."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-07-1","level":"ch","title":"The Long Apprenticeship: A Short History of Bookbinding","topic":"history of a craft","words":506,"passage":"<b>A.</b> For most of antiquity a book was a scroll: a strip of papyrus wound round a rod, read by unrolling with one hand and rolling up with the other. The codex — sheets folded, stacked and sewn through the fold — appeared in the Roman world in the first centuries of our era, and by the fourth century it had all but supplanted the scroll. It was easier to carry, easier to consult and, crucially for the early Church, easier to search for a particular passage. In this period binding was not a trade at all. The monk who copied a text usually sewed it as well, in the same room and with the same patience.<br><br><b>B.</b> The medieval binding that emerged from those monasteries was built to outlast its makers. Sheets of parchment were sewn onto stout cords, the cords were laced into oak boards, and the boards were covered in leather, often with brass clasps to hold the whole thing shut. The clasps were not a security measure: parchment swells and buckles in damp air, and only constant pressure keeps it flat. Decoration was pressed into the leather with heated tools, at first without colour and then, from the late fifteenth century, in gold — a technique that reached Venice from the Islamic world and spread north with astonishing speed.<br><br><b>C.</b> Printing altered the craft more than any invention before or since. A press could turn out a thousand identical copies in weeks, and no monastery could sew them. Binding became a separate business, carried on in town workshops with their own guilds, apprentices and jealously guarded secrets. Books were usually sold as loose gatherings of printed sheets; the buyer took them to a binder and chose the covering, so that a gentleman's library would match from shelf to shelf. Wooden boards gave way to pasteboard, sheepskin to cheaper calf, and a binding took days rather than months.<br><br><b>D.</b> The nineteenth century split the craft in two. Publishers began issuing books ready bound in cloth, and machines were built to make the cover separately and glue it to the sewn block — a method known as case binding, which is still how most hardbacks are produced. The trade binder became a factory worker. At the same time a small group of craftsmen reacted against the machine, reviving hand sewing, tooling and the use of fine leathers, and insisting that a book could be a work of art in its own right. The two branches have never rejoined.<br><br><b>E.</b> It might have been expected that the screen would finish off what the machine had started. The reverse has happened. As reading migrates to devices, the printed book has become an object people choose deliberately, and demand for hand-bound volumes, restored manuscripts and one-off artist's books has grown rather than shrunk. Training has not become any shorter, though. As one binder put it to me, you learn to sew in a week and to judge the grain of a piece of leather in ten years.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains the real purpose of the clasps on medieval books?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B says the clasps \"were not a security measure\": parchment buckles in damp air and only constant pressure keeps it flat."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives a reason why buyers preferred books that had not yet been bound?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C explains that books were sold as loose sheets so the buyer could choose the covering and make a whole library \"match from shelf to shelf\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) states that a division within the craft has proved permanent?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D describes the craft splitting into factory binding and art binding and ends: \"The two branches have never rejoined.\""},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes an outcome that is the opposite of what people expected?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E opens with what \"might have been expected\" — that screens would kill the craft — and then says \"The reverse has happened.\""},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why hand-bound books are worth more than machine-made ones","The role of monasteries in preserving classical literature","The technical steps involved in binding a book by hand","How bookbinding has been reshaped by each new technology over the centuries"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Each section pairs a technological change — the codex, printing, the binding machine, the screen — with what it did to the craft. The other options are single details or are not argued at all."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the binder's remark at the end of Section E suggest about the craft?","options":["Leather has been largely abandoned by modern binders.","Sewing is the hardest skill a new binder has to master.","The mechanical steps are quickly learned, but the judgement behind them takes years.","Most apprentices abandon the trade before they finish training."],"correct":2,"explanation":"\"A week\" for sewing against \"ten years\" for judging leather contrasts a mechanical action with an act of judgement; the remark says nothing about apprentices leaving or leather disappearing."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"supplanted\" in Section A is closest in meaning to","options":["supported","replaced","imitated","preserved"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The codex \"had all but supplanted the scroll\" by the fourth century — that is, it had almost entirely taken the scroll's place."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Gold tooling was invented by binders in Venice.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section B says the technique \"reached Venice from the Islamic world\" — Venice was where it entered Europe, not where it was invented."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-07-2","level":"ch","title":"How Verbs Turn into Grammar","topic":"linguistics","words":560,"passage":"Ask an English speaker what \"will\" means and the answer comes at once: it marks the future. Yet a thousand years ago the verb <i>willan</i> meant to want, and \"I will\" was a statement about desire rather than about time. [1] Linguists call this process grammaticalisation — the slow drift by which a word with a meaning of its own becomes a piece of machinery for expressing tense, number, negation or possession.<br><br>The process follows a recognisable path. A full lexical word — a verb, a noun, an adverb — begins to appear regularly in one fixed construction, where its original meaning fades and a more abstract one takes over. [2] Consider \"going to\". In its future sense it is routinely reduced to \"gonna\", but in its literal sense it never is: \"I'm gonna leave\" is ordinary speech, whereas \"I'm gonna the shops\" is impossible. The pronunciation has split exactly where the meaning has.<br><br>Why do languages keep doing this? One answer is that grammar wears out. Like a coin that loses its stamp through handling, a marker used in every sentence eventually becomes too faint to notice. [3] French negation shows the cycle in full. Old French said no with <i>ne</i> alone; <i>pas</i>, literally \"a step\", was added for emphasis, as in \"I won't go a step\"; in time <i>pas</i> became compulsory, and in today's spoken French it is <i>ne</i> that has quietly dropped out, leaving the former intensifier to do the whole job.<br><br>What is most striking is how alike the routes are across languages that have never been in contact. Verbs meaning \"go\" have become future markers in West Africa, in the Pacific and in Europe. Nouns for body parts — \"back\", \"head\", \"belly\" — have turned into prepositions meaning behind, on top of and inside on several continents independently. [4] Movement towards a place is, after all, a natural picture of intention, and a head is a natural picture of \"above\"; the metaphors are available to any mind, so any language may take them up.<br><br>None of this is visible to the people doing it. [5] It follows that the grammar in a textbook is not a set of laws but a snapshot of a river: accurate for the moment the photograph was taken, and already slightly out of date by the time it is printed.<br><br><b>A.</b> Speakers who want to be emphatic reach for a fresher expression, and in time that too is worn smooth by use.<br><b>B.</b> Borrowed words behave quite differently, usually keeping their foreign pronunciation for several generations before they are absorbed.<br><b>C.</b> The change did not happen overnight: in sentences such as \"I will go\", wanting and intending overlap so closely that a listener could hear a claim about the future where the speaker had meant one about desire.<br><b>D.</b> Nobody decides that a verb should become a tense marker; the shift is the sum of millions of tiny reinterpretations, each of which felt at the time like no change at all.<br><b>E.</b> Its pronunciation then erodes, since speakers spend little effort on elements that carry little information of their own.<br><b>F.</b> For this reason many schools now teach the history of grammar alongside its present-day rules.<br><b>G.</b> Such regularity suggests that the changes are driven not by the accidents of history but by the way human minds handle meaning.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gap sits between the old meaning of <i>willan</i> (to want) and the label \"this process\"; C explains how \"wanting\" was reheard as \"future\", which is the process being named."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"\"Its pronunciation then erodes\" continues the ordered path (\"begins to appear… then…\") and sets up the \"going to\" / \"gonna\" example that follows."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"After the worn coin image, A supplies the speakers' response — reaching for a fresher, emphatic form — which is exactly what <i>pas</i> was in the French example that follows."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"\"Such regularity\" refers back to the same routes appearing on several continents, and \"the way human minds handle meaning\" is then illustrated by the metaphors of movement and \"head\"."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D expands \"None of this is visible to the people doing it\" — nobody decides, the change is millions of unnoticed reinterpretations — and \"It follows that…\" then draws the conclusion."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","options":["Textbooks should stop teaching grammar rules because they are always out of date.","Pronunciation changes faster than meaning in every living language.","English grammar has borrowed most of its structures from French.","Ordinary words gradually and unconsciously turn into grammatical markers, following similar paths in many languages."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage names grammaticalisation, traces its path, explains why it recurs and shows that the routes are shared across unrelated languages. Nothing recommends abandoning textbooks, and French is only one example."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the French word <i>pas</i> from paragraph 3?","options":["In negative sentences it no longer carries any sense of \"a step\".","It was borrowed into French from a neighbouring language.","It is used only in formal written French.","It has recently been replaced by <i>ne</i> in everyday speech."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The writer calls <i>pas</i> \"the former intensifier\" that now does \"the whole job\" of negation; having become pure grammar, it has lost its literal meaning — the very process the passage describes. The passage says <i>ne</i>, not <i>pas</i>, has dropped out."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"erodes\" in piece E is closest in meaning to","options":["becomes louder","wears away gradually","changes suddenly","is written down"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Pronunciation \"erodes\" because speakers spend little effort on it — it is gradually worn down, as \"going to\" is worn down to \"gonna\"."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-07-3","level":"ch","title":"Counting the Unseen","topic":"wildlife conservation","words":526,"passage":"<b>A.</b> You cannot protect what you cannot count, and for most of the last century counting wild animals meant seeing them. Surveyors walked fixed lines through the bush noting every nest, tallied tracks in fresh snow, or flew over herds in light aircraft. For animals that live in the open this worked tolerably well. For the rest — a nocturnal cat in a mountain forest, a fish in a turbid river, a frog that calls for two nights a year — the published figures were often little more than guesses dressed up in decimals. More than one species declared extinct turned out simply to be one that nobody had looked for hard enough.<br><br><b>B.</b> The first tool to change this was almost absurdly simple: a camera strapped to a tree, triggered by movement and body heat. Left for weeks, it records whatever walks past, by day or by night, without a human within miles. Where animals carry individual markings — the stripes of a tiger, the rosettes of a leopard, the notched fin of a dolphin — each photograph can be matched to a known individual, and statisticians can estimate the whole population from how often familiar faces reappear. The results upended settled beliefs. A forest written off as empty of large predators proved to hold a breeding population; a reserve celebrated for its tigers held a fraction of the number printed in its brochure.<br><br><b>C.</b> The second tool does not need to see the animal at all. Every creature sheds skin cells, scales, mucus and waste into its surroundings, and a litre of river water, filtered and sequenced, yields a list of the species that have passed upstream in the previous few days. Environmental DNA, as the method is called, has found rare newts in ponds where a decade of netting had found nothing, and it can flag an invasive fish long before it is common enough to catch. Its limits are real, though. It says that a species is present, not how many there are, and because DNA drifts with the current, \"where\" is only approximate.<br><br><b>D.</b> Better numbers, it turns out, do not automatically produce better decisions. When a survey reveals fewer animals than everyone believed, funders may read the result as failure and withdraw; when it reveals more, a ministry may conclude that the species needs no protection after all and open the forest to logging. One field biologist told me, not entirely joking, that the most useful figure her organisation ever published was a slightly optimistic one. The new tools have made that kind of usefulness impossible.<br><br><b>E.</b> The next generation of methods is already in the field: microphones that log every call in a forest, software that sorts a million images in an afternoon, village volunteers who maintain the cameras and are paid for each verified sighting. What none of these can do is explain. A camera will tell you that a population has halved in five years; it will not tell you whether the cause is poaching, disease, a new road or a drought a hundred miles away. The count is where the work begins, not where it ends.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a method that can detect a threat before it has become serious?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says environmental DNA \"can flag an invasive fish long before it is common enough to catch\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains how researchers tell one animal from another?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes individual markings — stripes, rosettes, a notched fin — that let each photograph be matched to a known animal."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives an example of accurate information leading to a harmful decision?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D describes a ministry reading a higher count as proof that a species needs no protection and opening the forest to logging."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that knowing the size of a population is not the same as understanding it?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E says the new tools cannot \"explain\": a camera shows that a population has halved but not why, so \"the count is where the work begins\"."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main point?","options":["Camera traps have solved the problem of counting rare species.","New survey methods have transformed how wildlife is counted, but counting alone does not protect it.","Conservation organisations routinely exaggerate the numbers they publish.","Environmental DNA is more reliable than camera trapping."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sections B and C present the new tools, D and E show that better data bring their own difficulties and cannot explain decline. The passage does not rank the two methods and does not accuse organisations in general."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the field biologist's remark in Section D imply?","options":["An inflated estimate once did more for her cause than an accurate one would have.","Optimistic estimates are generally closer to the truth than pessimistic ones.","She wishes the new counting methods had never been introduced.","Her organisation deliberately falsified its data for many years."],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"The most useful figure… was a slightly optimistic one\" concedes that a generous number once helped — \"useful\" for winning support, not for accuracy. \"Not entirely joking\" and \"slightly\" rule out years of deliberate fraud, and nothing says she regrets the tools."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"turbid\" in Section A is closest in meaning to","options":["shallow","frozen","fast-flowing","cloudy"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The river is listed among places where animals cannot be seen, alongside a forest at night; a turbid river is one whose water is thick with mud or silt."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Environmental DNA sampling can tell researchers how many individuals of a species live in a river.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section C states the method's limit directly: \"It says that a species is present, not how many there are.\""}]},{"id":"rd-ch-08-1","level":"ch","title":"The Arithmetic of the Corner Shop","topic":"economics of small business","words":542,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Ask most people why small shops close and they will reach for the obvious villain: the supermarket down the road, the online giant, the landlord who doubled the rent. All of these matter. Yet economists who study small enterprises point to a quieter culprit, one that rarely makes the news because it is not dramatic. It is the slow mismatch between what a business earns and when it earns it. A bakery may be perfectly profitable on paper and still fail, because its bills arrive on the first of the month while its customers pay in dribs and drabs over thirty days.<br><br><b>B.</b> This gap between profit and cash is the first thing that surprises new owners. Suppose a café sells a coffee for three pounds that costs one pound to make. The margin looks handsome. What the figure hides is everything that must be paid before the first cup is poured: the lease, the machine, the staff who stand ready through the empty hours between breakfast and lunch. Fixed costs, as they are called, do not shrink when custom is slow, and it is these, far more than the price of milk, that decide whether the shutters stay up. Only when the daily takings clear that fixed threshold does a single sale begin to add anything to the owner's pocket.<br><br><b>C.</b> No sooner has an owner grasped this than a second lesson arrives: scale cuts both ways. A chain can negotiate cheaper flour, spread the cost of an accountant across fifty branches, and survive a bad month in one town because a good month elsewhere covers it. A single shop has none of these cushions. On the other hand, the chain must standardise, and standardisation is precisely what a small business need not do. It can know its regulars by name, change the menu on a Tuesday because a supplier turned up with unexpected plums, and turn a passing enthusiasm into a product by Friday. Had the corner shop tried to compete on price, it would have lost years ago; that it competes on nimbleness is the reason it is still there.<br><br><b>D.</b> Then there is the question of what an owner's time is actually worth. Many sole traders never put their own wage into the accounts, so the business appears to make money when, in reality, it is being subsidised by seventy-hour weeks at a rate no employee would accept. Economists call this the opportunity cost — the salary the owner is quietly forgoing elsewhere — and its omission is the single most common reason a shop that \"does well\" leaves its owner poorer each year.<br><br><b>E.</b> None of this is an argument against opening a shop. It is an argument for opening one with the sums done honestly. The most durable small businesses tend to share three habits: they keep a reserve equal to at least two months of fixed costs, they price for the quiet weeks rather than the busy ones, and they treat the owner's time as an expense from day one. What they offer that no spreadsheet can capture is the reason customers walk past a cheaper alternative to reach them — and that, in the end, is the only margin that cannot be undercut.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a cost that owners commonly leave out of their accounts?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D says many sole traders <b>never put their own wage into the accounts</b>, and names this missing item the opportunity cost."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives an example of a small business changing what it sells at very short notice?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C describes a shop that can <b>change the menu on a Tuesday</b> because a supplier arrived with unexpected plums, and turn an idea into a product by Friday."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) lists the practices shared by small businesses that last?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E says the most durable small businesses <b>share three habits</b> and then lists them: a two-month reserve, pricing for quiet weeks, and counting the owner's time as an expense."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why supermarkets and online retailers are driving small shops out of business","The less obvious financial reasons why small businesses survive or fail","How large chains use their size to negotiate lower prices from suppliers","Practical advice on attracting regular customers to a café"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage opens by setting aside the \"obvious villains\" and then examines the quieter causes: cash timing, fixed costs, scale and the owner's unpaid time. The chains and the customers are details within that argument."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer mean by \"the only margin that cannot be undercut\" in the final sentence?","options":["The gap between a product's selling price and the cost of its ingredients","The two-month reserve that protects a shop through its quiet weeks","Customers' attachment to a shop, which a cheaper rival cannot price away","The extra hours an owner works without paying themselves a wage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence follows \"the reason customers walk past a cheaper alternative to reach them\". A rival can beat a shop's prices but cannot undercut loyalty, so that loyalty is the <b>margin</b> the writer has in mind, not a figure in the accounts."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"nimbleness\" in section C is closest in meaning to","options":["the lowest prices in the area","long opening hours","friendly personal service","the ability to adapt quickly"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts competing on price with competing on <b>nimbleness</b>, right after examples of a shop changing its menu and its products within days. Nimble means quick and light on one's feet; here, quick to adapt."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"According to the writer, a rise in the price of milk matters less to a café's survival than its fixed costs do.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Section B says fixed costs, <b>far more than the price of milk</b>, decide whether the shutters stay up. The statement matches this."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Most small businesses in the writer's country close within their first three years.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage discusses why shops close but never gives a figure for how many close or how quickly. With no information either way, the answer is <b>Not Given</b>."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-08-2","level":"ch","title":"Small Boxes, Long Journeys","topic":"space exploration","words":505,"passage":"For most of the space age, a mission was an event. Years of design went into a single spacecraft, which was launched once, on a rocket that was thrown away, towards a target it would never be able to revisit. [1] The cost of failure was so enormous that engineers built in redundancy upon redundancy, which made every craft heavier, which made the rocket bigger, which made the whole enterprise dearer still.<br><br>That logic has begun to unwind. [2] A university department that could never have afforded a place on a rocket thirty years ago can today book a corner of a shared launch for less than the price of a laboratory instrument. What was once the preserve of a handful of national agencies is now open to start-ups, schools and, in a few cases, individuals.<br><br>The consequences for science are subtle rather than spectacular. A single large observatory can see farther than a swarm of small ones, and nobody expects a shoebox to photograph the rings of Saturn. [3] Twenty cheap satellites watching the same storm from twenty angles, or a dozen probes dropped into a comet's tail, produce a kind of knowledge that a lone, precious instrument cannot: knowledge of how things vary. Not until the price of a launch fell did it become possible to ask such questions at all.<br><br>There are costs, of course, and not only financial ones. Low orbit is filling up. [4] Astronomers complain that streaks of reflected sunlight now cross their long exposures, and the risk of collision rises with every launch. Whether the benefits of cheap access outweigh the clutter it creates is a question the field has only just started to take seriously.<br><br>What seems certain is that the age of the single, irreplaceable mission is passing. [5] It is the engineers who are learning to accept the occasional loss, and the scientists who are learning to think in populations rather than in individuals, who are shaping the next chapter. Had the old model persisted, the sky would be quieter — and a great deal less would be known about it.<br><br><i>Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There are two extra sentences you do not need to use.</i><br><br><b>A.</b> Yet there are questions that a single instrument, however powerful, is simply the wrong tool to answer.<br><b>B.</b> Its successor will not be one grander programme but a scattering of smaller, riskier and more frequent ones.<br><b>C.</b> The first human landing on another planet is now expected within the lifetime of today's students.<br><b>D.</b> Nothing could be repaired, replaced or retried once the countdown reached zero.<br><b>E.</b> Thousands of small satellites, many of them no longer working, now share a band of space that was almost empty two decades ago.<br><b>F.</b> Rockets whose first stages fly back and land have cut the price of reaching orbit dramatically, and satellites have shrunk to the size of a loaf of bread.<br><b>G.</b> Most of these instruments are assembled by the same three manufacturers.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap sits between a craft launched \"once\" on a rocket \"thrown away\" and the sentence about the enormous <b>cost of failure</b>. D explains why failure was so costly: nothing could be repaired, replaced or retried."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"\"That logic has begun to unwind\" needs a sentence saying what changed. F gives the two changes — reusable rockets and tiny satellites — that make the cheap shared launch in the next sentence possible."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence before concedes that a single large observatory sees farther; A's <b>Yet</b> turns the argument round, and the sentence after gives the questions (how things vary) that only many small instruments can answer."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"\"Low orbit is filling up\" is expanded by E's thousands of satellites in a once-empty band of space, which in turn explains the astronomers' streaks and the collision risk that follow."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"<b>Its successor</b> in B refers back to \"the age of the single, irreplaceable mission\"; the smaller, riskier missions it describes lead into engineers accepting the occasional loss."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer say that \"nobody expects a shoebox to photograph the rings of Saturn\"?","options":["To show that photographing Saturn is no longer a useful goal for science","To explain why university departments prefer to share a launch","To admit the limits of small satellites before arguing that their value lies elsewhere","To argue that small satellites have made large observatories unnecessary"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The remark is a concession: the writer grants what small satellites cannot do, then uses \"Yet\" and the storm example to show the different kind of knowledge they <b>can</b> produce. It weakens neither large observatories nor the Saturn goal."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"the preserve of\" in the second paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["something feared by","something restricted to a particular group","something protected from damage","something invented by"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts what was once limited to \"a handful of national agencies\" with what is \"now open to\" start-ups and schools. A <b>preserve</b> here is an activity kept for one group alone."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer believes the problem of orbital clutter has already been examined thoroughly by researchers.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says the question is one the field has <b>only just started to take seriously</b>, which contradicts the idea that it has been thoroughly examined."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-08-3","level":"ch","title":"What Travelled With the Silk","topic":"ancient trade routes","words":533,"passage":"<b>A.</b> The popular picture of an ancient trade route is a single caravan setting out from one end of a continent and, months later, arriving at the other. Almost no one made such a journey. Goods travelled far; people, on the whole, did not. A bale of cloth might pass through a dozen pairs of hands, each merchant carrying it only as far as the next market he knew, selling it on at a small profit and turning for home. The route was a relay, not a marathon, and its length was the sum of many short, familiar trips rather than one heroic expedition.<br><br><b>B.</b> What decided where those trips went was rarely the shortest line on a map. It was water. Caravans followed strings of oases across deserts, and the distance between one well and the next set the size of the load an animal could carry. Where rivers ran in the right direction, cargo left the road altogether. On the coasts, the seasonal winds mattered more than any harbour: a ship that missed the turning of the monsoon might wait half a year for the next one. The geography of trade, in other words, was written by rainfall and wind long before any ruler drew a border across it.<br><br><b>C.</b> Cities grew wherever a route was forced to pause. A mountain pass that closed in winter, a river that had to be crossed by ferry, a stretch of desert that could only be entered with a fresh supply of water — each created a place where goods piled up, where merchants waited, and where someone could make a living by feeding, lodging and taxing them. Some of these halts became great cities whose wealth depended entirely on the traffic passing through; when the traffic shifted, the city withered, sometimes within a single generation.<br><br><b>D.</b> The cargo that mattered most was frequently the cargo nobody had paid for. Along with the silk and the spices went the techniques for making paper, the method of casting a particular kind of metal, a new way of tuning a stringed instrument, and stories that surface, subtly altered, in the folk tales of peoples thousands of kilometres apart. Religions moved along the same paths, carried by merchants who prayed at each stop and by monks who found in the caravan the safest way to travel. So, less happily, did disease; a route that could carry a rumour from one end of a continent to the other could carry a plague just as efficiently.<br><br><b>E.</b> Why did the great overland routes fade? Not, as is often said, because ships were suddenly found to be faster; sailors had been carrying goods along the coasts for as long as caravans had crossed the interior. What changed was the balance of cost and risk. A single ship could carry the load of hundreds of animals and pass through the hands of one merchant rather than a dozen, and every pair of hands the cargo avoided was a toll, a bribe or a theft it also avoided. Once the sea became reliably cheaper, the halts along the old roads lost their reason to exist, and the relay quietly stopped being run.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why some cities lost their wealth quickly?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says some halts became cities whose wealth depended entirely on passing traffic, and <b>when the traffic shifted, the city withered</b>, sometimes within a generation."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that a common explanation for the decline of the overland routes is mistaken?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E rejects the idea that ships were \"suddenly found to be faster\" — <b>as is often said</b> — and offers cost and risk as the real reason."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes how natural conditions determined the direction a route took?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B says water, oases, rivers and seasonal winds decided where trips went: the geography of trade was <b>written by rainfall and wind</b>."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) mentions something harmful that spread along the routes?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D ends with disease: a route that could carry a rumour could carry <b>a plague</b> just as efficiently."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best sums up the passage?","options":["Ancient trade routes were chains of short journeys, shaped by geography and carrying far more than goods","Faster ships were the main reason that overland trade came to an end","Merchants in ancient times regularly travelled the full length of a continent","Great cities on the trade routes were founded by rulers in order to collect taxes"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each section develops one part of this picture: the relay (A), water and wind (B), cities at the halts (C), unpaid-for cargo (D) and why the relay stopped (E). The other options contradict sections A, C and E."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does section D imply about how merchants spread religion?","options":["They were paid by religious leaders to carry beliefs to new lands","They spread their faith simply by practising it wherever they stopped","They were forbidden to travel with monks in the same caravan","They regarded religious objects as the most valuable goods they carried"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Religions were \"carried by merchants who <b>prayed at each stop</b>\": the merchants were not missionaries but travellers whose everyday worship, seen at halt after halt, spread the faith. Nothing in the text supports payment, a ban or a sale of religious goods."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"withered\" in section C is closest in meaning to","options":["moved to a new site","expanded rapidly","shrank and lost its strength","changed its name"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A city that had depended on passing traffic lost that traffic; like a plant without water, it <b>withered</b>, that is, shrank and weakened."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer believes that rulers' borders determined the course of most ancient trade routes.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section B says the geography of trade was written by rainfall and wind <b>long before any ruler drew a border</b> across it, which contradicts the statement."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-09-1","level":"ch","title":"The Quiet Retreat from the League Table","topic":"education policy","words":490,"passage":"<b>A.</b> For the best part of two decades, the annual publication of the national school rankings was treated as a fixture of the calendar, as predictable as the examinations whose results it digested. Little did the ministry that introduced the tables imagine that it would one day be the body to bury them. Yet that is precisely what happened last spring, when, without so much as a press release, the rankings vanished from the ministry's website. In their place appeared a searchable dashboard on which no school is placed above any other.<br><br><b>B.</b> It is worth recalling why the tables were created in the first place. Their champions argued that parents were choosing schools in the dark, and that a public ranking would let the sunlight in. Weak schools would be exposed; strong ones would be rewarded with applications; all of them would be pushed to improve. \"Transparency was the whole argument,\" recalls a former adviser who helped to draft the policy. \"And in a narrow sense it worked. Scores in the subjects we measured went up, year after year.\" She pauses before adding that narrowness was precisely the problem.<br><br><b>C.</b> What the tables measured, schools taught; what the tables ignored, schools quietly dropped. Art, music and outdoor education shrank to make room for examination practice. Some schools discovered that discouraging their weakest pupils from sitting a paper did more for their position than teaching them ever could. Nowhere was this more visible than in the crowded middle of the table, where a single percentage point could move a school twenty places in either direction, and where head teachers came to feel that they were managing a number rather than a community. The tables had begun as a description of schools; before long, schools had reshaped themselves around the tables.<br><br><b>D.</b> The dashboard that replaced them offers a deliberately untidy picture. Alongside examination results sit measures of pupils' progress since entry, attendance, staff turnover and the findings of an annual wellbeing survey. No composite score is calculated, and the ministry has stated that it has no intention of calculating one. Critics call the result opaque. Parents seem divided: in a survey published last month, roughly a third said they missed having \"a simple answer\", but fewer than one in ten said they would now choose a different school for their child.<br><br><b>E.</b> Whether the reform succeeds will not be known for years, and perhaps not even then, since the question of what makes a good school has never been settled. What the episode does reveal is something about policy itself. A measure that begins as a description tends, once it carries consequences, to become a target, and a target, sooner or later, ceases to describe. Had the ministry foreseen this, it might have built its tables differently. It remains to be seen whether the dashboard, should parents ever come to lean on it as heavily, can escape the same fate.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes the ways in which schools changed their own behaviour in response to the rankings?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C lists what schools did: dropping unmeasured subjects, keeping weak pupils out of examinations, and reshaping themselves around the tables."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) sets out the original case for publishing the rankings?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B recalls the champions' argument: parents were choosing in the dark and transparency would push every school to improve."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) reports how parents have responded to the change?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D quotes the survey in which a third of parents missed a simple answer but fewer than one in ten would choose a different school."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best sums up the passage as a whole?","options":["Transparency in education has failed and should be abandoned altogether.","A ministry abolished school rankings because parents had stopped consulting them.","School rankings raised measured results but distorted schools, and their replacement may run the same risk.","The new dashboard has proved far more popular with parents than the rankings it replaced."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage traces the rise of the tables, the damage they did, and ends by warning that the dashboard could suffer the same fate. Parents' views are divided, and the writer never argues against transparency itself."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred from the way the rankings were removed (section A)?","options":["The removal was ordered by a court rather than by the ministry.","The ministry was reluctant to draw attention to reversing its own policy.","The ministry's website had been redesigned for purely technical reasons.","Journalists had lost interest in the rankings many years earlier."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The tables were removed 'without so much as a press release' by the very ministry that had introduced them. Quietly burying a policy you once championed suggests a wish not to publicise the U-turn."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'fixture' in section A is closest in meaning to","options":["a permanent and expected feature","a piece of sporting equipment","a controversial decision","a temporary arrangement"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The publication is compared to the examinations themselves, 'as predictable as' them, so a <b>fixture</b> here is something fixed and expected in the calendar."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The ministry plans to add a single overall score to the dashboard in the future.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section D says no composite score is calculated and the ministry 'has no intention of calculating one', which directly contradicts the statement."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply in the final sentence of the passage?","options":["Parents are unlikely ever to consult the dashboard at all.","The dashboard will be withdrawn as soon as a new minister takes office.","If parents rely on the dashboard as heavily as they did on the tables, it too may start to distort schools.","The ministry secretly intends to reintroduce the tables within a few years."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The 'same fate' is the one described just before: a measure that carries consequences becomes a target and stops describing. The condition 'should parents ever come to lean on it as heavily' signals that heavy reliance is what triggers the risk."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-09-2","level":"ch","title":"Why Forgetting Is Not a Failure","topic":"neuroscience of memory","words":481,"passage":"Ask most people what a good memory is and they will describe a kind of vault: things go in, stay put, and come out unchanged on demand. [1] The brain, on the evidence of the last thirty years, is less a vault than an editor, forever revising the manuscript it holds.<br><br>Consider what happens in the hours after learning. A new experience is first held in a fragile form that depends heavily on the hippocampus, a curved structure deep inside the temporal lobe. [2] During this period, and above all during deep, slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus appears to replay the day's patterns to the cortex at high speed, gradually handing over responsibility for the memory. Rats that have learned a maze show, while asleep, the very same sequences of neural firing that they showed while running it, only compressed, as though the recording were being played back on fast-forward.<br><br>What is striking is that this hand-over is never quite complete. Each time a memory is recalled, it seems to return briefly to a fragile state, open to alteration before it is stored once more. [3] The phenomenon, known as reconsolidation, goes a long way towards explaining why an eyewitness can come to remember, in perfect sincerity, details she was only told afterwards, and why a family story retold across three generations drifts so far from the events that gave rise to it.<br><br>Forgetting, too, turns out to be a far more active business than the vault picture allows. [4] Researchers have since identified cells in the hippocampus whose job appears to be exactly this: to weaken connections that are no longer being used, clearing space for the patterns that matter. Far from being a leak in the system, a certain amount of forgetting is the system working as intended.<br><br>None of this means that memory is unreliable in any everyday sense. [5] Rather, it suggests that the vault was always the wrong metaphor. What we carry is not a record of the past but a working model of it, tuned during sleep, revised at every retrieval and pruned continuously; an account that is generally good enough and occasionally, quietly, wrong.<br><br><b>A.</b> Were it merely a matter of decay, old memories would all fade at roughly the same rate, which they plainly do not.<br><b>B.</b> Anyone who has caught a cherished childhood memory in a mistake already knows how misleading that picture can be.<br><b>C.</b> The cortex, by contrast, is thought to hold very little of what it receives for longer than a few seconds.<br><b>D.</b> Over the following nights, however, something remarkable takes place.<br><b>E.</b> Most of what we remember about yesterday is, after all, broadly accurate.<br><b>F.</b> New information encountered at that moment can be folded into the original, so that the next recollection contains both.<br><b>G.</b> Such experiments, however, have so far been carried out only on human volunteers.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'That picture' in B points back to the vault image just described, and the idea that it is 'misleading' sets up the contrast in the next sentence ('less a vault than an editor')."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D introduces 'the following nights', which 'during this period' and the reference to slow-wave sleep then pick up. Piece C also mentions the brain but 'by contrast' has nothing here to contrast with."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"'At that moment' refers to the fragile state on recall, and 'folded into the original' is exactly what the eyewitness example and the family story then illustrate."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A argues that forgetting cannot be simple decay, which is the 'more active business' just announced, and the cells that deliberately weaken connections are then offered as the alternative."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E concedes that everyday memory is 'broadly accurate', which supports 'not unreliable in any everyday sense' and is then qualified by 'Rather, it suggests…'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Memory is a fixed store that only fails when the brain is damaged.","Memory is a constantly revised model rather than a permanent record, and that is how it is meant to work.","Sleep is the only period in which new memories can be formed.","Eyewitness testimony should never be accepted in court."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Every paragraph attacks the 'vault' image: consolidation in sleep, alteration at recall, and active forgetting all show a memory that is edited rather than stored. The eyewitness is one example, not the thesis."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the family story 'retold across three generations'?","options":["Older family members have far more reliable memories than younger ones.","The people who told it were deliberately embellishing it to entertain.","It changed a little at each retelling without anyone intending it to.","The original events were never properly written down."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The story is offered as an example of reconsolidation, in which each recall lets new details be 'folded into the original'. The eyewitness in the same sentence acts 'in perfect sincerity', so the drift is unintended, not dishonest."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'pruned' in the final paragraph is closest in meaning to","options":["concealed","strengthened","copied","trimmed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To <b>prune</b> is to cut back what is unneeded, as a gardener does. The passage has just described cells that weaken unused connections to clear space, so the memory model is being trimmed, not enlarged."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-09-3","level":"ch","title":"Half a House","topic":"architecture","words":484,"passage":"<b>A.</b> On the northern edge of a coastal city stands a row of houses that, at first sight, looks abandoned halfway through construction. Each has a concrete frame, a roof, a staircase, a kitchen and a bathroom, and beside each of these sits an empty void of exactly the same size, open to the sky. Nothing has gone wrong here. The void is the design. It was left, deliberately and at some cost to the architects' reputation, for the families themselves to fill.<br><br><b>B.</b> The reasoning was brutally simple. The public subsidy available for each family would buy either a small, complete house on cheap land an hour from the city, or half of a much better house in the neighbourhood where the families already lived and worked. Rather than build the whole of something poor, the architects chose to build the difficult half of something good: the structure, the plumbing, the party walls and the roof, the parts that a family with limited tools and no engineering training cannot safely construct alone. \"We did not design a house,\" the lead architect said at the time. \"We designed the hard half and got out of the way.\"<br><br><b>C.</b> A decade on, no two houses in the row are alike. Some voids have been filled in brick, some in timber, one in a patchwork of salvaged windows. One family has built outwards to open a small shop; another has added a second storey for a married daughter. Property values along the row have risen steadily, whereas a conventional, fully finished social-housing estate a few streets away has lost value over the same period. Not every outcome is happy, though. A handful of the extensions are poorly built, two families never completed theirs, and the row has lost the visual unity that architects are trained to prize.<br><br><b>D.</b> It was this last point that split the profession. To some, leaving half a house unbuilt was an abdication: handing families a problem and calling it participation. To others, self-building is simply what poorer households have always done, on hillsides and in back yards, and the scheme merely gave that instinct a safe skeleton to grow on. What the argument really turned on was not craftsmanship but the older question of who architecture is for, and whether the architect's vision should outrank the occupant's.<br><br><b>E.</b> The model has since been copied elsewhere, with mixed results. Where funding rules permit it, incremental schemes have flourished; where a subsidy may be spent only on a dwelling certified as complete, the approach is impossible. Yet the row's deepest challenge is not to housing policy but to the idea of completion itself. Buildings are normally at their best on the day they are handed over and decline from there. These houses invert that curve: at hand-over they are at their least finished, and, if their architects are right, at their most useful.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains the financial reasoning behind leaving half of each house unbuilt?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B weighs what the subsidy could buy: a whole poor house far away, or half of a good house where the families already lived."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) mentions a house being adapted for use as a business?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C reports that one family 'built outwards to open a small shop'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a disagreement among architects about the scheme?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D says the point 'split the profession' and sets the two positions against each other."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why the model cannot be adopted in every country?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E states that where a subsidy may only be spent on a dwelling certified as complete, the approach is impossible."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?","options":["To argue that visual unity is the most important quality of a housing estate","To describe the career of a well-known architect","To warn governments against spending public money on self-built housing","To examine a housing scheme that challenges the assumption that a building should be finished at hand-over"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage presents the scheme, its reasoning, its results and the debate it caused, then concludes that its deepest challenge is 'to the idea of completion itself'. Neither the architect's career nor visual unity is the focus."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the comparison with the 'conventional, fully finished social-housing estate' (section C) suggest?","options":["The families in the finished estate were wealthier to begin with.","The finished estate was built on more expensive land.","The freedom to extend and adapt is part of what gave the half-houses their lasting value.","Fully finished housing is always of poorer construction than self-built housing."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer places the rising value of the adaptable row directly against the falling value of a finished estate nearby. The only difference the passage has drawn between them is that one could be extended by its occupants, so that is what the contrast is meant to point to."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word 'abdication' in section D is closest in meaning to","options":["an act of rebellion","the giving up of a responsibility","a bold experiment","a generous gift"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence glosses <b>abdication</b> as 'handing families a problem and calling it participation', so the critics meant that the architects had surrendered a duty that was theirs."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The scheme has been reproduced in other places without any difficulty.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section E says the model has been copied 'with mixed results' and is 'impossible' wherever subsidies must be spent on completed dwellings, so the statement contradicts the text."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-10-1","level":"ch","title":"Traffic in the Twilight Zone","topic":"ocean science","words":531,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Ask most people to picture the deep ocean and they will describe a silent, lightless plain where nothing much happens. It is an understandable picture, and a wrong one. Below about two hundred metres, where sunlight fades to a faint blue and then to nothing, lies the largest habitat on the planet by volume, and it is neither quiet nor still. Marine scientists now describe this so-called twilight zone as a place of constant traffic, and one whose rhythms may matter for the climate of the entire globe.<br><br><b>B.</b> The traffic in question is a daily migration on a scale that dwarfs anything on land. Each evening, uncountable numbers of small fish, squid and shrimp-like creatures rise hundreds of metres towards the surface to feed under cover of darkness; each dawn they sink back into the gloom to hide from predators. Ship-mounted echo sounders first recorded this movement decades ago, when operators noticed a \"false seabed\" on their screens that rose at dusk and fell at daybreak. Only much later did anyone appreciate what the moving layer was made of, or how much carbon it carried down with it.<br><br><b>C.</b> That last point is what has drawn climate researchers to a zone they once ignored. Animals that feed near the surface and then descend are, in effect, a biological pump: the carbon in their food travels down inside their bodies and is released, through breathing and waste, at depths from which it may not return to the atmosphere for centuries. Estimates of how much carbon moves this way vary wildly, and therein lies the problem. Were the figure at the upper end of current guesses, the twilight zone would be storing more carbon each year than every forest on Earth combined; were it at the lower end, it would be a modest contributor at best. Nobody can yet say which.<br><br><b>D.</b> Closing that gap is harder than it sounds. Nets tear the soft-bodied animals apart or let them escape; cameras need light, which frightens off the very creatures they are meant to observe; and sound-based surveys cannot easily tell a shoal of fish from a cloud of jellyfish. One response has been to release fleets of small autonomous floats that drift with the currents for months at a time, recording temperature, oxygen and the faint acoustic signature of life around them. Another has been to listen in a different sense: hydrophones lowered into the darkness have picked up a low, rhythmic hum at dusk that some researchers believe is the collective sound of millions of animals beginning their nightly climb.<br><br><b>E.</b> All of this is unfolding just as commercial interest in the same waters is growing. Several fishing companies have begun to look at the twilight zone's small, oily fish as raw material for fishmeal and nutritional supplements, and a handful of governments have issued exploratory licences. Scientists have not, on the whole, called for a ban. What they have asked for is time: a pause long enough to learn what the zone does for the rest of the planet before anyone starts to remove pieces of it. Whether that request will be heard is, for now, an open question.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains how the daily migration was first detected?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes ship-mounted echo sounders recording a \"false seabed\" that rose at dusk and fell at daybreak — the first record of the movement."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) lists the practical difficulties of observing twilight-zone animals?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D explains why nets, cameras and sound surveys each fail, before describing the floats and hydrophones used instead."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) compares the zone's possible role with that of the world's forests?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says that at the upper end of estimates the zone would store more carbon each year than every forest on Earth combined."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes what scientists have asked decision-makers to do?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E states that scientists have not asked for a ban but for a pause — time to learn what the zone does before it is exploited."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?","options":["To explain how echo sounders and hydrophones work","To prove that the deep ocean absorbs more carbon than forests do","To argue that fishing in the twilight zone should be banned immediately","To show that a poorly understood part of the ocean may matter greatly and is now under pressure"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage moves from the zone's surprising activity, to its uncertain climate role, to the difficulty of studying it, and finally to growing commercial interest. Option D is only one end of an estimate, and the writer says scientists have not called for a ban."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the passage suggest about the timing of the commercial interest described in section E?","options":["Most scientists are opposed to any use of the zone's fish","Companies have deliberately waited for scientists to complete their research","It has arrived before anyone can say what removing the animals would cost the planet","Governments issued licences because research has shown the fishing is safe"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The phrase \"just as\" links growing commercial interest to a moment when the zone's role is still unknown, and scientists want a pause \"long enough to learn\" before removal begins. Nothing suggests companies waited or that research has shown fishing is safe."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section B, the word \"dwarfs\" is closest in meaning to:","options":["is hidden beneath","is far larger than","moves more slowly than","closely resembles"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To \"dwarf\" something is to make it look small by comparison; the migration is on a scale far larger than any on land."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The hum picked up by hydrophones has been proved to come from migrating animals.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says only that \"some researchers believe\" the hum is the sound of animals beginning their climb — a belief, not a proof — so the statement contradicts the text."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-10-2","level":"ch","title":"Every Map Has an Edge","topic":"the history of maps","words":549,"passage":"Five sentences have been removed from the passage. Choose from A–G the one that fits each gap. There are two extra sentences you do not need.<br><br>Long before a map was a tool for getting from one place to another, it was an argument about what the world was. [1] The town where the map was drawn sat at the centre; the edges were reserved for whatever the mapmaker feared, hoped for or had merely heard rumours of. To read such a map as a failed attempt at accuracy is to miss the point entirely. Its makers were not lost; they were explaining.<br><br>The shift towards maps that could actually guide a traveller came not from scholars but from sailors. Charts drawn for the Mediterranean trade in the later Middle Ages were covered in a web of straight lines radiating from compass points, and their coastlines were startlingly precise. [2] A captain who had sailed a stretch of coast a dozen times knew its bays and headlands far better than any professor in a library, and the charts recorded that hard-won knowledge with little decoration and no philosophy.<br><br>For the next few centuries, the two traditions ran side by side and borrowed from each other awkwardly. Printed world maps grew more accurate along the coasts while remaining inventive inland, where the mapmaker's only sources were travellers' tales. [3] It was, after all, easier to sell a map with a mountain range on it than one with an honest blank.<br><br>The blank spaces did not disappear because explorers filled them; they disappeared because a new kind of state demanded it. Governments that wished to tax land, raise armies and build roads needed to know, to within a few metres, where everything was. [4] The resulting surveys took decades, employed thousands, and produced maps that for the first time showed a country as its rulers wished to see it: measured, bounded and owned.<br><br>It is tempting to think of this as the end of the story, with today's satellite imagery as the final, perfect map. Yet the choices have not gone away; they have merely become harder to see. [5] Every map still has a centre and an edge, still decides what deserves a name, and still tells its reader, quietly, what kind of world they are living in.<br><br><b>A.</b> Their accuracy came from a source the universities had never taken seriously: the accumulated experience of working seamen.<br><b>B.</b> A blank space, however, was a poor advertisement, and so cartographers of the period filled it with invented rivers, speculative kingdoms and the occasional monster.<br><b>C.</b> Whoever decides which shops appear when a phone is unlocked, or which border is drawn as a solid line and which as a dotted one, is making exactly the kind of statement the medieval mapmaker made, only with far less candour.<br><b>D.</b> The earliest surviving maps of this kind are less concerned with distance than with meaning.<br><b>E.</b> Their answer was the national survey: teams of engineers with chains and instruments, triangulating from hilltop to hilltop across an entire territory.<br><b>F.</b> Sailors, by contrast, rarely learned to read and had little curiosity about what lay beyond the coast.<br><b>G.</b> The invention of printing made maps cheap enough for ordinary households to own for the first time.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Gap [1] follows the claim that a map was \"an argument about what the world was\" and precedes a description of centre and edges; D's \"less concerned with distance than with meaning\" introduces exactly that idea, and \"of this kind\" refers back to maps as arguments."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence before [2] praises the charts' precise coastlines and the sentence after explains a captain's experience; A's \"Their accuracy came from… working seamen\" bridges the two. F contradicts the paragraph, which credits sailors, not dismisses them."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"After \"inventive inland\", B explains why blanks were filled with invented features, and the next sentence (\"easier to sell a map with a mountain range on it\") continues the selling idea. G mentions printing but says nothing about blanks."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"\"Their answer\" in E responds to the governments' need described just before the gap, and \"The resulting surveys\" after the gap refers back to E's national survey."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Gap [5] follows \"the choices… have merely become harder to see\"; C gives modern examples of such hidden choices (phone maps, borders) and links them to the medieval mapmaker, matching the paragraph's argument."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best expresses the writer's central claim?","options":["Satellite imagery has finally freed maps from the errors of earlier centuries","Maps became truly useful only once governments began to survey their territory","Medieval maps were failures because their makers lacked accurate instruments","Maps have always expressed choices about the world, even when they look purely objective"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The opening calls a map \"an argument\", and the closing paragraph insists the choices \"have not gone away\" even in the satellite era. The writer explicitly rejects reading medieval maps as failed attempts at accuracy."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about today's satellite-based maps?","options":["They conceal their choices more effectively than older maps did","They are less accurate than the national surveys that preceded them","They have made the idea of a map's centre meaningless","They have removed the need for human judgement in mapmaking"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The writer says the choices have \"become harder to see\" and that modern mapmakers act \"with far less candour\" than medieval ones. That is a claim about concealment, not about accuracy or the absence of judgement."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the third paragraph, \"inventive\" is used to mean:","options":["copied from earlier maps","based on imagination rather than evidence","drawn with great technical skill","crowded with too much detail"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The inland areas were \"inventive\" because the mapmaker's only sources were travellers' tales — the features were made up, not measured. The contrast with \"more accurate along the coasts\" confirms this."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-10-3","level":"ch","title":"The Apprentice Problem","topic":"artificial intelligence and work","words":492,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Every profession has a bottom rung, and for most of history that rung has been where the learning happened. The junior lawyer who spends a year checking contracts for a single clause, the trainee radiologist who reads a thousand routine scans before seeing a rare one, the graduate analyst who builds the same spreadsheet a hundred times: none of them enjoys the work, and all of them are being trained by it. The dull tasks at the bottom of a career are not an accident of bad organisation. They are how expertise is passed on.<br><br><b>B.</b> It is precisely these tasks that the current generation of artificial intelligence performs best. Software that summarises documents, drafts routine letters, flags anomalies in images and writes first-pass code is not replacing the expert at the top of the ladder, whose judgement it cannot yet match. It is replacing the apprentice at the bottom, whose work was always somewhat mechanical. Firms that have adopted such tools report exactly what one would expect: fewer junior hires, faster turnaround, and senior staff freed to concentrate on the parts of the job that genuinely require them.<br><br><b>C.</b> What those same firms have been slower to notice is the debt they are running up. If nobody reads the thousand routine scans, nobody develops the eye that spots the thousand-and-first. Had the tools existed a generation ago, the very people now supervising them would never have acquired the judgement that makes their supervision valuable. The problem is not that machines will take the senior jobs; it is that, a decade from now, there may be no one qualified to hold them. Economists have a name for a resource that is consumed faster than it is renewed, and nothing about the label is reassuring.<br><br><b>D.</b> Not everyone accepts this gloomy account. Sceptics point out that every wave of automation has provoked the same alarm, and that the professions have survived the calculator, the search engine and the spreadsheet. Skills, they argue, do not vanish; they migrate. A junior who no longer checks contracts by hand will instead learn to interrogate the machine's checking, to notice where it is confidently wrong, and to take responsibility for a judgement she did not personally form. That, they say, is a higher skill than the one it replaces, not a lower one.<br><br><b>E.</b> Both sides may be right, which is what makes the question so uncomfortable. It is entirely possible that the skill of supervising machines is genuinely valuable and that it cannot be learned without first doing, at least for a while, the work the machines now do. If so, the firms that will thrive are not those that cut the bottom rung fastest, but those that deliberately keep some of the drudgery in place and pay for it, knowing full well that a machine could do it more cheaply. Whether shareholders will tolerate such an arrangement is a question no algorithm can answer.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) presents the view that the writer's concern is exaggerated?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D gives the sceptics' response: every wave of automation has caused the same alarm, and skills migrate rather than vanish."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) suggests that some firms may need to pay for work that a machine could do more cheaply?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E says the firms that thrive may be those that keep some drudgery \"and pay for it, knowing full well that a machine could do it more cheaply\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) uses an imagined past to show what today's senior staff would have lost?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C's inverted conditional — \"Had the tools existed a generation ago, the very people now supervising them would never have acquired the judgement…\" — imagines a different past to make the point."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that tedious work has a purpose beyond the task itself?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A lists dull entry-level tasks and concludes that they are \"how expertise is passed on\", not an accident of bad organisation."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best summarises the passage?","options":["Artificial intelligence will soon replace senior professionals as well as junior ones","Automation removes the tasks through which people used to learn their professions, with consequences nobody can yet be sure of","Sceptics have shown that fears about automation have always been unfounded","Firms should stop hiring junior staff and invest in supervising machines instead"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage sets out the problem (A–C), the sceptical reply (D) and an unresolved conclusion (E). It explicitly says machines cannot yet match senior judgement and does not endorse the sceptics or recommend cutting junior hiring."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply by saying \"nothing about the label is reassuring\" in section C?","options":["The writer regards the firms' current approach as unsustainable","The label refers to the falling number of junior staff being hired","Economists side with the sceptics described in section D","Economists have already found a solution to a similar problem"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The \"resource consumed faster than it is renewed\" is expertise itself; a resource used up faster than it is replaced eventually runs out. The writer never names the label but makes clear the situation cannot continue as it is."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section E, the word \"drudgery\" refers to:","options":["expensive equipment that firms must buy","the legal risks of using machines","the supervision of junior staff by seniors","dull, repetitive work that nobody enjoys"],"correct":3,"explanation":"\"Drudgery\" is hard, tedious work; the passage has just described the routine tasks of the bottom rung, which a machine \"could do more cheaply\"."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer states that current software can already match the judgement of an expert at the top of a profession.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section B says the software is not replacing the expert at the top, \"whose judgement it cannot yet match\" — the opposite of the statement."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-19-1","level":"ch","title":"The Second Life of a Shirt","topic":"the economics of second-hand clothes","words":546,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Wardrobes in wealthy countries now shed clothing at a rate that would have astonished earlier generations. A garment bought in the spring, worn a handful of times and dropped into a charity bin by autumn has not, however, reached the end of its life; it has merely entered a second market, with its own prices, middlemen and peculiar logic. Far from being a sideline to the fashion industry, the trade in used clothing has become a global business worth billions, and it rewards close study precisely because so few of the people who feed it ever think about where their donations go.<br><br><b>B.</b> The first surprise is how little of what is donated is sold where it was given. A charity shop on a European high street can display only a fraction of what arrives at its back door; the rest is sold by the kilo to sorting firms, whose graders flick each item into one of dozens of bales according to type, condition and likely destination. A wool coat may be routed to Eastern Europe, a summer dress to West Africa, a pair of jeans with a fashionable label to a vintage dealer in Tokyo. What looks like charity at one end of the chain is, by the middle, a commodity business in which margins are thin and volume is everything.<br><br><b>C.</b> The second surprise concerns who pays. Bales arriving at a port in Ghana or Kenya are bought sight unseen by traders, who gamble that the contents will contain enough saleable pieces to cover the cost. Open a bale and the gamble is revealed: a few prized items, many ordinary ones and a stubborn residue too worn, too stained or too unsuited to the climate to be sold at all. That residue, which by some estimates amounts to a third of every bale, ends up in landfill or in rivers, so that the environmental burden of overproduction is quietly shifted from the countries that bought the clothes new to those that inherit them used.<br><br><b>D.</b> Defenders of the trade point out, not unreasonably, that it employs enormous numbers of people and clothes many more at prices they can afford. Critics reply that the flood of cheap imports has hollowed out local garment industries that once thrived. Both are right, which is what makes the question so hard to settle. Had the imports never arrived, domestic tailors might have prospered; had they been banned, as several governments have attempted, the traders, porters and repairers who now depend on the trade would have lost their livelihoods overnight.<br><br><b>E.</b> What is clearer is that the whole system rests on a fiction: the belief, held by the donor, that giving a garment away absolves them of responsibility for it. Nothing in the economics supports this. The value of a donated shirt is set not by the generosity of the giver but by what a sorter in one country and a trader in another are willing to pay for it, and when the answer is nothing, the shirt does not vanish. Any serious reckoning with the waste of the fashion industry will therefore have to begin at the point where clothes are made and sold, rather than at the bin where their first owners stop thinking about them.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes how donated clothing is classified and sent to different countries?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes the graders 'flicking each item into one of dozens of bales' and gives the destinations: Eastern Europe, West Africa, Tokyo."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes goods being bought before anyone has examined them?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"In section C the bales 'are bought sight unseen by traders, who gamble' on what they contain."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) accepts that two opposing views are both partly correct?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D sets out the defenders' and the critics' cases and then says 'Both are right, which is what makes the question so hard to settle'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that the waste problem must be tackled at an earlier stage in a garment's life?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E concludes that any reckoning with fashion waste 'will have to begin at the point where clothes are made and sold, rather than at the bin'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main purpose in the passage?","options":["To show that the trade in used clothing is a large, complex business whose true costs are hidden from the people who donate","To argue that governments should ban the import of second-hand garments","To explain why vintage clothing has become fashionable in wealthy countries","To persuade readers to stop giving their old clothes to charity shops"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each section reveals a 'surprise' about the trade (its scale, its routes, who pays) and the last returns to the donor's ignorance of it. The writer never tells readers to stop donating, and treats bans as a two-sided question."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer point out in section A that few donors think about where their clothes go?","options":["To criticise donors for being less generous than earlier generations","To suggest that the trade has escaped scrutiny despite its enormous size","To explain why charity shops receive more clothing than they can sell","To show that the second market is a recent development"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The trade 'rewards close study precisely because' its suppliers never look at it: a billion-dollar business that its own suppliers ignore is one nobody has been examining. Generosity and the shops' capacity are not the point of the sentence."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section D, 'hollowed out' is closest in meaning to","options":["modernised","exposed to view","weakened from within","moved elsewhere"],"correct":2,"explanation":"To hollow something out is to remove its substance while leaving the shell; the local industries 'that once thrived' have been emptied of their strength by cheap imports."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Traders at African ports are able to inspect the contents of a bale before agreeing a price.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section C says the bales are 'bought sight unseen' and that the contents are only revealed once a bale is opened, so the statement contradicts the passage."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-19-2","level":"ch","title":"The Colour That Came Last","topic":"why some languages have no word for blue","words":548,"passage":"Readers of ancient Greek poetry have long been puzzled by its colours. The sea is 'wine-dark', honey is 'green', and the sky is scarcely given a colour at all. [1] The more likely explanation, is that the poets were not seeing differently but naming differently: their language had no word that carved out blue as a category of its own.<br><br>They were not unusual. When researchers compared the colour vocabularies of dozens of unrelated languages, a striking pattern emerged. Every language had terms for dark and light. If it had a third term, that term was red. Yellow and green came next, in either order, and blue arrived only after them, if at all. [2] A language with two colour words would never possess 'blue' but lack 'red'; the sequence ran in one direction only.<br><br>Why blue should be the straggler has several plausible answers, none complete. One points to the scarcity of blue in the world our ancestors handled: there are few blue foods, blue animals or blue stones, and the blue of sky and sea, being neither an object nor a surface, may not have seemed to need a name. [3] Cultures that acquired the means to produce a stable blue pigment tend, it turns out, to have acquired a word for it at roughly the same time, as though the ability to make a colour is what finally fixes it in the language.<br><br>None of this means that speakers of a blue-less language cannot see blue. Their eyes are the same as anyone else's. What differs is how quickly and reliably they sort what they see. In one experiment, speakers of a language that groups blue and green under a single term took measurably longer than English speakers to pick out a blue square from a set of green ones, yet were quicker to spot a subtly different green that English lumps together with its neighbours. [4] The vocabulary does not build the eye, but it does tune the attention.<br><br>The lesson runs the other way too. Russian, unlike English, obliges its speakers to choose between two words for what we call blue, one for the lighter shade and one for the darker, and Russian speakers tell those two shades apart faster than English speakers do. [5] Colour, which feels like the least negotiable of our perceptions, turns out to be partly a matter of the words we have been handed; and the sky, which has been blue for as long as there have been eyes to look at it, waited a surprisingly long time for anyone to say so.<br><br><b>A.</b> Another looks not at nature but at manufacture.<br><b>B.</b> Children, tellingly, learn the names of colours in much the same order.<br><b>C.</b> Some early scholars concluded from this that the Greeks must have suffered from a collective defect of vision, a theory that did not survive long.<br><b>D.</b> Having a word, it seems, does not merely record a distinction; it sharpens it.<br><b>E.</b> The order was not merely common; it was, as far as anyone could tell, without exception.<br><b>F.</b> Greek painters, by contrast, used blue pigments freely, which only deepens the mystery.<br><b>G.</b> The distinction their language insists upon, in other words, is precisely the one their perception has been trained to make.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C offers the theory of a 'defect of vision', which is what 'the more likely explanation' in the next sentence is being compared with. F adds a new puzzle rather than a theory, so 'more likely' would have nothing to answer."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E says the order was 'without exception', which the next sentence illustrates ('would never possess blue but lack red'). B raises children, whom the passage never mentions again."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'One points to…' is followed by 'Another looks…', and 'manufacture' sets up the sentence about cultures that learned to 'produce a stable blue pigment'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"G sums up the experiment ('in other words'): the distinction the language makes is the one perception has been trained on, leading to 'the vocabulary… does tune the attention'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D draws the conclusion from the Russian example: having a word 'sharpens' a distinction, which is why Russian speakers separate the two blues faster."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best summarises the writer's conclusion?","options":["The words a language provides affect how readily its speakers notice colour differences, without changing what their eyes can see","Ancient Greek poets deliberately avoided naming the colour of the sky","Every language eventually develops the same set of colour terms","Languages without a word for blue are spoken by people who cannot see it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fourth paragraph states that eyes are the same and 'what differs is how quickly and reliably they sort what they see'; the final paragraph calls colour 'partly a matter of the words we have been handed'. The Greeks were not deliberately avoiding anything, and the passage says blue arrives 'if at all'."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer mention that there are few blue foods, animals or stones?","options":["To prove that early humans lived far from the sea","To show that blue is a rarer colour than red in nature","To explain why blue pigments were expensive to produce","To suggest that people tend to name only what they need to handle or use"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The list is followed by the remark that sky and sea, 'being neither an object nor a surface, may not have seemed to need a name': the implication is that a colour is named when it belongs to things people deal with. The passage compares nothing with red's frequency and says nothing about the cost of pigment."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the third paragraph, 'the straggler' refers to","options":["the one that appears most frequently","the exception that proves the rule","the one that falls behind the others","the most disputed case"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A straggler is someone who lags behind a group; blue is the colour term that 'arrived only after' the others, so the word describes its late position in the sequence."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-19-3","level":"ch","title":"What Holds a Bridge Up","topic":"the engineering of bridges","words":545,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Ask a child to draw a bridge and you will get a plank laid across a gap. For short spans it is also a correct one: a beam resting on two supports carries a load by bending, its upper surface squeezed and its lower surface stretched. The trouble begins when the gap widens. Double the length of a beam and the bending force at its middle does not double but quadruples, so that a plank which spans a stream will sag hopelessly across a river. Every other bridge form is, in one way or another, an attempt to escape that arithmetic.<br><br><b>B.</b> The arch escapes it by refusing to bend at all. Shape the structure as a curve, and the weight pressing down on its crown is turned into a squeezing force that travels along the arch and into the ground at either end. Stone, which cracks the moment it is stretched but tolerates almost any amount of squeezing, is therefore the ideal arch material, which is why the Romans could build arches that still carry traffic two thousand years later while leaving no stone beam of any length behind them. The price is paid at the ends: the ground must be able to resist the outward thrust, and where it cannot, the arch spreads and falls.<br><br><b>C.</b> The suspension bridge inverts the logic. Instead of pushing the load into the ground, it hangs the roadway from cables draped over tall towers and anchored at either shore, so that the main members are in pure tension. Steel wire, which is extraordinarily strong when pulled, allows such a bridge to leap gaps that no arch could attempt. Yet lightness brings a vulnerability of its own. A deck that weighs little relative to its span can be set swaying by wind, and if the rhythm of the gusts happens to match the natural rhythm of the deck, each push arrives just as the structure is already moving that way, and the motion grows. Engineers learned this watching a newly opened bridge twist itself apart in a wind that a heavier structure would not have noticed.<br><br><b>D.</b> Modern designers therefore spend as much effort taming movement as they do resisting weight. Decks are shaped in wind tunnels so that air flows past them cleanly; heavy pendulums are hung inside towers to swing against any oscillation; and cable-stayed bridges, in which the deck is held by straight cables fanning out from the towers, offer a stiffer alternative for spans too long for a beam and too short to justify a suspension bridge. What has changed is the ability to model, before a single pile is driven, how a structure will respond to every load it is likely to meet.<br><br><b>E.</b> For all that, the forces that eventually bring most bridges down are humbler than wind. Water finds its way into a joint, rusts a bearing, freezes and expands, and does so unremarked for decades, because inspection is unglamorous and budgets favour whatever can be photographed at an opening ceremony. The engineering of bridges, in the end, is not only the art of making a structure stand but the discipline of keeping it standing, and the second of these has always been harder to pay for than the first.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains that the difficulty of spanning a gap grows faster than the gap itself?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A says that doubling a beam's length makes the bending force 'not double but quadruple', and calls this 'that arithmetic'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why a certain material suits one kind of bridge but not another?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B explains that stone 'cracks the moment it is stretched but tolerates almost any amount of squeezing', which makes it ideal for arches but useless for beams."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes measures taken to stop a structure moving dangerously?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D lists wind-tunnel shaping, pendulums inside towers and the stiffer cable-stayed design, all aimed at 'taming movement'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) suggests that bridges are neglected for reasons that have nothing to do with engineering?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E blames unglamorous inspection and budgets that 'favour whatever can be photographed at an opening ceremony'; the obstacle is money and attention, not technical knowledge."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply by mentioning the 'opening ceremony' in section E?","options":["Most bridges fail shortly after they are opened","Ceremonies are an important way of testing a new bridge","Money is more readily spent on building new bridges than on caring for existing ones","Photographs of a bridge are useful evidence for inspectors"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Budgets 'favour whatever can be photographed' at an opening, in contrast to unglamorous inspection: the point is that visible new construction attracts funding while quiet maintenance does not. Nothing is said about ceremonies as tests or about early failure."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section D, 'taming' is closest in meaning to","options":["predicting","encouraging","measuring","bringing under control"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To tame something is to make it manageable; the devices listed (streamlined decks, pendulums, stiffer cables) all exist to restrain movement, not to measure or predict it."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How each bridge form answers the same basic problem, and what threatens a bridge once it is built","Why suspension bridges are the safest type for long spans","The use of computer modelling in modern construction","The reasons the Romans built better bridges than modern engineers"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Sections A–C present beam, arch and suspension bridges as different escapes from 'that arithmetic', D covers movement, and E turns to decay and maintenance. Modelling and the Romans are details within that argument."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Some stone beam bridges built by the Romans are still in use today.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section B says the Romans left 'no stone beam of any length behind them'; it is their arches, not beams, that still carry traffic."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-19-4","level":"ch","title":"A Clock That Nobody Set","topic":"sleep in adolescents","words":557,"passage":"Few domestic arguments are as predictable as the one about bedtime. The parent sees a child exhausted in the morning and wide awake at midnight, and concludes that laziness or the phone is to blame. [1] Around the onset of puberty, the internal clock that governs when we feel sleepy shifts later by as much as two hours, so that a fifteen-year-old told to sleep at ten is, biologically speaking, being asked to do what an adult would find difficult at eight.<br><br>The mechanism is chemical. Melatonin, the hormone that signals to the body that night has come, is released later in adolescents than in either children or adults, and it lingers later into the morning as well. [2] Set against that shift is a school timetable which, in most countries, begins earlier for secondary pupils than for primary ones, precisely the reverse of what the biology would recommend. The result is a population of young people who are chronically short of sleep on weekdays and who attempt to repay the debt with long lie-ins at the weekend, pushing the clock later still.<br><br>The costs are not trivial. Adolescents who regularly sleep for fewer than eight hours show poorer concentration, lower mood and a weaker ability to lay down new memories, an unfortunate profile in a group whose main occupation is learning. [3] What looks like a personality, in other words, may in part be a symptom.<br><br>Screens do play a part, but a subtler one than the usual complaint suggests. The light from a phone held close to the face is bright enough to delay melatonin further, and the content, designed to hold attention, offers no natural stopping point. [4] A teenager scrolling at one in the morning is not the cause of the problem so much as its most visible expression: the device fills a wakefulness that was going to be there anyway.<br><br>Where schools have tried later start times, the findings have been consistent. Pupils sleep longer, attendance rises, and lateness, accidents and reported low mood all fall. [5] Yet the case is strengthened by the very fact that so modest an adjustment can change thousands of lives at once. The argument at the bedroom door, it turns out, has been aimed at the wrong target; the teenager and the parent are on the same side, and the opponent is a clock that neither of them set.<br><br><b>A.</b> Yet studies that remove the devices altogether find that the delay shrinks but does not disappear.<br><b>B.</b> Caffeine, by contrast, has been shown to have a much smaller effect on teenagers than on adults.<br><b>C.</b> Nor is this a small effect confined to a few individuals; it has been recorded across cultures and appears in other mammals as well.<br><b>D.</b> Both may be part of the story, but neither is the whole of it, and the larger cause is one that no amount of nagging can address.<br><b>E.</b> Adults who work night shifts report a similar pattern of weekend recovery, though for entirely different reasons.<br><b>F.</b> Objections, predictably, come from those who must reorganise transport, sports fixtures and parents' working days around the new hours.<br><b>G.</b> Irritability, risk-taking and a tendency to doze off in the first lesson are routinely put down to adolescence itself when they are, at least partly, effects of too little sleep.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Both' in D refers to laziness and the phone, and 'the larger cause' introduces the shift in the internal clock described in the next sentence."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C insists the melatonin effect is real and widespread, so that 'Set against that shift' in the following sentence has a firmly established shift to refer to. E introduces adult night workers, whom the passage never returns to."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"G lists behaviours wrongly 'put down to adolescence itself', which is what 'What looks like a personality… may in part be a symptom' then sums up."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A's 'Yet' concedes that removing screens does not remove the delay, which leads directly to the claim that the phone is 'not the cause… so much as its most visible expression'. B's point about caffeine is never developed."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F raises the objections to later start times, and the next sentence answers them with 'Yet the case is strengthened…'."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer suggest with the closing phrase 'a clock that neither of them set'?","options":["Alarm clocks are ineffective for adolescents","Parents should allow teenagers to choose their own bedtime","The real cause of the conflict lies outside the control of both parent and child","Teenagers deliberately stay up late to annoy their parents"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The image places the 'opponent' outside both people: a biological timing that neither chose. The passage argues for later school hours, not for letting teenagers decide bedtimes, and says nothing about deliberate defiance."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the third paragraph, 'lay down' new memories means","options":["forget","form and store","write out","rest from"],"correct":1,"explanation":"'Lay down' here is the collocation used for memory formation: the brain records and stores what has been learned. The context, 'a group whose main occupation is learning', confirms it."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Schools that start later have recorded improved examination results.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph lists longer sleep, higher attendance and fewer accidents and low moods, but says nothing about examination results either way."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-20-1","level":"ch","title":"Borrowed Letters: How the Alphabet Travelled","topic":"the history of the alphabet","words":529,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Consider the letter you are reading now. Its shape was settled long before any language you speak existed, and its journey to this page passed through the hands of people who would not have recognised a single word of English. Most writing systems that humans have devised record meanings or syllables; the alphabet, by contrast, records sounds so small that they cannot be pronounced on their own. That was a strange idea, and it appears to have been invented only once.<br><br><b>B.</b> The earliest trace of it lies in a set of scratched marks found on rock faces and mining tools in a desert region, dating to roughly four thousand years ago. Whoever made them was not a professional scribe. Scribes of that era were trained in an elaborate script of hundreds of pictorial signs, and they had every reason to keep it complicated, since their livelihood depended on the rarity of the skill. The desert marks instead borrowed a couple of dozen of those pictures and used each one to stand for the first sound of its name. A drawing of a house no longer meant \"house\"; it meant the consonant with which the word for house began. Not until the trick had been simplified in this way could a labourer, rather than a court official, hope to learn to write.<br><br><b>C.</b> The system travelled north with traders, and it is to those traders that the word \"alphabet\" ultimately owes its first two syllables. Being merchants rather than priests, they cared about speed, and under their pens the pictures were worn down to abstract strokes, much as a coin loses its detail through handling. Crucially, they wrote only consonants. A reader supplied the vowels from context, which works well enough for a language whose vowels are largely predictable, and rather badly for one whose are not.<br><br><b>D.</b> Herein lay the problem for the next borrowers, who spoke a language in which vowels carried real weight. Their solution was less a stroke of genius than a happy accident. Several of the inherited letters stood for throat sounds that did not exist in the new language and so were, in effect, unemployed. Rather than discard them, the adopters reassigned them to vowels. Had those sounds happened to exist in the borrowing language, we might still be writing without an A or an E. It was this modified set that later peoples inherited, turned in another direction, gave new names and, over several centuries, carried across a continent.<br><br><b>E.</b> What is striking, looking back over the whole story, is how little of it was designed. No committee sat down to invent a writing system for the masses. Each step was taken by people solving an immediate, local problem: how to mark a tool, how to record a cargo, how to write a name that the borrowed letters could not spell. The alphabet's efficiency, so often praised, is the residue of thousands of such small acts of laziness and improvisation. The one deliberate decision in its history, that of the desert miners who first let a picture stand for a sound, was made by people whose names no letter ever recorded.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why professional scribes had no interest in making writing simpler?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B says the scribes 'had every reason to keep it complicated, since their livelihood depended on the rarity of the skill'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes letters changing shape as a result of frequent use?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C: under the traders' pens 'the pictures were worn down to abstract strokes, much as a coin loses its detail through handling'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a feature of the alphabet that arose because certain letters had nothing to do?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D: letters for throat sounds absent from the new language were 'in effect, unemployed', so the adopters 'reassigned them to vowels'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that the alphabet was not the product of planning?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E: 'how little of it was designed' and 'No committee sat down to invent a writing system for the masses'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best sums up the writer's view of the alphabet's history?","options":["It was a series of practical shortcuts by ordinary people rather than a deliberate invention.","It was the achievement of a small class of highly trained scribes.","It developed separately in several regions before the versions merged.","It has changed so much that its origins can no longer be traced."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage repeatedly stresses accident and improvisation: labourers, traders in a hurry, a 'happy accident' with unused letters, and 'small acts of laziness and improvisation'."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the spoken language of the traders in section C?","options":["It had no vowel sounds at all.","Its vowels could usually be worked out from the surrounding consonants.","It contained more consonants than any later language.","It was used mainly for religious ceremonies."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The traders wrote only consonants, and the writer says this 'works well enough for a language whose vowels are largely predictable' — since they used it successfully, their language must have been of that type. The passage never says they lacked vowels."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section E, the word 'residue' is closest in meaning to:","options":["a reward given for hard work","a mistake that was later corrected","what is left behind after a process","a formal record kept for the future"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The alphabet's efficiency is described as the 'residue' — what remains — of thousands of small acts, not something anyone aimed at."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The people who introduced vowel letters set out deliberately to improve the writing system.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section D calls their solution 'less a stroke of genius than a happy accident': they simply reused letters that had no sound to represent."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-20-2","level":"ch","title":"Hives Above the Traffic","topic":"urban beekeeping","words":540,"passage":"For most of the last century, anyone who wished to keep bees was assumed to need a field, or at the very least a large garden well away from the neighbours. [1] Hives now sit on hotel roofs, in school courtyards and behind the wheelie bins of terraced houses, and city councils that once banned them compete to advertise how many they host.<br><br>The appeal is easy to understand. Bees ask for little space, they are quiet, and they produce something you can spread on toast. There is also a widespread belief that the city is, against all expectation, a rather good place for them. Suburban gardens, railway embankments and municipal parks offer a variety of flowers that a field of a single crop cannot match, and the plants in them are far less likely to have been sprayed. [2] Several studies have found that colonies on urban rooftops come through the winter at least as well as their rural cousins, and sometimes better.<br><br>Yet the story has a complication that the brochures rarely mention. The honeybee is a livestock animal, and like any livestock it competes for food. [3] Each of those colonies sends out tens of thousands of foragers, and every flower they strip is one that a wild bee cannot visit. Since wild bees, of which most temperate countries have several hundred species, neither live in hives nor make honey that anyone harvests, nobody counts them, and their decline goes unnoticed until it is well advanced.<br><br>The arithmetic is unforgiving. Ecologists who have measured the nectar available in a typical city district estimate that it can support a certain density of colonies before the foragers begin to go hungry. [4] In several capitals that ceiling has already been passed several times over, with the predictable result that honey yields per hive are falling even as the number of hives climbs.<br><br>None of this means that the rooftop hive is a mistake. It means that its benefit has been misdescribed. [5] What it does is bring the people who install it into daily contact with the question of what is flowering, where, and for how long, and that question, followed to its conclusion, leads not to more hives but to more flowers. The beekeepers who understand this tend, after a season or two, to spend less on equipment and more on seed. Their neighbours' balconies fill with lavender and thyme; the wild bees, whom nobody invited, come back to feed. The hive, in the end, was never the point. It was the excuse.<br><br><b>A.</b> A single city block may now house a dozen hives where a generation ago it held none.<br><b>B.</b> Whatever good it does is done not by the bees but by the beekeeper.<br><b>C.</b> That assumption has collapsed in the space of a couple of decades.<br><b>D.</b> Rural beekeepers, by contrast, have always relied on a single crop for most of their yield.<br><b>E.</b> That figure is low, and it does not rise simply because a new hive has been installed.<br><b>F.</b> Cities are also a degree or two warmer, so something is in bloom for more of the year.<br><b>G.</b> Most of these species are protected by law, and harming them can carry a fine.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"'That assumption' picks up the belief that beekeeping needs a field, and its collapse leads straight into the list of unlikely places hives now sit."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"'Cities are also…' adds a further advantage (warmth, longer flowering) to the list of reasons the city suits bees, before the studies that confirm it. D contrasts rural keepers but the paragraph is about why cities are good, not about rural yields."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'Each of those colonies' needs a preceding mention of many colonies in one place, which A supplies with 'a dozen hives' on one block."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"'That figure' refers to the estimated density of colonies a district can support, and 'that ceiling' in the next sentence refers back to E's fixed limit."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B explains how the benefit has been 'misdescribed' — it belongs to the beekeeper, not the bees — and 'What it does is bring the people…' then develops that idea."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's central claim about rooftop beekeeping?","options":["Its real value lies in what it leads people to do rather than in the bees themselves.","It should be banned in cities where wild bees are declining.","It produces more honey than rural beekeeping because city flowers are unsprayed.","It has been oversold by councils but is harmless to other species."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The final paragraph argues that the hive's benefit 'has been misdescribed': it matters because it turns its owner into someone who plants flowers — 'The hive… was never the point. It was the excuse.'"},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer suggest about the falling honey yields in overcrowded cities?","options":["They prove that urban bees are less healthy than rural ones.","They are mainly caused by pesticides used in municipal parks.","They will recover once councils stop advertising their hives.","They were foreseeable by anyone who had grasped the limit on available food."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer calls the fall 'the predictable result' of passing the ceiling 'several times over': given fixed nectar and more hives, the outcome follows from 'unforgiving' arithmetic. The passage says urban bees overwinter as well as or better than rural ones, so B is contradicted."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase 'The arithmetic is unforgiving' means that the calculation:","options":["changes from one season to the next","has rarely been published in full","leaves no room for argument or exception","is too difficult for most beekeepers to follow"],"correct":2,"explanation":"'Unforgiving' describes something harsh that allows no escape; the nectar limit is fixed and exceeding it has an inevitable consequence."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-20-3","level":"ch","title":"The Long Road from Laboratory to Arm","topic":"how vaccines are tested","words":539,"passage":"<b>A.</b> A vaccine that reaches a clinic has already survived a process designed, at every stage, to kill it. This is not a figure of speech. The overwhelming majority of candidates that enter testing never leave it, and the ones that do have usually spent the better part of a decade being challenged, doubted and measured. The public tends to hear only about the final months, when a result is announced and a decision made. What precedes that announcement is far less visible and far more important.<br><br><b>B.</b> Before a candidate goes anywhere near a human being, it must earn the right to do so in the laboratory and in animals. Researchers want to know two things: whether it provokes the immune system to respond at all, and whether that response comes at an unacceptable cost. A candidate that produces a splendid antibody count in mice but leaves them visibly unwell will go no further, however promising the count. Only when both questions have been answered to a regulator's satisfaction is permission given to move on, and the permission is narrow: it covers a small number of volunteers, a single dose, and nothing else.<br><br><b>C.</b> Those first volunteers, rarely more than a few dozen, are healthy adults, and the question asked of them is not whether the vaccine works but whether it is safe enough to keep asking. Doses are increased slowly, one small group at a time, so that any problem reveals itself at the lowest level that causes it. Seldom does anything dramatic happen at this stage, precisely because the animal work has already filtered out the worst candidates; what emerges instead is a picture of the sore arms, mild fevers and headaches that are the price of provoking an immune system on purpose.<br><br><b>D.</b> The decisive stage is the one that involves thousands of people and takes the longest. Half receive the candidate and half a placebo, and neither the volunteers nor the doctors examining them know which is which until the trial is over. The reason for this secrecy is not distrust of anyone's honesty. It is that people who believe themselves protected behave differently, and doctors who know which patient got the real thing may, without meaning to, judge a cough more leniently in one group than the other. Then the trial waits. Nothing can be concluded until enough participants have, in the ordinary course of their lives, encountered the disease, and the only way to shorten that wait is to run the trial where the disease is common. A vaccine tested during a lull in infections may take years to produce an answer; the same vaccine tested during an outbreak may produce one in months, with no corner cut.<br><br><b>E.</b> Approval is not the end. Rare side effects, by definition, cannot show themselves among a few thousand people, so every vaccine in use is watched across the millions who receive it, and a signal that appears at that scale can pause a programme within days. Critics sometimes cite such pauses as evidence that the testing failed. The opposite is true. A system that could not detect an event affecting one person in a hundred thousand would be a system that had stopped looking.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why doctors, as well as volunteers, must not know who received the vaccine?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D: doctors who know 'may, without meaning to, judge a cough more leniently in one group than the other'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives a reason why a candidate with impressive results might still be rejected?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B: a candidate with 'a splendid antibody count in mice' that leaves them unwell 'will go no further, however promising the count'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) corrects a mistaken interpretation of a programme being halted?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E: critics cite pauses as evidence of failure, but 'The opposite is true' — a pause shows the system is still looking."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes the minor discomforts that early testing is expected to reveal?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C lists 'the sore arms, mild fevers and headaches that are the price of provoking an immune system on purpose'."},{"kind":"purpose","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the writer contrast a trial run during a lull with one run during an outbreak?","options":["To explain why some vaccines are approved without a placebo group.","To suggest that outbreaks make results less reliable.","To show that a trial can be fast without any step being skipped.","To argue that trials should never be run while a disease is spreading."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The point of the contrast is the final phrase: an outbreak trial 'may produce one in months, with no corner cut' — speed comes from how often participants meet the disease, not from lower standards."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the public's understanding of how vaccines are tested?","options":["It has improved since results began to be announced publicly.","It is deliberately kept incomplete by regulators.","It concentrates too heavily on animal experiments.","It is shaped by the shortest and least significant part of the process."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Section A says the public 'tends to hear only about the final months', while 'what precedes that announcement is far less visible and far more important' — so their picture is built on the least important stage."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section D, 'judge a cough more leniently' means to judge it:","options":["less strictly","more quickly","more carefully","less often"],"correct":0,"explanation":"'Leniently' means with less severity; a doctor who expects a patient to be protected may treat a symptom as less serious than it is."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Most of the candidates that fail do so during the animal stage.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says most candidates fail somewhere, and that animal work filters out 'the worst', but it never says at which stage the majority are lost."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-20-4","level":"ch","title":"Who Does the Car Decide to Hit?","topic":"the ethics of self-driving cars","words":581,"passage":"Ask a room of engineers what troubles them most about a car that drives itself and they will talk about sensors in fog, or software that has never seen a cow. Ask a room of philosophers and you will hear a different worry altogether. [1] Should it swerve into a wall and sacrifice its passenger, or hold its course and strike the people in the road? Versions of this dilemma have filled lecture halls and newspaper columns for a decade, and surveys have found, with tedious consistency, that most people want other people's cars to sacrifice their passengers while wanting their own car to protect them.<br><br>The engineers, for their part, find the question faintly ridiculous. [2] A car that finds itself choosing between two groups of victims has already failed, several seconds earlier, to do the one thing it was built to do, which is to notice the situation developing and slow down. Real crashes are rarely a clean choice between two evils; they are a muddle of partial information, in which the most valuable decision is almost always the dull one of braking sooner.<br><br>Yet the philosophers are not wrong that something has changed, even if they have located it in the wrong place. [3] When a human driver swerves, the choice is made in a fraction of a second by someone who will have to live with it. When a self-driving car swerves, the choice was made months earlier, in an office, by people who will never meet anyone involved, and it was made not once but for every car of that model, in every city, for as long as the software runs. What was a reflex has become a policy.<br><br>That shift raises questions that no lecture-hall dilemma captures. [4] The company will say the choice was made by the software; the programmers will say they followed a specification; the specification will turn out to have been written to satisfy a regulation whose authors were thinking about something else entirely. Responsibility, which once sat squarely in the driver's seat, has been distributed so widely that nobody appears to be holding any of it.<br><br>There is, too, a subtler difficulty. Every self-driving system is trained on records of how humans drive, and humans drive unequally. [5] A system that learns from such data will reproduce the pattern faithfully and at scale, and it will do so without any of the human driver's capacity to be embarrassed about it. Had the technology been introduced with these questions already answered, the debate might have been a short one. Instead, the questions were asked only once the cars were on the road, and the answers are being written, line by line, in code that very few people are permitted to read.<br><br><b>A.</b> It assumes, they point out, a machine that has been paying no attention until the last possible moment.<br><b>B.</b> If such a car strikes a pedestrian, who, exactly, is to blame?<br><b>C.</b> Most passengers, in practice, never read the terms they agree to when they enter the vehicle.<br><b>D.</b> Suppose, they say, that the brakes fail and a collision can no longer be avoided.<br><b>E.</b> Insurance companies have so far declined to comment on any of these scenarios.<br><b>F.</b> The novelty lies not in the decision itself but in when, where and by whom it is taken.<br><b>G.</b> Drivers slow down more readily in some neighbourhoods than in others, and give more room to some pedestrians than to others.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D introduces the philosophers' scenario ('they say… the brakes fail'), and the next sentence asks what the car should then do."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A gives the engineers' objection ('they point out'): the dilemma assumes an inattentive machine, which the following sentence develops — such a car 'has already failed… to notice the situation developing'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F names what has really changed — 'when, where and by whom' the decision is taken — and the paragraph then contrasts a driver's split-second choice with one made 'months earlier, in an office'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B poses the question of blame, which the next sentences answer with the company, the programmers and the regulation each passing it on. E mentions insurers, whom the paragraph never returns to."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"G gives concrete examples of humans driving 'unequally', and 'such data' in the next sentence refers to records of that behaviour."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the writer's main argument?","options":["Self-driving cars should not be permitted until the public agrees on whom they should protect.","Philosophers understand the technology better than the people who build it.","The real ethical problem is not the crash dilemma but the way decisions and responsibility have been relocated.","Engineers have solved the ethical problems that philosophers still worry about."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer sets aside the swerve dilemma ('located it in the wrong place') and argues that the change lies in reflex becoming policy, responsibility being scattered, and bias being copied into code."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the survey results mentioned in the first paragraph?","options":["They were conducted mainly among engineers.","They reveal more about people's self-interest than about ethics.","They show that most people would refuse to travel in a self-driving car.","They have shifted noticeably over the past decade."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The phrase 'with tedious consistency' and the finding that people want other cars to sacrifice passengers but their own to protect them show the writer regards the surveys as exposing self-interest, not moral insight."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer believes the engineers' objection settles the ethical debate.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer reports the engineers' view but immediately adds that 'the philosophers are not wrong that something has changed', and spends the rest of the passage on what that is."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-21-1","level":"ch","title":"The Crowd Within","topic":"the microbiome","words":525,"passage":"<b>A.</b> It is a humbling thought that the body you call your own is, by cell count, only about half human. The remainder is a shifting population of bacteria, fungi and viruses, the great majority of them crowded into the large intestine, and together they are known as the microbiome. For most of medical history these residents were regarded, when they were regarded at all, as passengers at best and stowaways at worst. Only in the last two decades, as the cost of reading genetic material has collapsed, have researchers been able to take a census of the crowd, and what they have found has unsettled some long-held assumptions about where the boundary of a person lies.<br><br><b>B.</b> Far from being idle, the gut's inhabitants earn their keep. They break down fibres that human enzymes cannot touch, manufacture vitamins the body does not make for itself, and train the immune system to tell a genuine threat from a harmless visitor. Some of the chemicals they release travel along the nerve that links the gut to the brain, which is why a growing number of scientists suspect that mood, appetite and even the quality of sleep are partly negotiated in the intestine. Not until a colony is disturbed does its contribution become obvious: a single course of broad-spectrum antibiotics can thin the population dramatically, and it may take months for the original balance to reassert itself, if it ever does.<br><br><b>C.</b> Where does this internal ecosystem come from? Nobody is born with one. A newborn acquires its first residents during birth and in the weeks that follow, from the parents' skin, from milk, from the household and, later, from food and from the soil under its fingernails. The pattern laid down in those early months appears remarkably persistent, which has led some researchers to argue that the first thousand days of life represent a window during which the microbiome is unusually open to influence, for better or worse.<br><br><b>D.</b> Inevitably, so intriguing a story has attracted people eager to sell it. Shelves now groan under supplements promising to \"restore\" gut health, and diets are marketed with the confidence of a science that is, in truth, still in its infancy. The difficulty is that a healthy microbiome has never been defined. Two perfectly well people may carry communities that barely overlap, and a microbe that is benign in one host may be troublesome in another. Most of the striking results so far come from mice, whose guts and diets bear only a loose resemblance to ours, and the handful of human trials have been small, short and hard to reproduce.<br><br><b>E.</b> None of this makes the field a fraud; it makes it young. The sensible conclusion is not that we should ignore the crowd within, but that we should be wary of anyone claiming to command it. What the evidence does support is unglamorous: a varied diet rich in plants, fewer needless antibiotics, and a certain tolerance for dirt. Had our great-grandparents been told that the secret of good health was to eat vegetables and go outside, they would hardly have needed a laboratory to believe it.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains how a person first comes to have a microbiome?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C answers its own opening question, 'Where does this internal ecosystem come from?', by describing how a newborn acquires its first residents from birth, milk and the household."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) warns readers against commercial claims about gut health?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D describes 'people eager to sell' the story, supplements that promise to 'restore' gut health, and diets marketed with more confidence than the science justifies."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) lists specific jobs that the gut's inhabitants perform for the body?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B says the microbes 'earn their keep' and lists what they do: breaking down fibres, manufacturing vitamins and training the immune system."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that the weaknesses of the research show it is immature rather than dishonest?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E opens with 'None of this makes the field a fraud; it makes it young', which is exactly the contrast between dishonesty and immaturity."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best states the writer's overall position?","options":["Antibiotics have done more damage to human health than any other medicine.","Research on mice has proved that gut bacteria determine mood and sleep.","A healthy microbiome can be restored with the right supplements and diet.","The microbiome is genuinely important, but claims about controlling it run far ahead of the evidence."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Sections A–C establish that the microbes matter; sections D–E insist the science is young and that anyone 'claiming to command it' should be treated warily. The other options are either overstated or contradicted in section D."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer's remark about great-grandparents at the end of section E suggest?","options":["Laboratory research into the microbiome is a waste of money.","Vegetables grown in the past contained more vitamins than modern ones.","The most reliable advice the science currently offers is something people already accepted long ago.","Earlier generations enjoyed better health than people do today."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer calls the supported advice 'unglamorous' and then notes that great-grandparents 'would hardly have needed a laboratory to believe it' — the point being that the science confirms old common sense rather than revealing anything new. Nothing is said about past health or past vegetables, and section E explicitly defends the field."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section D, the phrase 'groan under' is closest in meaning to","options":["collapse beneath","are heavily loaded with","are emptied of","complain loudly about"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Shelves that 'groan under' supplements are so full that they seem to creak with the weight — an idiom for being heavily loaded. The literal 'complain' and 'collapse' do not fit shelves in a shop."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"According to the passage, the first thousand days of life are the only period during which the microbiome can be changed.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section C calls the first thousand days a window in which the microbiome is 'unusually open to influence', not the only such period, and section B shows that a course of antibiotics can change it much later in life."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-21-2","level":"ch","title":"The Vanishing Hand","topic":"the decline of handwriting","words":498,"passage":"There is a drawer in most family homes that nobody opens very often. [1] Read one today and the striking thing is not what it says but how it looks: the slope of the letters, the pressure of the pen, the small flourish under a signature. A page in a familiar hand carries its writer into the room in a way that no typed message can.<br><br>That such pages are becoming rarer is beyond dispute. Surveys across several countries find that a majority of adults have not written anything longer than a shopping list in the past month, and a substantial minority cannot recall the last time they wrote a full paragraph by hand. [2] Their essays are typed, their notes are photographed, and the cursive script that their grandparents laboured over is, in more than one education system, no longer taught at all.<br><br>To some, the trend is nothing to mourn. Handwriting, they point out, was never the point; it was merely the technology available for getting thoughts onto a surface, and technologies are replaced. Nobody weeps for the quill. [3] What matters, on this view, is that ideas are recorded and shared, and the keyboard does that faster, more legibly and for more people than the pen ever did.<br><br>Yet a body of research has begun to complicate this cheerful account. Children who learn their letters by forming them, rather than by pressing keys, appear to recognise those letters sooner and to read more fluently. Students who take notes by hand tend to remember more of a lecture than those who type, and not, as one might guess, because they write more down. [4] The slowness of the pen, in other words, is not a defect to be engineered away but the very mechanism by which the mind is made to work on what it hears.<br><br>None of this will bring the letter-writing drawer back into daily use, nor should it. [5] The sensible course is to treat handwriting as we treat mental arithmetic: a skill we no longer depend on for everyday business, but one whose disappearance would leave something in the mind a little weaker than before.<br><br><b>A.</b> It is there that the letters are kept — from a grandmother, from a friend abroad, from a parent who is no longer alive.<br><b>B.</b> Among the young, the figures are starker still.<br><b>C.</b> It is precisely because they cannot keep up that they are forced to select, compress and rephrase, and it is this processing that fixes the material in memory.<br><b>D.</b> Printing, the argument runs, was greeted with the same alarm, and it hardly ruined civilisation.<br><b>E.</b> Few of them, it turned out, could name a single letter they had received in the past year.<br><b>F.</b> The keyboard, after all, is here to stay, and a child who cannot type fluently is at a real disadvantage.<br><b>G.</b> Doctors, whose handwriting was once a byword for illegibility, have been the first to welcome the change.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The sentence before gives figures for adults; 'Among the young, the figures are starker still' moves to a new group, and the next sentence's 'their essays', 'their notes' and 'their grandparents' can only refer to the young. E looks tempting but leaves 'their grandparents' without a sensible referent."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Nobody weeps for the quill' is one example of a replaced technology; D adds printing as a second, and 'the argument runs' keeps us inside the view of the people who think the trend is 'nothing to mourn'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The sentence before denies that hand-writers remember more 'because they write more down'; C supplies the real reason — they cannot keep up, so they must process what they hear — and the following sentence sums this up as 'the slowness of the pen'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F explains why the drawer should not return to daily use — the keyboard 'is here to stay' — and its 'after all' justifies the 'nor should it' just before. The next sentence then proposes the balanced 'sensible course'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the main point the writer makes about handwriting?","options":["Schools should stop teaching typing until children can write cursive script.","Letters written by hand are more emotionally powerful than typed messages.","It should be kept alive as a skill because of what it does for the mind, even though it is no longer needed for daily tasks.","It is a dying technology that deserves no more sympathy than the quill."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph states the position directly: handwriting is like mental arithmetic, no longer depended on but worth keeping. The quill view is the one the writer 'complicates', and the emotional power of letters is only the opening illustration."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about those who say the decline of handwriting is 'nothing to mourn'?","options":["They have a financial interest in the sale of keyboards.","They secretly regret the loss of handwriting themselves.","They have misread the research on note-taking.","Their argument is coherent but does not tell the whole story."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer presents their view fairly and calls it a 'cheerful account' that research 'has begun to complicate' — that is, it is not wrong so much as incomplete. Since the research is described as new, they cannot be accused of misreading it, and no motive is attributed to them."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 2, 'laboured over' is closest in meaning to","options":["worked hard at","complained about","quickly abandoned","quietly admired"],"correct":0,"explanation":"To labour over something is to put slow, effortful work into it; the grandparents spent hours practising cursive script. The contrast is with the young, whose essays are simply typed."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Cursive script is still taught in every education system the passage refers to.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says cursive script is, 'in more than one education system, no longer taught at all', which directly contradicts the statement."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-21-3","level":"ch","title":"The Pale Line on the Rock","topic":"glacier retreat","words":507,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Anyone who has stood at the foot of a mountain glacier will have noticed the line. Above it the rock is dark, lichen-covered and weathered; below it, pale and raw, as if scrubbed. That line marks how high the ice reached within living memory, and in most valleys of the world it now sits a dizzying distance above the ice itself. Glaciers have advanced and retreated throughout the planet's history, but what the pale rock records is a retreat of a speed and scale that the geological archive struggles to match.<br><br><b>B.</b> A glacier is best understood not as a frozen object but as a slow river with a budget. Snow that falls on its upper reaches is compressed into ice and flows downhill under its own weight; at the lower end, the ice melts. So long as the two roughly balance, the glacier holds its ground. Tip the balance — a little less snow in winter, a few more warm days in summer — and the front begins to withdraw. What makes the process so hard to reverse is that a shrinking glacier exposes darker ground, which absorbs more heat, which speeds the melting further. The ice, in effect, helps to write its own obituary.<br><br><b>C.</b> Nowhere are the consequences felt more directly than in the mountain communities that have long treated glaciers as reservoirs. In the dry months, meltwater is what keeps rivers flowing, fields irrigated and turbines turning. Paradoxically, retreat brings a temporary abundance: for a few decades, a shrinking glacier releases more water than it stores, and the valleys below enjoy a windfall. Hydrologists call the moment when that flow begins to fall \"peak water\", and for many ranges it has either arrived or is not far off. Whatever comes after it will have to be managed by people who have never known scarcity.<br><br><b>D.</b> The retreat also leaves behind hazards of a kind that few communities are prepared for. Meltwater pools behind walls of loose rubble left by the ice, forming lakes that can burst without warning and send a wall of water down a valley in minutes. Slopes that were held together by ice for millennia are freed to slide. Several countries have begun draining the most dangerous of these lakes, an expensive and delicate business, but the number of them grows faster than the budgets set aside to deal with them.<br><br><b>E.</b> It is tempting to look at all this and ask how the ice can be saved. The honest answer is that, for most small glaciers, it cannot; the warming already locked into the system will see them gone within a lifetime whatever is done now. What remains within reach is more modest and more urgent: securing the water that the ice once secured, moving the villages that stand in the path of the lakes and, above all, ensuring that the great ice sheets of the far north and south, whose fate depends on decisions not yet taken, do not follow the same pale line down the rock.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why the retreat of a glacier tends to speed up once it has begun?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes the feedback loop: a shrinking glacier 'exposes darker ground, which absorbs more heat, which speeds the melting further'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a benefit of glacier retreat that will not last?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C calls the extra meltwater a 'temporary abundance' and a 'windfall' lasting 'a few decades', after which the flow begins to fall."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) mentions a visible sign of how far the ice has already withdrawn?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A describes the pale line on the rock that 'marks how high the ice reached within living memory' and now sits far above the ice."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) states that some of the loss can no longer be prevented?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E says that most small glaciers cannot be saved, because 'the warming already locked into the system will see them gone within a lifetime whatever is done now'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","options":["Draining glacial lakes is the most effective way to protect villages from flooding.","Glacier retreat is fast, largely irreversible for small glaciers, and its human consequences must now be managed rather than prevented.","Mountain communities have benefited from glacier retreat and should welcome the extra water.","Glaciers have always advanced and retreated, so the current retreat is nothing unusual."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage moves from the scale of the retreat (A), through why it accelerates (B) and what it does to water supply and safety (C, D), to the conclusion that saving small glaciers is impossible and managing the consequences is what remains (E). Option 3 is contradicted in section A; the others are single details."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply with the words 'people who have never known scarcity' at the end of section C?","options":["The communities will be poorly prepared for the shortage because recent decades of abundance have hidden the problem.","The mountain communities are wealthier than those in the lowlands.","The coming shortage will turn out to be milder than the hydrologists predict.","Younger people manage water more carefully than their parents did."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Section C has just explained that retreat brings a temporary windfall; the closing sentence points out that those who must manage the decline have only ever known that windfall. The implication is a lack of experience and preparation, not wealth or skill."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section C, the word 'windfall' is closest in meaning to","options":["a sudden storm","a serious shortage","a long-term investment","an unexpected gain"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A windfall is a piece of good fortune that arrives without effort; here it is the extra water released by the shrinking glacier. The context — 'temporary abundance' — rules out shortage or investment."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer believes that the great polar ice sheets are already beyond saving.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section E says the fate of the ice sheets 'depends on decisions not yet taken' and urges that they be prevented from following the small glaciers — so the writer regards them as still within reach, unlike most small glaciers."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-21-4","level":"ch","title":"Sixty-Four Squares","topic":"the origins of chess","words":551,"passage":"Few games can claim to be played, in something close to their present form, on every inhabited continent. Chess can, and the ease with which a player from one country sits down opposite a stranger from another disguises how long and improbable the game's journey has been. [1] The board it was played on had sixty-four squares, all of one colour, and the pieces on it were not a king and queen but a rajah and his adviser, flanked by elephants, horses, chariots and foot soldiers — an army, in short, of the kind that fought on the plains of the region.<br><br>From there the game travelled west along the trade routes, and with each border it crossed, it was quietly translated. [2] The elephant, an animal few of them had seen, was reimagined first as a bishop's mitre and, in some places, as a jester or a ship. The chariot, its purpose forgotten, became a tower. Only the horse, which needed no explanation anywhere, kept its shape.<br><br>The most consequential change of all came late and, as far as anyone can tell, in a single generation. [3] Almost overnight, a slow game of manoeuvre became one of ambush and sudden collapse, and the manuals written under the old rules were rendered obsolete. Historians still debate what prompted the change; that it coincided with the reign of several powerful queens in Europe has struck more than one of them as unlikely to be an accident.<br><br>[4] Coffee-house players of the eighteenth century would have recognised the board their descendants play on, but not the clocks, the written notation or the ranking systems that now surround it. The game as an organised sport, with its championships and its ratings, is a creation of the last hundred and fifty years, younger than the railway.<br><br>What survives across all these transformations is the underlying grammar: two equal forces, no dice, no hidden information and no luck beyond the order in which one's opponent makes mistakes. [5] That, more than any piece or rule, may be why a game invented to model one kind of warfare has outlived every army it was meant to represent.<br><br><b>A.</b> The adviser, a modest piece that could shuffle only a single diagonal step, was transformed into the queen, the most powerful piece on the board, able to sweep its whole length and breadth.<br><b>B.</b> Persian players, who received it first, kept the general shape but renamed the pieces in their own tongue; the Arabs, who took it from them, carried it as far as Spain; and the Europeans who inherited it there set about redrawing the pieces to match a world of castles and clergy.<br><b>C.</b> Its earliest traceable ancestor emerged in northern India some fourteen centuries ago, under a name that meant \"four divisions of the army\".<br><b>D.</b> Whoever plays it is stripped of every excuse, and the appeal of a contest in which the only variable is the quality of one's own thinking appears to be very nearly universal.<br><b>E.</b> The Vikings, by contrast, took the game north and appear to have played it largely for money.<br><b>F.</b> Nor did the modern game arrive all at once, even after the rules had settled.<br><b>G.</b> The board itself, meanwhile, has never once been altered in all that time.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gap must introduce where the 'journey' began; C names the origin and the old name, and the next sentence's 'the board it was played on' and 'rajah', 'elephants' and 'chariots' all follow naturally from an Indian ancestor."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B spells out the westward route and ends with Europeans 'redrawing the pieces'; the next sentence, 'an animal few of them had seen', needs those Europeans as its referent. E mentions a direction but breaks the chain and has no 'them' to supply."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The paragraph promises 'the most consequential change of all' and later mentions 'powerful queens'; A describes the adviser becoming the queen, which explains why a slow game became one 'of ambush and sudden collapse'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D comments on the 'grammar' just described — no dice, no hidden information, no luck — by noting that the player 'is stripped of every excuse', and the following 'That … may be why' refers back to this universal appeal. G contradicts paragraph 1, which says the board was once all one colour."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How chess changed as it travelled across cultures, and what has stayed constant beneath those changes.","Why the queen is the most powerful piece on the chessboard.","The role of powerful European queens in the history of the game.","How chess became an organised sport with clocks and ratings."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each paragraph follows a stage of the game's journey and its transformations, and the final paragraph names what 'survives across all these transformations'. The queen, the European monarchs and the sporting apparatus are individual stages, not the whole."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer suggest about the link between the queen's new powers and the powerful queens of Europe?","options":["The change was actually made in Asia rather than in Europe.","It is a plausible explanation that has not been proved.","It has been rejected by every serious historian.","The monarchs themselves ordered the rules to be changed."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer says historians 'still debate' the cause, and only that the coincidence 'has struck more than one of them as unlikely to be an accident' — a cautious suggestion, not a settled fact. Nothing supports the monarchs giving orders or an Asian origin for this particular change."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In paragraph 3, 'rendered obsolete' is closest in meaning to","options":["sold cheaply","written down","made useless","translated again"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Something obsolete is out of date and no longer of use; manuals describing the old rules could not help anyone once the rules had changed. 'Rendered' here simply means 'made'."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The chariot was turned into a tower because European players understood its role in battle.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the chariot changed 'its purpose forgotten' — the opposite of players understanding its role."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-22-1","level":"ch","title":"The Myth of the Mindless Mob","topic":"crowd behaviour","words":542,"passage":"<b>A.</b> For more than a century the received wisdom about crowds ran roughly as follows: put enough people together, take away their names, and something ancient rises to the surface. Individuals who would blush at a raised voice in an office become, in a stadium or a square, capable of anything. The image is a seductive one — it flatters the reader, who is never part of the crowd in question — and it has shaped how police are trained, how stadiums are built and how newspapers report a disturbance. It is also, according to most researchers who now study the subject, largely wrong.<br><br><b>B.</b> The evidence against it began to accumulate when investigators stopped relying on eyewitness accounts and started counting. Analysis of footage from emergencies — a fire in a nightclub, a crush at a station, an evacuation after an explosion — reveals a pattern that is almost the opposite of panic. Far from trampling one another, people tend to slow down, look for those they arrived with, and help strangers who have fallen. Deaths, when they occur, are seldom caused by a stampede in any meaningful sense; they are caused by too many bodies being funnelled through too small an exit, a failure of architecture rather than of character. Had the crowd behaved as the old theory predicts, the toll in such incidents would typically have been higher, not lower.<br><br><b>C.</b> What, then, explains the disturbances that undeniably do happen? The most persuasive account holds that people in a crowd do not lose their identity so much as exchange it. A supporter who walks into a ground as an accountant walks out of it as a member of something larger, and the norms of that larger thing — not a hidden animal self — govern what he does next. Those norms can be generous or vicious, but they are norms all the same, and they are surprisingly sensitive to how the crowd is treated. Treat a peaceful gathering as a threat, and the gathering has a way of becoming one, since the bystanders who would otherwise have restrained the hotheads now share their grievance.<br><br><b>D.</b> This has practical consequences that cut against instinct. Officers who patrol in pairs, talk to people and remove their helmets tend to see fewer arrests than those who form a line and wait. Festival organisers who post clear, honest information about delays report calmer queues than those who say nothing for fear of \"alarming\" anyone. The common thread is that the crowd is treated as a collection of adults capable of judgement, which, on the evidence, is exactly what it is.<br><br><b>E.</b> None of this makes crowds safe, and no serious researcher claims it does. Density remains dangerous whatever the mood: above a certain number of bodies per square metre, physics takes over and intentions become irrelevant. The lesson is narrower and, in a way, more demanding. If the crowd is not a monster, then its worst moments cannot be blamed on some collective madness; they must be traced to decisions — about gates, about information, about tone — that somebody made. That is a less dramatic story than the mob of legend. It is also one in which the outcome can be changed.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why the traditional view of crowds has remained so attractive?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A calls the old image \"seductive\" and says it <b>flatters the reader</b>, who never imagines being part of the crowd described."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that fatalities in emergencies are usually the result of design rather than behaviour?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B says deaths are caused by bodies \"funnelled through too small an exit, a failure of architecture rather than of character\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes how the way a crowd is handled can turn a calm gathering hostile?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C: \"Treat a peaceful gathering as a threat, and the gathering has a way of becoming one\", because bystanders come to share the grievance."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) acknowledges a danger that no change of attitude can remove?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E says density \"remains dangerous whatever the mood\": beyond a certain number of people per square metre, \"physics takes over\"."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","options":["Eyewitness accounts of emergencies are generally more reliable than video footage","Crowds are far more dangerous than the public realises, and only better architecture can protect them","Crowds are not irrational mobs; their behaviour reflects shared norms and the way they are treated, so their worst moments can be prevented","Police forces have abandoned the old theory of crowds but festival organisers have not yet followed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Every section builds the same case: the \"mindless mob\" is a myth, crowd behaviour is governed by norms and by treatment (C, D), and so its outcomes are the result of decisions that can be changed (E)."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the author's use of the phrase \"some collective madness\" in section E suggest about their attitude?","options":["They believe it accurately describes a small minority of crowds","They think it is a term used only by police officers and journalists","They consider it the main cause of dangerous density in crowds","They regard it as a convenient explanation that lets the people who made decisions escape responsibility"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts blaming \"collective madness\" with tracing events to \"decisions that somebody made\"; the word \"some\" is dismissive, and the point is that the mob story lets decision-makers off the hook. Nothing in the passage says the madness is real for any crowd."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section D, the phrase \"cut against instinct\" is closest in meaning to","options":["contradict what people would naturally expect","reduce the need for training","are difficult to measure","are dangerous to carry out"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The consequences that follow — fewer arrests when officers remove helmets, calmer queues when delays are announced — are the opposite of what instinct would suggest; \"cut against\" means to go contrary to."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"According to the passage, most deaths in crowd emergencies are caused by people running over one another.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"False. Section B states that deaths \"are seldom caused by a stampede in any meaningful sense\" and blames exits that are too small for the number of people."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-22-2","level":"ch","title":"Borrowed from the Air","topic":"the physics of bird flight","words":550,"passage":"A bird in level flight is doing something that looks effortless and is anything but. Every second it is airborne, its weight pulls it towards the ground, and all that prevents this is the shape of its wing and the speed of the air across it. [1] Both features deflect the air downwards as it passes, and by Newton's third law, air pushed down means a wing pushed up. That upward push is lift, and when it equals the bird's weight, the bird neither rises nor falls.<br><br>Lift is never free. Deflecting air costs energy, and the cost shows up as drag, a backward force that grows sharply with speed. [2] Somewhere between the two lies a speed at which the total effort is lowest, and most birds, left to themselves, cruise close to it. A pigeon does not know the equation, but it descends from millions of generations in which those that flew inefficiently starved.<br><br>Flapping complicates the picture, because a flapping wing is a propeller and a wing at once. On the downstroke the wing is driven forwards and down, tilting the lift so that part of it points ahead and pulls the bird along. [3] Small birds make this cycle dozens of times a second; a heron completes it in a leisurely one.<br><br>The wingtips are where the most ingenious engineering is found. Because the pressure beneath a wing is higher than the pressure above it, air leaks around the tip from the underside to the top, curling into a vortex that trails behind the bird and wastes energy. [4] Aircraft designers, having reached the same conclusion eighty years later, now fit upturned \"winglets\" to airliners for exactly the same reason.<br><br>Then there is the question of size, which is why there are no sparrows the size of eagles. As an animal grows, its weight increases with the cube of its length while its wing area increases only with the square. [5] Beyond a certain point the sums no longer work, which is why the largest birds borrow their lift from rising air rather than manufacturing it with muscle: the condor and the albatross flap rarely and soar constantly. The very smallest, meanwhile, live in a world where the air feels thick and treacly, and the hummingbird's whirring hover is less like an eagle's flight than like a bee's.<br><br><b>A.</b> Large soaring birds counter this by spreading their outermost feathers apart like fingers, breaking the single large vortex into several small ones that cost far less.<br><b>B.</b> Unlike a bat's wing, feathers can be replaced one at a time when damaged.<br><b>C.</b> A bird therefore faces a trade-off that any cyclist would recognise: go faster and the air resists you more; go slower and you must work harder to stay up at all.<br><b>D.</b> A bird twice as long is therefore eight times as heavy but has only four times as much wing to hold it up.<br><b>E.</b> Most migrating birds travel at night, when the air is cooler and predators fewer.<br><b>F.</b> A wing is curved on top and flatter underneath, and it meets the oncoming air at a slight upward angle.<br><b>G.</b> On the upstroke the wing is partly folded and twisted so that it slips through the air with as little resistance as possible.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"The gap follows \"the shape of its wing\" and precedes \"<b>Both features</b> deflect the air downwards\"; F names the two features — the curved profile and the upward angle."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The next sentence says \"Somewhere between <b>the two</b> lies a speed\"; C supplies the two extremes (faster with more drag, slower with more effort) that the trade-off sits between."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"The paragraph has just described the <b>downstroke</b>; G completes the cycle with the upstroke, and the following sentence refers to \"this cycle\"."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A begins \"counter <b>this</b>\", referring to the wasteful wingtip vortex just described, and it gives the birds' solution that aircraft designers later copied with winglets."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D turns the cube-and-square rule of the previous sentence into a concrete example (twice as long, eight times as heavy, four times the wing), leading into \"the sums no longer work\"."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The reasons large birds are more efficient fliers than small ones","Why aircraft designers have always copied birds","The physical principles that allow birds to fly and the limits those principles impose","How different species of bird evolved different wing shapes"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage moves through lift, drag, flapping, wingtip vortices and the scaling problem of size: the physics of flight and its constraints. Aircraft and species differences are examples, not the theme."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the author mention that aircraft designers reached their conclusion \"eighty years later\"?","options":["To show that winglets were invented before scientists understood birds","To suggest that evolution had arrived at a solution long before human engineering did","To explain why airliners are less efficient than large birds","To criticise engineers for ignoring biology"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The phrase follows a description of the \"ingenious engineering\" of feathered wingtips; saying humans reached \"the same conclusion\" much later quietly ranks nature ahead of the engineers. Nothing in the passage attacks engineers or compares aircraft efficiency."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the final paragraph, the word \"treacly\" is closest in meaning to","options":["thick and sticky","warm and rising","fast-moving","thin and cold"],"correct":0,"explanation":"\"Thick and treacly\" describes how the air feels to a very small creature; treacle is a thick syrup, and the comparison with a bee reinforces the idea of moving through something resistant."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-22-3","level":"ch","title":"Where the City's Dinner Goes","topic":"food waste in cities","words":517,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Ask where a city's food waste comes from and most people picture the back door of a restaurant: sacks of peelings, unsold bread, plates scraped into a bin. Restaurants do waste food, but surveys in city after city tell a more uncomfortable story. In wealthy urban areas, more than half of the food that is thrown away is thrown away at home, and most of it was perfectly edible when it went into the bin. The problem is not a few careless businesses. It is millions of kitchens, each discarding a small amount for reasons that seem, at the time, entirely reasonable.<br><br><b>B.</b> Those reasons are worth taking seriously, because a solution that ignores them will fail. Households buy more than they need because shops reward it, and because a fridge that looks full feels like security. They cook more than they eat because a small portion feels mean. They discard food that is still good because the date on the packet, which was printed to protect the manufacturer as much as the customer, told them to. And they do all this in cities that have made the bin the easiest option: a lid at the end of the kitchen, emptied by somebody else, with nothing to remind anyone what went in.<br><br><b>C.</b> The obvious answer, urged by campaigners for decades, is to make people feel worse about it. Posters, school lessons and figures about the tonnage wasted each year have been tried in every large city, and the evidence on their effect is thin. Guilt, it turns out, is a poor instrument: it is felt strongly at the moment the poster is read and hardly at all at the moment the bin is opened. What moves the numbers is friction. When one European city introduced smaller general-waste bins alongside free food-waste caddies, the amount of edible food thrown away fell by nearly a third within a year, not because residents had been persuaded of anything but because the wrong bin had become slightly more trouble than the right one.<br><br><b>D.</b> Further along the chain, the picture changes. Supermarkets discard produce not because it has spoiled but because it has stopped looking the way shoppers expect: a bent cucumber, an apple with a blemish, a loaf baked four hours ago. Here the waste is a business decision rather than a habit, and business decisions respond to prices. Where cities have taxed the disposal of edible food while making donation to charities cheap and legally safe, shops have discovered a sudden enthusiasm for giving it away. Nobody's conscience was awakened; the arithmetic simply changed.<br><br><b>E.</b> What unites the successful schemes is that none of them relied on people becoming better than they are. They accepted that a tired parent at nine in the evening will do whatever is easiest, and that a store manager will do whatever is cheapest, and they arranged matters so that the easy and the cheap were also the right thing. It is a less inspiring approach than the posters, and it lacks their moral glow. It has the single advantage of working.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) challenges a popular assumption about who produces most of a city's food waste?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A opens with the picture most people have (the restaurant back door) and then shows that more than half of the waste comes from homes."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) suggests that the design of the home itself makes wasting food easy?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B ends by describing the bin as \"the easiest option: a lid at the end of the kitchen, emptied by somebody else, with nothing to remind anyone what went in\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why a widely used method of reducing waste has had little effect?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says posters and lessons have been tried everywhere with thin results because guilt is felt when the poster is read, not when the bin is opened."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that shops throw food away for reasons that have nothing to do with its safety?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D: supermarkets discard produce \"not because it has spoiled but because it has stopped looking the way shoppers expect\"."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best summarises the writer's argument?","options":["Waste falls when cities make the right choice the easiest or cheapest one, rather than when they appeal to conscience","European cities have solved the food-waste problem by taxing supermarkets","Food waste will only fall when citizens are properly educated about its cost to the planet","Restaurants and supermarkets, not households, are the real source of urban food waste"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Sections C, D and E all make the same point: guilt fails, while changes to convenience (bins) and cost (taxes, cheap donation) succeed because they do not require people to become better than they are."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the \"moral glow\" of the poster campaigns in section E?","options":["It is a quality that supermarkets lack but households possess","It is what makes such campaigns appealing to their supporters, even though it is not what reduces waste","It is the main reason the campaigns have succeeded in some cities","It has been deliberately copied by the designers of the bin schemes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer concedes the practical schemes are \"less inspiring\" and lack the posters' glow, then adds that they have \"the single advantage of working\" — implying the glow is attractive to people but beside the point. The passage never says the posters succeeded anywhere."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section C, the word \"friction\" is used to mean","options":["a strong emotional reaction","disagreement between neighbours","a small practical obstacle that makes an action less convenient","heat produced when surfaces rub together"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The example that follows explains it: waste fell because \"the wrong bin had become slightly more trouble than the right one\" — a minor inconvenience, not an argument or an emotion."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The European city described in section C charged its residents for the food-waste caddies it introduced.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"False. The passage says the city introduced smaller general-waste bins \"alongside <b>free</b> food-waste caddies\"."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-22-4","level":"ch","title":"In Defence of the Empty Hour","topic":"the philosophy of boredom","words":550,"passage":"Boredom has a poor reputation among the emotions. Fear warns us, anger protects us, grief honours what we have lost; boredom, by contrast, seems to do nothing but complain. [1] For most of history it was barely discussed, and when it was, it was as a moral failing — the sin of the monk who could not bring himself to pray. Only in the last two centuries has it been treated as something that might deserve an explanation rather than a rebuke.<br><br>The philosophers who took it seriously reached an unsettling conclusion. Boredom, they suggested, is not the absence of stimulation but the presence of a question that stimulation normally hides. [2] The child who groans \"there's nothing to do\" is wrong about the facts — there is a great deal to do — and right about something deeper: none of it, at that moment, seems to matter. Seen this way, boredom is less like hunger than like doubt.<br><br>If that is so, then the modern cure may be worse than the disease. We now carry in our pockets a device whose entire purpose is to ensure that the question never arises. [3] Never before has it been so easy to avoid being bored, and rarely has so much been said about how bored everyone is. The two facts are not in tension. An appetite fed constantly with sweets does not go away; it simply loses the ability to recognise a meal.<br><br>There is a further cost, and it is a creative one. Studies that give people a dull task before a test of imagination have found, fairly consistently, that the dull task helps. [4] Writers and mathematicians have long reported the same thing in plainer language: the idea arrives in the shower, on the walk, in the waiting room — anywhere the mind has been left unattended for a while.<br><br>None of this is a recommendation to seek boredom out, any more than to seek out doubt. [5] The claim is more modest. Boredom is a signal, and like any signal it can be silenced or heeded. Silence it, and it returns in a form harder to name — as restlessness, as a vague dissatisfaction with a life that by every visible measure is full. Heed it, and it asks a question that is worth an honest answer: not \"what can I do?\" but \"what is worth doing?\" The empty hour is not a problem to be solved. It is, on occasion, the only room in the house where that question can be heard.<br><br><b>A.</b> The moment a queue forms or a conversation pauses, a screen fills the gap before the gap can be noticed.<br><b>B.</b> Both are uncomfortable, and both, endured for too long, curdle into something closer to despair.<br><b>C.</b> This is why many thinkers have argued that happiness is impossible without leisure.<br><b>D.</b> It is the feeling of a mind that wants to be occupied and cannot find anything worth occupying it.<br><b>E.</b> When nothing demands our attention, we are left alone with the matter of what, if anything, is worth attending to.<br><b>F.</b> The most bored people, surveys suggest, are those with the most demanding jobs.<br><b>G.</b> Deprived of anything interesting outside itself, the mind begins to generate interest from within, wandering, combining, rehearsing possibilities.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The paragraph has just said boredom \"seems to do nothing but complain\"; D defines what that complaint is, and the next sentence (\"For most of history <b>it</b> was barely discussed\") refers back to the feeling."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"The previous sentence says stimulation hides \"a question\"; E states the question (what is worth attending to), and the child's complaint that follows illustrates it."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A describes the pocket \"device\" in action — a screen filling every pause — and leads into \"Never before has it been so easy to avoid being bored\"."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"G explains <b>why</b> the dull task helps: the mind starts producing its own interest. The next sentence says writers have \"reported the same thing\", which is this wandering of the unattended mind."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The previous sentence pairs boredom with doubt; B's \"<b>Both</b> are uncomfortable\" picks up that pair, and \"The claim is more modest\" then follows naturally."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best captures the writer's central claim?","options":["Boredom is a signal worth attending to, because it raises a question that constant distraction prevents us from hearing","People should deliberately make themselves bored in order to become more creative","Philosophers have always regarded boredom as the most important of the emotions","Boredom is a moral weakness that modern technology has finally cured"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage reframes boredom as \"the presence of a question\", warns that phones silence it, and concludes that it should be \"heeded\". The final paragraph explicitly denies that we should seek boredom out."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer mean by saying that the two facts \"are not in tension\"?","options":["Surveys about boredom are unreliable because people exaggerate","People who complain of boredom are usually the ones without phones","Being bored and avoiding boredom are two ways of describing the same experience","Constant distraction is the very reason boredom is now so widely complained of, not evidence against the argument"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sweets analogy that follows makes the link: an appetite fed constantly with distraction \"loses the ability to recognise a meal\", so the ease of avoiding boredom and the volume of complaint about it are cause and effect rather than a contradiction."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In piece B, the phrase \"curdle into\" is closest in meaning to","options":["become stronger and healthier","be mixed together with","gradually turn into something worse","suddenly disappear"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Milk curdles when it goes off; the metaphor describes an uncomfortable state deteriorating, \"endured for too long\", into despair."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-23-1","level":"ch","title":"Rescuing the Silent Screen","topic":"restoring old films","words":507,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Somewhere in a climate-controlled vault outside Amsterdam sits a reel of film that nobody has watched for ninety years. Its cellulose nitrate base has begun to weep a sticky amber residue, and the smell — vinegar, sweet and sharp — tells the archivist what she already suspects: left alone for another decade, the reel would turn into a solid, unspoolable brick. Nitrate stock, used for almost every film made before 1951, is not merely fragile; it is chemically unstable and, given a little heat, spectacularly flammable. Not until the 1930s did studios treat it as anything other than a disposable commercial product, and by then a large share of the silent era had already been melted down for the silver it contained.<br><br><b>B.</b> Restoration begins, unglamorously, with a search. A single title may survive only as scattered fragments: a reel in Prague, a duplicate print in Buenos Aires, a censor's copy in Tokyo with twelve minutes missing. Each must be located, compared frame by frame and ranked according to its quality and its closeness to the original release. Restorers speak of \"elements\" rather than \"copies\", because no two are alike; the version screened in one country might have carried different intertitles, a different ending, or a colour tint that the domestic release never had.<br><br><b>C.</b> Once the best elements are assembled, the physical work starts. Torn perforations are mended by hand under a magnifier. Shrunken film, which may have lost as much as two per cent of its length, is fed through a specially built scanner whose sprockets can be adjusted to fit. Every frame is then digitised at a resolution far higher than any cinema will ever display, so that the rescue itself does not become a fresh source of loss. What emerges is not a film but a mountain of data — several terabytes for a feature of modest length.<br><br><b>D.</b> Here the ethical questions crowd in. Software can remove scratches, steady a wobbling image and erase the flicker that audiences of 1925 took for granted. Had the technology existed then, would the director have used it? Nobody knows, and that is precisely the difficulty. A restoration that removes every trace of age risks producing something the original audience never saw; one that removes nothing leaves the work barely watchable. Most archives now aim for a middle path: mend what is damage, keep what is design. A splice made by the editor stays; a splice made by a careless projectionist goes.<br><br><b>E.</b> The final irony is that the restored digital file is itself less durable than the nitrate it replaces. File formats fall out of use, hard drives fail without warning, and the cost of migrating a large collection every few years is one that few institutions can meet. Several major archives have therefore begun printing their restorations back onto film — polyester this time, stable for centuries — and locking the reels away in the cold. The medium once dismissed as disposable turns out to be the safest place to keep the past.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why a restorer may have to choose between surviving versions that differ from one another?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes the scattered 'elements' of a title, which must be ranked, and notes that versions screened in different countries could carry different intertitles, endings or tinting."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a decision about which imperfections should be left in place?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D sets out the 'middle path': mend what is damage, keep what is design — the editor's splice stays while the projectionist's goes."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) mentions a reason why many early films were deliberately destroyed?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A says a large share of the silent era 'had already been melted down for the silver it contained' — destruction for profit, not by accident."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) points out that a modern solution has a weakness the old material did not?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E calls it the 'final irony' that the digital file is less durable than nitrate, because formats and drives fail and migration is expensive."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","options":["Digital technology has finally solved the problem of preserving early cinema","Restoring an old film demands both technical care and difficult judgement, and even the result is not safe","Nitrate film should never have been used because it is dangerous and unstable","The most important task for archives is to find every surviving copy of a film"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage moves from the search and the physical repair (skill) through the ethical questions (judgement) to the irony that the digital result is itself fragile. Options A, C and D each pick up one section only, and A contradicts section E."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply in section A by saying that studios did not treat nitrate as anything other than a disposable commercial product until the 1930s?","options":["Attitudes, as much as the material itself, were responsible for the early losses","Films made after the 1930s are of no interest to restorers","Studios were forced by law to change how they stored film","The chemistry of nitrate was not understood until that decade"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The sentence links the studios' <b>attitude</b> to the melting-down that followed: films were lost because nobody thought them worth keeping, not only because nitrate decays. Nothing is said about laws or about later films being uninteresting."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The phrase \"crowd in\" in section D is closest in meaning to:","options":["are quietly set aside","are answered one by one","lose their importance","become impossible to ignore"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Questions that 'crowd in' press forward all at once and demand attention — the section then lists several of them and admits 'nobody knows'. They are neither set aside nor answered."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Films are digitised at a very high resolution so that cinemas can project them at that resolution.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section C says the resolution is 'far higher than any cinema will ever display'; the purpose is to stop the rescue itself becoming a source of loss, not to serve projection."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-23-2","level":"ch","title":"The Unwritten Rules of the Line","topic":"the sociology of queues","words":544,"passage":"A queue looks like the simplest of social arrangements: people arrive, they wait, and they are served in the order they came. Yet sociologists who have watched lines — outside bakeries, at bus stops, in hospital corridors — describe something closer to a miniature legal system, complete with rights, offences and punishments, none of which is written down anywhere. [1] What makes this remarkable is that the rules are enforced almost entirely by people who have no authority whatsoever over one another.<br><br>The central principle is that time already spent waiting is a form of property. [2] It is this sense of ownership that explains the fury directed at a queue-jumper, out of all proportion to the minute or two actually lost. The offender has not merely delayed the others; he has, in their eyes, stolen something from them.<br><br>Not every departure from strict order is treated as theft, however. A person who leaves briefly and returns to the same spot is usually tolerated, provided a neighbour has been told. A parent who steps forward to join a child near the front attracts frowns but rarely open protest. [3] Observers have noted that the acceptable size of such a group varies from one country to another, and even from one kind of queue to another: what passes without comment at a cinema would cause an outcry at a petrol station during a shortage.<br><br>Punishment, when it comes, is graduated with surprising precision. It begins with a look, moves to a pointed remark addressed to nobody in particular, and only then becomes a direct challenge. [4] Rarely does anyone call for an official; the queue polices itself, and it prefers to do so quietly, since a loud confrontation costs everybody the calm they were relying on to get through the wait.<br><br>Perhaps the most telling finding concerns what happens when a queue is made invisible. Ticket-number systems and virtual waiting lists remove the physical line, and with it much of the tension — but also much of the solidarity. People no longer glare at latecomers, but nor do they hold a place for a stranger, chat to pass the time or warn a newcomer that the counter is about to close. [5] Whether that trade is worth making is a question the designers seldom stop to ask.<br><br><i>Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There are two extra sentences you do not need to use.</i><br><br><b>A.</b> The rule of thumb seems to be that joining a companion is forgivable so long as the number of people who benefit stays small.<br><b>B.</b> What the system gains in fairness, in other words, it pays for in fellow-feeling.<br><b>C.</b> Bakeries, for that reason, are busiest early in the morning.<br><b>D.</b> Newcomers absorb these conventions within seconds of arriving, and children as young as four can be seen enforcing them.<br><b>E.</b> The escalation is collective: a second speaker is emboldened by the first, and a jumper who shrugs off one remark seldom withstands three.<br><b>F.</b> The first written regulations for orderly queuing were introduced by department stores in the nineteenth century.<br><b>G.</b> The longer someone has stood in line, the more strongly they feel that their place belongs to them and cannot be taken away.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap follows rules that are 'not written down anywhere'; D's 'these conventions' picks them up, and its image of four-year-olds enforcing them sets up the next sentence about enforcement by people with no authority. F contradicts 'none of which is written down'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"The paragraph states that waiting time is 'a form of property'; G explains how that feeling grows with time, and the cleft sentence 'It is <b>this sense of ownership</b> that…' refers straight back to it."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"After the example of a parent joining a child, A gives the general rule about joining a companion, and the next sentence's 'the acceptable size of <b>such a group</b>' depends on A's 'number of people who benefit'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"The paragraph describes punishment rising step by step; E explains that this escalation is a group effort, which leads naturally into 'Rarely does anyone call for an official; the queue polices itself'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paragraph weighs less tension against lost solidarity; B sums that up as a gain in fairness paid for in fellow-feeling, and the final sentence calls it 'that trade'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why queue-jumping is more common in some countries than in others","How queues are governed by unspoken rules that the people waiting enforce among themselves","The advantages of ticket-number systems over physical queues","The history of queuing as a social custom"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Every paragraph develops the idea of the queue as a self-policing 'miniature legal system'. National differences and ticket systems are details within that argument, and no history is given."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the final sentence suggest about the designers of virtual queuing systems?","options":["They consulted sociologists before building the systems","They deliberately set out to reduce conversation between strangers","They have not recognised that their systems remove something of value","They believe that fairness matters less than speed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Designers 'seldom stop to ask' whether the trade is worth making — the implication is that they have not noticed there is a trade at all, not that they chose it on purpose. Nothing supports options A, C or D."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"graduated\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to:","options":["delayed until the end of the wait","measured in minutes","handed out by qualified people","arranged in stages of increasing force"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentences that follow describe a sequence — a look, then a remark, then a direct challenge — so 'graduated' means arranged in steps of rising severity, not connected with qualifications or timing."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-23-3","level":"ch","title":"Reading the Shaking Earth","topic":"how earthquakes are measured","words":504,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Ask most people how big an earthquake was and they will answer with a single number \"on the Richter scale\". The phrase has outlived the thing it describes. Charles Richter's scale, devised in the 1930s for the earthquakes of southern California, was calibrated for one type of instrument at one range of distances, and it saturates: above roughly magnitude 7 it stops distinguishing between a large earthquake and an enormous one, much as a bathroom scale would give the same reading for a car and a lorry. Seismologists abandoned it decades ago, yet the name lives on in headlines, largely because \"moment magnitude\" is a mouthful nobody wants to say.<br><br><b>B.</b> Moment magnitude, the measure now in universal use, is built not on how far a needle swung but on physics. It combines the area of the fault that slipped, the distance it moved and the rigidity of the rock, yielding a figure for the total energy released. Because the scale is logarithmic, each whole step represents roughly thirty-two times more energy, so that a magnitude 8 is not \"twice as strong\" as a magnitude 4 but about a million times more energetic. Had this been widely understood, a great deal of confused reporting might have been avoided.<br><br><b>C.</b> Magnitude, however, tells you what happened at the source, not what happened to you. For that there is a second, older family of scales — intensity scales — which describe effects at a particular place: whether dishes rattled, whether chimneys fell, whether people were thrown from their beds. Intensity is reported in Roman numerals precisely so that nobody confuses it with magnitude. A moderate earthquake directly beneath a city can produce a higher intensity there than a far larger one out at sea, and it is intensity, not magnitude, that engineers consult when they decide how strong a building must be.<br><br><b>D.</b> Neither number could be produced without a global network of seismometers, instruments so sensitive that a large earthquake on one side of the planet is recorded on the other within twenty minutes. Because the waves travel at different speeds through different materials, the pattern of their arrival lets analysts locate the source in three dimensions and say something about the rocks in between. It is from such records, rather than from any direct observation, that almost everything known about the Earth's interior has been deduced; the liquid outer core, for instance, was inferred from the shadow it casts in the arriving waves.<br><br><b>E.</b> What the instruments still cannot do is tell anyone in advance. Early-warning systems exist, but they work by detecting the first, faster waves and racing an alert ahead of the slower, more damaging ones — a margin of seconds, not days. No sooner had one such system given schoolchildren twenty seconds' notice than commentators demanded to know why it had not given twenty hours. The honest answer is that forecasting where stress will next be released remains beyond the science, and those who claim otherwise are usually selling something.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why one measure is written with a different kind of number from another?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says intensity is reported in Roman numerals 'precisely so that nobody confuses it with magnitude'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives an example of a comparison that most people would get badly wrong?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B points out that a magnitude 8 is not 'twice as strong' as a magnitude 4 but about a million times more energetic, and blames confused reporting on this misunderstanding."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) mentions a discovery made without anyone seeing the thing discovered?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D says the Earth's interior has been deduced from wave records 'rather than from any direct observation', with the liquid outer core inferred from its shadow."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a measure that stops working properly at the top of its range?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A explains that Richter's scale 'saturates' above about magnitude 7, unable to separate a large earthquake from an enormous one."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best sums up the passage?","options":["Engineers should pay more attention to magnitude when designing buildings","Early-warning systems will soon be able to forecast earthquakes days ahead","The Richter scale is still the most reliable way to describe an earthquake","Earthquakes are described by several distinct scales, each answering a different question, but none of them can predict one"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The passage moves through magnitude, intensity and the instruments behind both, then ends on what they cannot do. Option A contradicts section A, C contradicts section C, and D contradicts section E."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the commentators mentioned in section E?","options":["They wanted the system to be switched off","They had been warned about the earthquake earlier than the schoolchildren","They misunderstood what an early-warning system is designed to do","They believed the system had failed to detect the first waves"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The system works by racing ahead of slower waves — 'a margin of seconds, not days'. Asking for twenty hours shows the commentators took it for a forecasting tool, which the writer says does not exist. The passage never says the system failed or that anyone wanted it removed."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"saturates\" in section A is closest in meaning to:","options":["becomes soaked with water","reaches a limit beyond which it cannot rise","gives readings that are too high","loses accuracy over time"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer explains the word at once: above magnitude 7 the scale 'stops distinguishing' between large and enormous, like a bathroom scale that reads the same for a car and a lorry — it has hit a ceiling."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Engineers decide how strong a building must be on the basis of magnitude.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Section C states the opposite: 'it is intensity, not magnitude, that engineers consult when they decide how strong a building must be'."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-23-4","level":"ch","title":"The Long Road of the Peppercorn","topic":"the trade in spices","words":550,"passage":"For most of recorded history, a sack of pepper was worth carrying halfway round the world. The plant grows only in a narrow band of hot, wet coastline, while the demand for it stretched from the kitchens of Rome to the courts of northern China. [1] Between the two lay a chain of intermediaries so long that the people at either end had only the haziest notion of where the other lived, and traders on every link had every reason to keep it that way.<br><br>Ignorance, in fact, was the trade's most valuable commodity. A merchant who knew where cloves grew, or which monsoon carried a ship from Arabia to the Malabar coast in forty days rather than ninety, held an advantage worth more than the cargo itself. [2] Medieval European buyers, for their part, were assured that cinnamon was gathered from the nests of giant birds and that pepper groves were guarded by serpents — tales that conveniently justified the price.<br><br>And the price was extraordinary. In fourteenth-century London a pound of nutmeg could be exchanged for a cow, and a year's wages bought a few handfuls of saffron. [3] Not all of this appetite was culinary; spices were prescribed as medicines, burned as perfumes and displayed at table as proof that the host could afford them, much as an expensive car is parked where the neighbours can see it.<br><br>Such margins were bound to attract someone willing to cut out the middlemen. When Portuguese ships rounded the southern tip of Africa at the end of the fifteenth century, their object was not discovery for its own sake but the source of the pepper. [4] Within a generation the overland routes that had fed the Mediterranean for a thousand years had withered, and the wealth of the cities built on them drained away to the Atlantic coast.<br><br>The ending is one the early merchants would have found hard to credit. Once the growing regions were known, the plants were carried elsewhere: nutmeg to the Caribbean, cloves to East Africa, pepper to Brazil. Supply rose, secrecy became impossible, and the price collapsed. [5] What had once been locked in a merchant's strongbox now sits in a supermarket rack, and the descendants of the men who were told about serpents sprinkle it over their food without a second thought.<br><br><i>Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There are two extra sentences you do not need to use.</i><br><br><b>A.</b> Had they arrived a century earlier, the great trading houses of Venice might have adapted; as it was, the shift came too fast to be answered.<br><b>B.</b> Today Vietnam grows more than a third of the world's pepper.<br><b>C.</b> No single trader ever made the whole journey; the goods passed from hand to hand, and each pair of hands added its share to the price.<br><b>D.</b> The commodity that had once justified voyages across unknown oceans became, within two centuries, something almost too cheap to tax.<br><b>E.</b> Such knowledge was guarded as jealously as any state secret, and passed on, if at all, only within a family.<br><b>F.</b> Salt, by contrast, was available almost everywhere and never counted as a luxury.<br><b>G.</b> Nor did the cost do anything to dampen demand; if anything, scarcity was part of the attraction.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The gap sits between the two distant ends of the trade and the 'chain of intermediaries'; C introduces that chain — goods passing 'from hand to hand', each adding to the price."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E's 'Such knowledge' refers back to knowing where cloves grew and which monsoon to use; its point about secrecy leads into the false tales fed to European buyers 'for their part'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"After the examples of extraordinary prices, G says the cost did not reduce demand, and the next sentence's 'this appetite' picks up G's 'demand'. F mentions salt, which is never discussed."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The Portuguese have just reached the source of the pepper; A's 'they' are the Portuguese, and its point that the shift came too fast to be answered leads to the overland routes withering 'within a generation'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The price has just 'collapsed'; D describes that collapse — too cheap to tax — and the final sentence contrasts the strongbox with the supermarket rack. B is a modern statistic that fits no gap."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the tales of giant birds and serpents?","options":["They were invented by the Portuguese to discourage rivals","They described real dangers on the spice routes","Medieval buyers refused to believe them","They were spread by people who profited from them"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The tales 'conveniently justified the price', in a paragraph about ignorance being the trade's most valuable commodity — the implication is that those selling the spices had every reason to encourage such stories. The Portuguese appear only later, as the ones who broke the system."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"The word \"withered\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to:","options":["shrank and lost their importance","became more expensive to travel","were officially closed","were blocked by armies"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A route that 'withers' declines like a plant losing its strength; the same sentence says the cities' wealth 'drained away', so the routes faded rather than being closed or fought over."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Venice was the wealthiest of the cities that depended on the overland spice routes.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Venice is mentioned only through its 'great trading houses' that might have adapted; the passage never compares its wealth with that of other cities on the routes."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-24-1","level":"ch","title":"Seeing Red: What Colour Sells","topic":"the psychology of colour in advertising","words":496,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Walk through any supermarket and the shelves are arguing with you, though not in words. The cereal box that shouts in yellow, the cleaning product that whispers in clinical white, the chocolate wrapped in deep purple as though it were a minor royal — none of this is accidental. Colour is the first thing a shopper registers and, according to those who study the matter, very nearly the last thing they are aware of registering. By the time the conscious mind has read the brand name, the emotional verdict has already been delivered.<br><br><b>B.</b> Rarely has a field been so eager to oversimplify itself. Popular guides insist that red means urgency, blue means trust and green means health, as though the human eye came fitted with a dictionary. Were these rules as reliable as they sound, every bank would be blue and every fast-food chain red — and, admittedly, a striking number of them are. But the research behind such claims is thinner than advertisers care to admit. Much of it was carried out on small groups of university students in a single country, and what a colour means depends heavily on where, and to whom, it is shown. White, which signals purity in one culture, is the colour of mourning in another.<br><br><b>C.</b> What the more careful studies do show is subtler. Colour works less as a message than as a filter for messages. A product whose colour is at odds with its promise — a lime-green sleeping pill, say, or a bright pink firm of accountants — creates a moment of friction in which the shopper hesitates, and hesitation is expensive. Conversely, where colour and promise pull in the same direction, the shopper is spared the effort of deciding whether the product is what it claims to be. It is this fit, rather than any inherent magic in the shade itself, that seems to move sales.<br><br><b>D.</b> No sooner had brands grasped this than they began to treat colour as property. Certain firms have registered particular shades as trademarks, so that a competitor using a similar tone on a similar product may find itself in court. The logic is that a colour can become so bound up with a company that it functions as a signature; the irony is that the same firms spend fortunes trying to establish an association which, according to their own consultants, the public forms almost instantly and forgets almost as fast.<br><br><b>E.</b> Perhaps the most honest conclusion is that colour in advertising is powerful precisely because it goes unexamined. Had shoppers been asked to justify a preference, they would have talked about price, quality or habit; not one in a hundred would have mentioned the wrapper. Yet strip the purple from the chocolate and the very same customers, tasting it blind, describe the same product as cheaper and less satisfying. We do not choose with our eyes, exactly. But our eyes have a quiet word with us first.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) mentions companies taking legal action over the use of a colour?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D describes firms registering shades as trademarks, so that a rival using a similar tone \"may find itself in court\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why the popular rules about what each colour means cannot be trusted?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B calls the red-urgency, blue-trust guides an oversimplification: the research is thin, based on small student samples in one country, and meanings vary between cultures."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that the match between a colour and a product's claim matters more than the colour itself?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says colour is \"a filter for messages\" and that it is \"this fit, rather than any inherent magic in the shade itself\", that moves sales."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) reports a test in which changing the packaging changed how a product was judged?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E describes customers tasting the chocolate blind, without its purple wrapper, and finding it \"cheaper and less satisfying\"."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best captures the writer's overall position?","options":["Colour influences shoppers powerfully, but not in the simple, dictionary-like way that is usually claimed.","Trademarking colours has made advertising less effective than it used to be.","Shoppers are fully aware of how packaging colours influence their choices.","Colour rules such as \"red means urgency\" are reliable enough for most advertisers."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The passage accepts that colour matters (sections A and E) while dismantling the crude rules (B) and replacing them with the idea of \"fit\" (C). Options A and D are contradicted; C is a side point."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about banks being blue and fast-food chains being red?","options":["It proves that the colour rules in popular guides are scientifically sound.","Such choices may reflect industry convention rather than solid evidence.","Banks and restaurants were the subjects of the original research.","These industries have been forced by law to use those colours."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer concedes \"a striking number of them are\" and then immediately says the research is \"thinner than advertisers care to admit\" — so the pattern is not treated as proof. Nothing suggests law or that these industries were studied."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section C, \"at odds with\" is closest in meaning to","options":["more expensive than","hidden behind","in conflict with","dependent on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The examples — a lime-green sleeping pill, a bright pink accountancy firm — are colours that clash with the product's promise, so \"at odds with\" means in conflict with."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer has worked as a consultant for an advertising firm.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage mentions consultants employed by brands but says nothing about the writer's own career, so the statement is Not Given."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-24-2","level":"ch","title":"The Moon's Slow Engine","topic":"tidal energy","words":542,"passage":"Twice a day, with a punctuality no power station could match, the sea rises and falls along every coast on Earth. The engine driving it is not the wind or the sun but the Moon, whose gravity drags a bulge of water around the planet as it orbits. [1] It is this reliability, more than anything else, that has kept engineers returning to the idea of harnessing the tides long after other schemes have been quietly abandoned.<br><br>The oldest approach is the barrage: a dam built across the mouth of an estuary, with turbines set into it. As the tide comes in, water is held back until a difference in level has built up; it is then released through the turbines, and the process is repeated in reverse as the tide goes out. [2] A barrage completed on a French river in the 1960s is still generating, which is more than can be said for most machinery of that era.<br><br>Yet barrages have fallen from favour, and not because they fail to work. An estuary is not an empty channel but a habitat, and a wall across it alters everything upstream — the mudflats where birds feed, the salt marshes, the movement of fish. [3] Several ambitious proposals have been shelved for exactly this reason, their engineering sound and their politics impossible.<br><br>The alternative that has emerged is less like a dam and more like an underwater wind farm. Tidal stream turbines sit on the seabed, or hang beneath floating platforms, in places where the current runs fast between islands or through narrow straits. [4] Because water is roughly eight hundred times denser than air, a tidal turbine can be far smaller than a wind turbine of equal output, and it spoils nobody's view.<br><br>Not that the technology is free of problems. Seawater is corrosive, maintenance means sending divers or vessels into some of the roughest water on the coast, and the sites where the current is strongest are, by definition, the sites where anything placed in it takes the greatest battering. [5] The cost of tidal power per unit remains well above that of wind or solar, and it will stay there until enough machines have been built for the lessons of one to cheapen the next.<br><br>Whether that day arrives depends less on the sea than on patience. The tide, after all, is in no hurry; the question is whether the people paying for it can afford to be equally calm.<br><br><b>A.</b> The blades turn as the water flows past, first in one direction and then, some six hours later, in the other. <b>B.</b> Environmental groups, who might have been expected to welcome a source of clean energy, have instead led the opposition. <b>C.</b> For this reason, the turbines are usually placed where the water is calm and shallow. <b>D.</b> Unlike sunshine or wind, the timing and size of a tide can be predicted years in advance, almost to the minute. <b>E.</b> Early prototypes have been known to lose blades within months of being installed. <b>F.</b> This is also why the tides are impossible to forecast more than a few days ahead. <b>G.</b> The principle is simple, the technology mature and the results, where it has been tried, remarkably durable.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap sits between the Moon's gravity and \"It is this reliability\", so the missing sentence must introduce the idea of predictability — piece D. Piece F says the opposite."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"The paragraph has just explained how a barrage works and goes on to praise a 1960s barrage still running, so G — simple principle, mature technology, durable results — bridges the two."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The next sentence says proposals were shelved \"for exactly this reason\" with \"their politics impossible\"; opposition from environmental groups (B) supplies both the reason and the politics."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The paragraph is describing tidal stream turbines in fast currents; A explains how their blades turn with the tide in each direction. C contradicts the \"runs fast\" placement."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"The list of problems — corrosion, dangerous maintenance, battering — leads naturally to prototypes losing blades (E) and then to the high cost that follows."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What can be inferred about the barrage proposals mentioned in the third paragraph?","options":["They were technically workable but could not win public acceptance.","They were rejected because the estuaries were too shallow.","They were later revived as tidal stream projects.","They were abandoned because the turbines proved unreliable."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The writer says barrages did not fall from favour \"because they fail to work\" and calls the proposals' \"engineering sound and their politics impossible\" — sound machines, unacceptable to the public. No revival or shallowness is mentioned."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the third paragraph, \"shelved\" is closest in meaning to","options":["built on a smaller scale","stored in a warehouse","approved after a long delay","put aside without being carried out"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Proposals that are \"shelved\" are set aside and not carried out; the context — sound engineering, impossible politics — confirms that they were dropped rather than built."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer believes tidal power can never become as cheap as wind or solar.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The writer says the cost \"will stay there until enough machines have been built for the lessons of one to cheapen the next\" — the cost is expected to fall once enough are built, so \"never\" is False."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-24-3","level":"ch","title":"Counting the Sky","topic":"the invention of the calendar","words":528,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Nobody invented the calendar in the way that somebody invented the light bulb. It was less an invention than a surrender: an admission, made independently by farmers in several river valleys, that the sky kept better time than they did. Crops had to be sown before the floods and gathered before the frosts, and the only clock large enough to be trusted was the one overhead. The trouble was that the sky offered not one clock but two, and they disagreed.<br><br><b>B.</b> The Moon was the obvious choice. Its phases are visible to anyone, they repeat every twenty-nine and a half days, and a month — the word itself is a fossil of 'moon' — is short enough to count on the fingers. The difficulty is that twelve lunar months come to only 354 days, eleven short of the solar year that governs the seasons. A calendar built on the Moon alone drifts: within a decade, a spring festival is being celebrated in the snow. Some societies accepted this and let their months wander; others bolted on an extra month every few years, a fix which required a priest or official to decide when, and which handed whoever made that decision considerable power.<br><br><b>C.</b> The Egyptians, whose whole economy hung on the annual flooding of the Nile, took the other road. They noticed that the river rose at roughly the same time as a certain bright star reappeared before dawn after months of absence, and they built a year of 365 days around it, ignoring the Moon altogether. It was a masterpiece of practicality — twelve tidy months of thirty days plus five festival days — and it was wrong by about a quarter of a day. Nobody minded at first. But a quarter of a day a year is a full day every four years, and over the centuries the Egyptian calendar slid slowly through the seasons until it had come all the way round.<br><br><b>D.</b> Only when the Romans borrowed and patched the Egyptian model did the leap year appear, and even that was not the end of the matter. The solar year is not 365.25 days but slightly less, and by the sixteenth century the error had accumulated to ten days, enough to unsettle the dating of religious festivals. The correction, when it came, was drastic: ten days were simply deleted from the month of October, so that people went to bed on the fourth and woke on the fifteenth. Not every country adopted the change at once, and for nearly two centuries a traveller crossing certain borders would find the date shifted by a week and a half.<br><br><b>E.</b> What is striking, looking back, is how little of this was about astronomy and how much was about authority. Whoever controlled the calendar controlled the rhythm of taxes, markets, feasts and fasts. Had the sky been more cooperative — had the Moon's cycle divided neatly into the Sun's — the story might have been a purely mathematical one. It was not, and so the calendar became what it remains: a compromise negotiated between the heavens and the people who wished to govern beneath them.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a reform that removed days from the year?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D recounts the sixteenth-century correction in which ten days were deleted from October, so that people went to bed on the fourth and woke on the fifteenth."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains why a calendar based only on the Moon falls out of step with the seasons?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B gives the arithmetic: twelve lunar months make 354 days, eleven short of the solar year, so a lunar calendar \"drifts\"."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) links a river flood to the reappearance of a star?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C says the Egyptians noticed the Nile rose at about the same time as a bright star reappeared before dawn, and built their year around it."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that calendars were shaped as much by power as by astronomy?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E states that the history \"was about authority\" — whoever controlled the calendar controlled taxes, markets, feasts and fasts."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How the Egyptians produced the first accurate calendar","Why the Moon is a better timekeeper than the Sun","How the calendar grew out of repeated compromises between the sky's two clocks and human needs","Why modern countries still disagree about the date"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Section A sets up two disagreeing clocks, B–D show successive attempts to reconcile them, and E names the result \"a compromise negotiated between the heavens and the people\". The other options are single details or untrue."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply about the practice of adding an extra month \"every few years\"?","options":["It was adopted by every society that used a lunar calendar.","It was invented by the Egyptians.","It solved the problem permanently.","It was open to being used for political advantage."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer notes that the fix \"handed whoever made that decision considerable power\", and section E develops the theme of calendars as a tool of authority — the implication is that the power could be exploited. Some societies simply let their months wander, so not every society used it."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section B, the word \"fossil\" is used to suggest that the word \"month\"","options":["preserves a trace of an older meaning","was invented by astronomers","refers to something found underground","is no longer used in modern English"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A fossil is a preserved remnant of something ancient; the writer means that \"month\" still carries the buried form of \"moon\" from the days when months followed lunar phases."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The Egyptian calendar took no account of the Moon.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Section C says the Egyptians built a 365-day year around the star and the Nile flood, \"ignoring the Moon altogether\" — True."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-24-4","level":"ch","title":"Gone by Breakfast","topic":"why we forget dreams","words":565,"passage":"Everyone knows the experience. You wake with the certainty that something vivid has just happened — a chase, a conversation, a house that was and was not yours — and by the time you have reached the kitchen it has thinned to nothing. [1] Far from being a failure of memory, this forgetting appears to be something the brain does on purpose, and working out why has taught researchers a great deal about memory itself.<br><br>The first clue lies in the chemistry of sleep. During the phase in which most dreaming occurs, the brain is awash with some signalling chemicals and almost empty of others. [2] Without it, experiences are processed but not filed; the dream is lived and, in the same moment, let go. It is like writing on a slate from which the chalk fades as fast as the hand moves.<br><br>A second explanation concerns what the sleeping brain regards as worth keeping. Waking memories are strengthened when they are emotionally charged, repeated, or connected to something already known. [3] They break the rules of time and place, they are rarely rehearsed, and they seldom attach themselves to any real event that might serve as a hook. A memory with no hook has nowhere to hang.<br><br>There is also the awkward matter of the transition itself. The moments after waking are precisely when the mind is busiest with other things: the alarm, the day ahead, whether it is Tuesday. [4] Those who keep a notebook by the bed and write before doing anything else report remembering far more, which suggests that the dreams were there, briefly, for the taking.<br><br>Not everyone forgets equally. People who surface naturally during dreaming sleep, rather than being dragged out of a deeper stage, tend to recall more; so do the anxious, the imaginative and — for reasons nobody has yet explained — those who are simply interested in their dreams. [5] It may be that they are not better at remembering so much as better at noticing, catching the memory in the second before it dissolves.<br><br>Why should the brain bother to erase its own night's work? One theory holds that dreams are a by-product of the sorting and tidying that memory undergoes in sleep, and that recording it would be as pointless as keeping every draft of a letter. Another suggests that a mind which could not tell dreams from memories would be a mind in trouble, and that forgetting is the price of staying sane. Either way, the loss is no accident. The kitchen is not where the dream goes; it is where we discover it was never meant to stay.<br><br><b>A.</b> Attention turns outward, and whatever was fragile in the mind is crowded out before it can be secured. <b>B.</b> This explains why children, who sleep far longer than adults, almost never remember their dreams. <b>C.</b> Chief among the missing is a chemical that, in waking life, is essential for turning an experience into a lasting memory. <b>D.</b> Whether such people dream more, or merely remember more of it, is far from clear. <b>E.</b> For this reason, dreams that are written down are forgotten just as quickly as those that are not. <b>F.</b> Ask most people what they dreamed last night and, after a pause, they will admit that they have no idea. <b>G.</b> Dreams, on the whole, fail every one of these tests.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["F"],"answer":"F","explanation":"The paragraph moves from one person's fading dream to \"this forgetting\" as a general phenomenon; F, in which most people admit they have no idea what they dreamed, makes that generalisation."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The following sentence begins \"Without it\", so the gap must introduce a single missing thing — the chemical needed for lasting memory (C)."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["G"],"answer":"G","explanation":"The previous sentence lists three conditions that strengthen waking memories, and the next explains how dreams break each of them; G — dreams \"fail every one of these tests\" — is the hinge between them."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The paragraph describes the mind being busy on waking; A explains that attention turns outward and the fragile dream is crowded out, which the notebook evidence then supports. E contradicts that evidence."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which piece (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The gap follows a list of people who recall more dreams and precedes \"It may be that they are not better at remembering so much as better at noticing\" — D raises exactly that uncertainty. B makes an unsupported claim about children."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the notebook evidence in the fourth paragraph suggest?","options":["The chemistry of sleep has no effect on dream recall.","Writing dreams down makes people dream more often.","Dreams are stored normally but are lost through distraction on waking.","Only imaginative people can remember their dreams."],"correct":2,"explanation":"People who write before doing anything else \"report remembering far more\", which the writer takes to mean the dreams \"were there, briefly, for the taking\" — available, then lost to the morning's distractions. The passage does not say writing causes more dreaming."},{"kind":"reference","type":"mcq","q":"In the final sentence, \"it\" in \"it was never meant to stay\" refers to","options":["the brain","the dream","the theory","the kitchen"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts where \"the dream goes\" with what \"we discover\" — that the dream itself was never meant to stay. The kitchen is the place of discovery, not the thing that leaves."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which of the following best states the passage's central claim?","options":["Forgetting dreams is a deliberate feature of how the brain handles memory, not a malfunction.","Dreams are remembered only by anxious or imaginative people.","Scientists have finally explained the purpose of dreaming.","Forgetting dreams is a defect that better sleep habits can cure."],"correct":0,"explanation":"The opening calls the forgetting \"something the brain does on purpose\" and the close says \"the loss is no accident\". Option C is one detail, and the writer offers competing theories rather than a final explanation."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-25-1","level":"ch","title":"The Last Public Living Room","topic":"the future of libraries","words":550,"passage":"<b>A.</b> Twenty years ago it was fashionable to write the library's obituary. Once every text on earth could be summoned to a screen, the argument ran, a building full of paper would go the way of the telegraph office, and its staff would follow. The prediction was confident, widely repeated and wrong. In several large cities, visits to public libraries have actually risen over the very years in which book-borrowing fell. What the forecasters had failed to grasp was that a library had never chiefly been a warehouse for books. The books were the excuse; the building was the point.<br><br><b>B.</b> Ask a librarian what the institution offers now and the first answer is rarely reading. It is room. A public library is almost the last indoor space in a modern city where a person may sit for six hours without being expected to buy anything, explain themselves or leave. Teenagers who cannot study in a crowded flat, freelancers who cannot afford an office, pensioners for whom the walk to the reading room is the day's only conversation: all of them are using the library for something other than its collection. One head of service calls her building, only half joking, the city's last public living room.<br><br><b>C.</b> Around that core, the range of services has spread in directions that would have baffled an earlier generation. Branches now lend sewing machines, seeds, musical instruments and power tools. They house recording booths, language conversation circles and drop-in clinics where a bewildered resident is walked through an online tax form. Far from making the profession redundant, the flood of information has made it more demanding. Fetching a volume from a shelf was a modest skill; steering a nervous seventy-year-old past three fraudulent websites to the one genuine one is not. Scarcity, as one commentator put it, has simply changed sides: information is now abundant and judgement is what is rationed.<br><br><b>D.</b> The real threat to the library, then, is not irrelevance but arithmetic. A council that took the obituaries at face value could see no reason to go on paying for a building whose supposed function had migrated online. One town in the north of England closed its branch and replaced it with a 'digital hub', a room of touch-screen kiosks staffed by nobody. Within eighteen months, and without any announcement, a desk with a person behind it had been reinstated. The kiosks had turned out to be used mainly to ask where the person was. The episode illustrates a wider difficulty: there is no line on a budget spreadsheet for 'somewhere warm to do your homework', and what cannot be counted is easily cut.<br><br><b>E.</b> If the library survives, it will do so by looking less like a temple of books and more like a town square with a roof. That transformation carries its own risk. An institution that becomes a general-purpose welfare outpost, with a few shelves kept for decoration, may find it has lost the one thing nobody else was offering, which was the permission to sit quietly with a difficult book. Whether the future library is a workshop, a refuge or a reading room is not a question for technologists to settle. It belongs to the people who walk through the doors, and they have not stopped coming.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) describes a replacement for a library that was quietly undone?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D: the branch became a 'digital hub' of kiosks, but 'within eighteen months, and without any announcement, a desk with a person behind it had been reinstated'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) argues that the librarian's job has become harder rather than unnecessary?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C: 'Far from making the profession redundant, the flood of information has made it more demanding', contrasting fetching a book with steering someone past fraudulent websites."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains what the people who predicted the library's end had overlooked?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Section A: 'What the forecasters had failed to grasp was that a library had never chiefly been a warehouse for books.'"},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) presents the library as a place where visitors are under no obligation to spend money?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes 'the last indoor space in a modern city where a person may sit for six hours without being expected to buy anything'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best expresses the writer's overall argument?","options":["Libraries should stop lending books and concentrate on providing social services.","Digital kiosks are a cheaper and more reliable alternative to trained librarians.","Public libraries are closing because fewer people want to read.","Libraries have outlived the prediction of their death because their real value was never the books alone."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Every section returns to the same claim: the obituaries were wrong because the building, the space and the human help were the point, not the collection. Option B is a risk the writer warns against in section E, not a recommendation."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the story of the touch-screen kiosks most strongly suggest?","options":["Residents in the north of England are less confident with screens than elsewhere.","The council reopened the branch because of public protests.","What the town's residents had valued in the branch was a person, not access to technology.","The council had not installed enough kiosks to meet demand."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The kiosks were 'used mainly to ask where the person was' and the desk came back without any campaign or announcement — the writer lets the reader conclude that human help was the service people had actually been using. Nothing is said about protests or the number of kiosks."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section D, the word \"reinstated\" is closest in meaning to:","options":["redesigned","brought back","moved elsewhere","paid for"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To reinstate something is to restore it to its former position; the staffed desk that had been removed was put back."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"According to the passage, the digital hub cost the council less to run than the branch it replaced.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The passage says the council 'could see no reason to go on paying' for the building, but it never states what the hub cost or compares the two. The reader may assume savings, but the text does not give the figure."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-25-2","level":"ch","title":"Six Legs on the Menu","topic":"insects as food","words":503,"passage":"In a warehouse on the edge of a mid-sized European city, several million crickets are being raised on vegetable trimmings that the supermarket next door would otherwise have thrown away. [1] Within six weeks the crickets will be roasted, ground and packed into a protein powder whose label makes no mention of legs.<br><br>The case for eating insects is, on paper, overwhelming. Crickets convert feed into body mass roughly twelve times more efficiently than cattle, need a fraction of the water and land, and produce almost none of the methane that makes beef so costly to the climate. [2] Around two billion people already eat insects as a matter of course, and in many of those cuisines a bowl of fried grasshoppers is a treat to be looked forward to rather than a hardship to be endured.<br><br>Why, then, has the industry grown so slowly in precisely the countries that could most easily afford to switch? The obstacle is not nutrition, nor safety, nor even price, which has fallen steadily as the farms have scaled up. It is disgust, and disgust, as psychologists have long observed, is not an argument that can be met with a better argument. [3] The reaction is learned early, reinforced by every cartoon in which a bug crawls out of a sandwich, and remarkably resistant to information.<br><br>The industry's response has been to make the insect disappear. Powders, protein bars, pasta with a discreet percentage of cricket flour: the strategy is to get the nutrition into the shopper without the shopper ever having to confront the animal. [4] A generation that grows up eating cricket crackers without a second thought may come to find the whole crawling question rather quaint. Critics, however, point out that concealment cedes the argument before it has begun. If the only way to sell a product is to hide it, its image has not improved; the problem has merely been outsourced to a label.<br><br>There is a further wrinkle on which enthusiasts prefer not to dwell. Most of the cricket powder now produced does not end up in human food at all, but in feed for pets and farmed fish, where nobody's stomach needs persuading. [5] Whether that counts as a success or a detour depends on what one thought the point was.<br><br><b>A.</b> The arrangement suits both parties: the shop pays nothing to dispose of its waste, and the farm pays nothing for feed.<br><b>B.</b> Nor is the practice anything like as exotic as diners in Europe tend to assume.<br><b>C.</b> One cannot reason a person out of a feeling they were never reasoned into.<br><b>D.</b> It is a patient strategy, and its real target is not today's consumer but tomorrow's.<br><b>E.</b> The insect economy, in other words, is thriving, just not on the plates it was designed for.<br><b>F.</b> Even so, the powder retains a faint nutty flavour that some tasters describe as pleasant.<br><b>G.</b> This is why most of the farms remain small family businesses with a handful of staff.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'The arrangement' and 'both parties' refer back to the supermarket's trimmings feeding the farm; the sentence explains why each side benefits before the text moves on to what happens to the crickets."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Nor is the practice ... exotic' adds a second point to the case for insects and is immediately supported by 'Around two billion people already eat insects as a matter of course'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The sentence before says disgust 'is not an argument that can be met with a better argument'; C restates that idea as a maxim, and 'The reaction is learned early' then describes the feeling."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"'A patient strategy' whose target is 'tomorrow's' consumer sets up the next sentence about 'a generation that grows up eating cricket crackers'."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"'In other words' sums up the point that the powder goes into animal feed, and 'not on the plates it was designed for' leads straight into 'whether that counts as a success or a detour'."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply with the final sentence, \"Whether that counts as a success or a detour depends on what one thought the point was\"?","options":["Feeding insects to fish and pets was always the main aim of the cricket farmers.","The cricket farms are on the verge of going out of business.","Enthusiasts have deliberately misled the public about how much powder is produced.","The industry is making money without achieving the change in human diets it was originally meant to bring about."],"correct":3,"explanation":"The powder is selling, so commercially it is a 'success'; but if the point was to get people eating insects, sales to pet and fish farms are a 'detour'. The writer leaves the reader to notice that the original purpose has been sidestepped. Nothing supports B, C or D."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the fourth paragraph, \"concealment cedes the argument\" means that hiding the insect:","options":["gives up the argument without contesting it","wins the argument decisively","delays the argument until later","makes the argument more complicated"],"correct":0,"explanation":"To cede something is to surrender or give it up. The critics' point is that by hiding the insect, the industry admits defeat on the question of its image rather than fighting it."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"Most of the people who eat insects regularly do so because they cannot afford other food.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage says that in many of those cuisines fried grasshoppers are 'a treat to be looked forward to rather than a hardship to be endured', which directly contradicts the idea that insects are eaten only out of poverty."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-25-3","level":"ch","title":"A Ledger of Losses: What Translators Actually Do","topic":"the art of translation","words":550,"passage":"<b>A.</b> A literary translator I know is asked the same question at every dinner party: are the machines about to take her job? Her answer has been polished by repetition. They already do, she says, the part of it that was never the job. The guests usually laugh and move on; had they pressed her, they would have found that she meant it quite precisely, and that the remark contains most of what there is to say about translation.<br><br><b>B.</b> The popular picture of the work is that of swapping words: a sentence goes in, its equivalent comes out, and the only skill involved is knowing enough vocabulary. But languages do not divide the world at the same joints. One has a single word for a colour that another splits into two; one obliges a speaker to say whether an event was witnessed or merely reported; one, like Vietnamese, folds the age and relationship of two people into the very pronoun by which they address each other, information that the English 'you' silently throws away. A translator therefore spends less time finding equivalents than deciding what to lose. Every translated page is a ledger of losses and compensations, and no two translators keep the same accounts.<br><br><b>C.</b> Nor is there agreement about what the finished page should feel like. One school holds that a translation ought to read as though it had been written in the reader's own language, every seam invisible. The rival school insists that the reader should be kept aware of the text's foreignness, reminded on every page that it came from somewhere else and was made by someone unlike them. Neither camp has won, and in practice a translator's choice frequently owes less to principle than to the commission. A publisher wants a novel that will sell in airports; a university press wants one a scholar can cite with confidence; the same translator may serve both in a single year.<br><br><b>D.</b> Wordplay is where these difficulties reach their limit. Take a joke that turns on two meanings of a single word. The translator has three options: explain it in a footnote, which is where jokes go to die; replace it with a different joke that does equivalent work, faithful to the spirit at the expense of the letter; or quietly drop it. A friend once translated a children's story in which a character's name was itself a pun. She invented a new name, with a new pun, in the target language. When the author was told, he replied that he wished he had thought of it first. That is a gracious response, but it leaves an awkward question hanging: whose page is it now?<br><br><b>E.</b> Which brings us back to the machines. They perform impressively exactly where translation comes closest to substitution: manuals, weather forecasts, contracts, the great bulk of text that nobody reads for pleasure. They falter where translation comes closest to writing, because there the task is not to find the right word but to choose among several wrong ones. What remains for the human is the residue: the judgement about which loss is bearable, which betrayal is the lesser, which joke deserves to be rebuilt from scratch. My friend's dinner-party answer, in other words, was not a boast. It was a job description.","questions":[{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) suggests that a translator's approach may be shaped by who has commissioned the work?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Section C: 'a translator's choice frequently owes less to principle than to the commission', followed by the contrast between a publisher and a university press."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) gives an example of an alteration that the original author welcomed?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Section D: the translator invented a new punning name and the author 'replied that he wished he had thought of it first'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) explains how a single word in one language may carry information that another language leaves out?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Section B describes the Vietnamese pronoun that encodes age and relationship, 'information that the English \"you\" silently throws away'."},{"kind":"section","type":"text","q":"Which section (A–E) identifies the kinds of text that computers translate well?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Section E: machines 'perform impressively' on 'manuals, weather forecasts, contracts, the great bulk of text that nobody reads for pleasure'."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"What is the passage mainly concerned with?","options":["Why jokes and puns should never be included in translated children's books.","Why the essence of translation lies in judgement about what to sacrifice, not in finding equivalent words.","How translators can protect their profession from being replaced by software.","The superiority of translations that keep the reader aware of the text's foreign origin."],"correct":1,"explanation":"From the ledger of 'losses and compensations' to 'which betrayal is the lesser', every section argues that translating is deciding what to lose. The passage takes no side between the two schools in C and does not advise avoiding puns."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"When the translator says that machines already do \"the part of it that was never the job\", what does she mean?","options":["Replacing words with their equivalents was never the real substance of translating.","Machines have taken over the badly paid work that translators used to refuse.","Translators have never been asked to translate manuals or contracts.","Her own job was abolished some years ago and she now works in another field."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Section B dismisses 'swapping words' as the popular but false picture of the work, and section E says machines succeed only where translation is 'closest to substitution'. Put together, her remark means the mechanical part was never what translating really consisted of."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In section E, \"the residue\" refers to:","options":["the errors that machines leave in a text","the fee a translator receives","the original text before translation","what is left over after the mechanical part has been removed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A residue is what remains after something has been taken away. Here it is the part of the job that survives once machines have done the substitution: the judgements."},{"kind":"tfng","type":"mcq","q":"The writer believes the \"invisible seams\" approach produces better translations than the rival approach.","options":["True","False","Not Given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Section C presents both schools and states that 'neither camp has won'; the writer describes the disagreement without saying which side he or she prefers."}]},{"id":"rd-ch-25-4","level":"ch","title":"The Official and the Stubborn","topic":"how cities name their streets","words":519,"passage":"A visitor arriving in a city for the first time reads its streets before reading anything else, and the streets, whether or not anyone intended it, read back. [1] A grid of numbered avenues announces a place laid out in one go by people who prized efficiency over sentiment; a tangle of lanes named after long-vanished trades, Tanners' Row, Rope Walk, Candle Court, announces one that grew by accident and has chosen to remember it.<br><br>Who does the choosing varies more than one might expect. In some cities the power sits with a committee that meets four times a year and works through a list of the recently deceased; in others it belongs to the property developer, who names the roads of a new estate and shows a marked fondness for the trees that were felled to build it. [2] The results, however arrived at, are seldom neutral. A city that names a boulevard after a general has taken a position, and one that later renames it after a poet has taken another.<br><br>Renaming is where the politics show most clearly. After a revolution or a change of regime, one of the new authorities' first acts is commonly to overwrite the map, so that every journey across town becomes a small lesson in who now matters. [3] Older residents go on using the previous names for decades, taxi drivers keep both versions in their heads, and the city ends up with two layers of memory laid one over the other, the official and the stubborn.<br><br>There is also a quieter, more practical kind of naming, concerned less with honour than with being found. Ambulance crews dislike duplicate names, so a city with three Church Streets is under steady pressure to lose two. [4] The compromise reached in one European capital was to keep every name but let each district add its own number, producing addresses that are easy to deliver to and impossible to say aloud in a single breath.<br><br>Perhaps the most telling names, though, are the ones nobody chose at all. [5] A shortcut through a car park becomes 'the cut'; a corner where teenagers gather acquires a name that appears on no sign and on every phone; a bridge is known for a generation by the shop that once stood at its end. Officials may put up whatever plates they like. A street, in the end, is called what the people who walk along it call it.<br><br><b>A.</b> Names are the most public form of memory a city possesses, and also the cheapest.<br><b>B.</b> Elsewhere the choice was made centuries ago by nobody in particular and has simply stuck.<br><b>C.</b> Yet the overwriting is rarely as complete as its authors hope.<br><b>D.</b> The residents, who have written the old name on a thousand forms, dislike losing it even more.<br><b>E.</b> These arise from use rather than from decision, and they are the hardest of all to remove.<br><b>F.</b> The general, for his part, had never set foot in the city that honoured him.<br><b>G.</b> Postcodes were introduced for much the same reason, though they honour nobody.","questions":[{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [1]?","accept":["A"],"answer":"A","explanation":"'Names are ... memory' explains how streets 'read back', and the following sentences give two examples of what a city's names reveal about its history."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [2]?","accept":["B"],"answer":"B","explanation":"'Elsewhere' adds a third answer to 'who does the choosing' after the committee and the developer; 'however arrived at' in the next sentence then covers all three."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [3]?","accept":["C"],"answer":"C","explanation":"'Yet the overwriting' picks up 'overwrite the map' and 'rarely as complete' is illustrated by the residents and taxi drivers who keep the old names alive."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [4]?","accept":["D"],"answer":"D","explanation":"'Dislike losing it even more' balances the ambulance crews' dislike of duplicates, and the two-sided objection is what makes 'the compromise' in the next sentence necessary."},{"kind":"gap","type":"text","q":"Which sentence (A–G) fits gap [5]?","accept":["E"],"answer":"E","explanation":"'These' refers to 'the ones nobody chose at all', and 'arise from use' is shown by the shortcut, the corner and the bridge that follow."},{"kind":"main-idea","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement best captures the writer's central point?","options":["Renaming streets after a revolution successfully erases the old regime from memory.","Street names are a record of who has held power in a city, but everyday use has the final say.","Cities should adopt numbered grids because they cause the fewest arguments.","Most street names are chosen by property developers with little regard for history."],"correct":1,"explanation":"The passage moves from official naming and political renaming to the 'stubborn' layer of memory and the unofficial names that 'are the hardest of all to remove', ending with the claim that a street is called what its walkers call it. D is contradicted by the third paragraph."},{"kind":"inference","type":"mcq","q":"What does the writer imply by saying that a city which renames a boulevard after a poet \"has taken another\" position?","options":["Cities should avoid naming streets after any real person.","Streets named after generals are usually renamed within a generation.","Choosing a poet over a general is itself a political statement, not a retreat from politics.","Poets are generally more popular with naming committees than generals."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence pairs the two acts as parallel: if honouring a general 'has taken a position', replacing him with a poet is not neutral either but a second position. The passage says nothing about popularity, timing or a ban on real names."},{"kind":"vocab","type":"mcq","q":"In the final paragraph, \"the most telling names\" means the names that are most:","options":["widely used","recently invented","difficult to pronounce","revealing"],"correct":3,"explanation":"'Telling' as an adjective means significant or revealing; these unofficial names show most clearly how a city really thinks of itself."}]}];
if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) module.exports = { READING_PASSAGES };
